(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqThermo=[{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"In an adiabatic process, which of the following statements is true?",o:["The molar heat capacity is infinite","The internal energy of the gas decreases as the temperature increases","Work done by the gas equals the increase in internal energy","The molar heat capacity is zero"],c:3,s:`In an adiabatic process no heat is exchanged, so dQ = 0.
Molar heat capacity C = dQ/(n dT) = 0.
Work done by the gas equals the decrease (not increase) in internal energy, and U rises with T, so the other options are false.
Hence the molar heat capacity is zero.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Identify the characteristics of an adiabatic process in a monoatomic gas.
(A) Internal energy is constant.
(B) Work done in the process is equal to the change in internal energy.
(C) The product of temperature and volume is a constant.
(D) The product of pressure and volume is a constant.
(E) The work done to change the temperature from T\u2081 to T\u2082 is proportional to (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081).
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(B), (E) only","(A), (C) only","(A), (C), (D) only","(B), (D) only"],c:0,s:`Adiabatic: Q = 0, so \u0394U + W = 0 and |W| = |\u0394U|. (B) is true; (A) is false because U changes.
For an adiabatic process TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant and PV^\u03B3 = constant, so (C) and (D) are false.
W = nR(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1), which is proportional to (T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081). (E) is true.
Correct statements: (B) and (E) only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"During the melting of a slab of ice at 273 K at atmospheric pressure",o:["Internal energy of ice-water system decreases.","Positive work is done on the ice-water system by the atmosphere.","Internal energy of ice-water system remains unchanged.","Positive work is done by the ice-water system on the atmosphere."],c:1,s:`Ice absorbs latent heat while melting at constant temperature, so its internal energy increases. This rules out (a) and (c).
Water is denser than ice, so the volume of the system decreases on melting.
The atmosphere pushes the shrinking system inwards, so positive work is done on the system by the atmosphere.
Answer: (b).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:`A piston of mass M is hung from a massless spring, whose restoring force law goes as F = \u2212kx\xB3, where k is the spring constant of appropriate dimension. The piston separates the vertical chamber into two parts, where the bottom part is filled with 'n' moles of an ideal gas. An external work is done on the gas isothermally (at a constant temperature T) with the help of a heating filament (with negligible volume) mounted in lower part of the chamber, so that the piston goes up from a height L\u2080 to L\u2081, the total energy delivered by the filament is
(Assume spring to be in its natural length before heating)`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"Vertical chamber with a piston hung from a spring at the top; gas and a heating filament below the piston; piston rises from height L0 to L1",d:[["r",60,20,100,180],["p","M110 20 L110 28 L100 34 L120 46 L100 58 L120 70 L100 82 L120 94 L110 100 L110 110"],["r",62,110,96,6,"F"],["l",60,60,160,60,"d"],["p","M80 190 L88 184 L96 196 L104 184 L112 196 L120 184 L128 196 L136 190"],["t",110,168,"n mol gas","ms"],["t",110,216,"heating filament","ms"],["l",42,200,42,113,"b"],["t",38,160,"L\u2080","e"],["l",184,200,184,60,"b"],["t",190,130,"L\u2081"],["t",170,16,"spring",""],["t",170,116,"piston (M)","s"]]},o:["nRT ln({L\u2081\xB2;L\u2080\xB2}) + {Mg;2}(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080) + {k;4}(L\u2081\u2074 \u2212 L\u2080\u2074)","3nRT ln({L\u2081;L\u2080}) + 2Mg(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080) + {k;4}(L\u2081\xB3 \u2212 L\u2080\xB3)","nRT ln({L\u2081;L\u2080}) + Mg(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080) + {3k;4}(L\u2081\u2074 \u2212 L\u2080\u2074)","nRT ln({L\u2081;L\u2080}) + Mg(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080) + {k;4}(L\u2081\u2074 \u2212 L\u2080\u2074)"],c:3,s:`Add up the energy needed in three parts.
Isothermal change of the gas (V \u221D L): nRT ln(V\u2081/V\u2080) = nRT ln(L\u2081/L\u2080).
Gain in gravitational energy of the piston: Mg(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080).
Energy stored in the spring: \u222Bkx\xB3dx = {k;4}x\u2074, so the change is {k;4}(L\u2081\u2074 \u2212 L\u2080\u2074).
Total = nRT ln(L\u2081/L\u2080) + Mg(L\u2081 \u2212 L\u2080) + {k;4}(L\u2081\u2074 \u2212 L\u2080\u2074).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A gas is kept in a container having walls which are thermally non-conducting. Initially the gas has a volume of 800 cm\xB3 and temperature 27\xB0C. The change in temperature when the gas is adiabatically compressed to 200 cm\xB3 is
(Take \u03B3 = 1.5; \u03B3 is the ratio of specific heats at constant pressure and at constant volume)`,o:["300 K","600 K","327 K","522 K"],c:0,s:`Adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, with T\u2081 = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
T\u2082 = T\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^(\u03B3\u22121) = 300 \xD7 (800/200)^0.5 = 300 \xD7 2 = 600 K.
Change in temperature = 600 \u2212 300 = 300 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isobaric and isochoric processes",q:"Pressure of an ideal gas, contained in a closed vessel, is increased by 0.4% when heated by 1\xB0C. Its initial temperature must be",o:["25\xB0C","250 K","2500 K","250\xB0C"],c:1,s:`A closed vessel means constant volume, so P \u221D T.
\u0394P/P = \u0394T/T, so 0.4/100 = 1/T.
T = 250 K (which is \u221223\xB0C).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"An ideal gas exists in a state with pressure P\u2080, volume V\u2080. It is isothermally expanded to 4 times of its initial volume (V\u2080), then isobarically compressed to its original volume. Finally the system is heated isochorically to bring it to its initial state. The amount of heat exchanged in this process is",o:["P\u2080V\u2080(2 ln 2 \u2212 0.25)","P\u2080V\u2080(ln 2 \u2212 0.75)","P\u2080V\u2080(2 ln 2 \u2212 0.75)","P\u2080V\u2080(ln 2 \u2212 0.25)"],c:2,s:`The gas returns to its initial state, so \u0394U = 0 and the net heat equals the net work.
Isothermal expansion V\u2080 to 4V\u2080: W\u2081 = P\u2080V\u2080 ln 4 = 2P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2. The pressure falls to P\u2080/4.
Isobaric compression at P\u2080/4 from 4V\u2080 to V\u2080: W\u2082 = \u2212{P\u2080;4} \xD7 3V\u2080 = \u22120.75P\u2080V\u2080.
Isochoric heating: W\u2083 = 0.
Q = W = P\u2080V\u2080(2 ln 2 \u2212 0.75).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A monoatomic gas having \u03B3 = {5;3} is stored in a thermally insulated container and the gas is suddenly compressed to ({1;8})th of its initial volume. The ratio of final pressure and initial pressure is
(\u03B3 is the ratio of specific heats of the gas at constant pressure and constant volume)`,o:["16","28","40","32"],c:3,s:`Sudden compression in an insulated container is adiabatic: PV^\u03B3 = constant.
P\u2082/P\u2081 = (V\u2081/V\u2082)^\u03B3 = 8^(5/3).
8^(5/3) = (2\xB3)^(5/3) = 2\u2075 = 32.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given are statements for certain thermodynamic variables.
(A) Internal energy, volume (V) and mass (M) are extensive variables.
(B) Pressure (P), temperature (T) and density (\u03C1) are intensive variables.
(C) Volume (V), temperature (T) and density (\u03C1) are intensive variables.
(D) Mass (M), temperature (T) and internal energy are extensive variables.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A) and (B) only","(D) and (A) only","(C) and (D) only","(B) and (C) only"],c:0,s:`Extensive variables depend on the amount of gas: mass, volume, internal energy.
Intensive variables do not depend on the amount: pressure, temperature, density.
(A) and (B) are correct. (C) is wrong because volume is extensive, and (D) is wrong because temperature is intensive.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:"Using the given P-V diagram, the work done by an ideal gas along the path ABCD is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"P-V diagram: A at (2V0, P0), B at (3V0, P0), C at (3V0, 2P0), D at (V0, 2P0); path A to B to C to D",d:[["l",50,170,300,170,"a"],["l",50,170,50,15,"a"],["l",110,50,110,170,"d"],["l",170,110,170,170,"d"],["l",230,50,230,170,"d"],["l",50,50,110,50,"d"],["l",50,110,170,110,"d"],["l",170,110,230,110,"a"],["l",230,110,230,50,"a"],["l",230,50,110,50,"a"],["t",44,20,"P","es"],["t",300,186,"V","e"],["t",110,186,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",170,186,"2V\u2080","ms"],["t",230,186,"3V\u2080","ms"],["t",44,54,"2P\u2080","e"],["t",44,114,"P\u2080","e"],["t",170,126,"A","m"],["t",238,114,"B"],["t",238,46,"C"],["t",102,44,"D","e"]]},o:["3P\u2080V\u2080","\u22123P\u2080V\u2080","4P\u2080V\u2080","\u22124P\u2080V\u2080"],c:1,s:`From the diagram: A (2V\u2080, P\u2080), B (3V\u2080, P\u2080), C (3V\u2080, 2P\u2080), D (V\u2080, 2P\u2080).
A to B at P\u2080: W = P\u2080(3V\u2080 \u2212 2V\u2080) = P\u2080V\u2080.
B to C: volume constant, W = 0.
C to D at 2P\u2080: W = 2P\u2080(V\u2080 \u2212 3V\u2080) = \u22124P\u2080V\u2080.
Total W = P\u2080V\u2080 + 0 \u2212 4P\u2080V\u2080 = \u22123P\u2080V\u2080.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`An ideal gas goes from an initial state to final state. During the process, the pressure of the gas increases linearly with temperature.
A. The work done by gas during the process is zero.
B. The heat added to gas is different from change in internal energy.
C. The volume of the gas is increased.
D. The internal energy of the gas is increased.
E. The process is isochoric (constant volume process)
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A, D, E only","A, B, C, D only","E only","A, C only"],c:0,s:`P \u221D T means P/T is constant, so V is constant (Gay-Lussac's law): the process is isochoric. (E) is true and (C) is false.
No change in volume, so W = 0. (A) is true.
By the first law Q = \u0394U, so (B) is false.
T increases, so the internal energy increases. (D) is true.
Correct: A, D, E only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"The magnitude of heat exchanged by a system for the given cyclic process ABCA (as shown in figure) is (in SI unit)",fig:{w:340,h:220,alt:"P-V graph: circle centred at 300 cc and 300 kPa with radius 100 units; upper half A to B to C then chord C to A; A at 400 cc, C at 200 cc, B at 400 kPa, D at 200 kPa",d:[["l",50,190,290,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,50,200,50,"d"],["l",50,150,200,150,"d"],["l",150,190,150,100,"d"],["l",250,190,250,100,"d"],["l",200,190,200,150,"d"],["p","M150 100 A50 50 0 0 0 250 100","d"],["p","M250 100 A50 50 0 0 0 200 50","a"],["p","M200 50 A50 50 0 0 0 150 100","a"],["l",150,100,250,100,"a"],["t",44,54,"400","e"],["t",44,154,"200","e"],["t",150,206,"200","ms"],["t",250,206,"400","ms"],["t",56,14,"P (in kPa)","s"],["t",330,216,"V (in cc)","es"],["t",258,98,"A"],["t",200,42,"B","m"],["t",142,98,"C","e"],["t",200,166,"D","m"]]},o:["5\u03C0","zero","40\u03C0","10\u03C0"],c:0,s:`For a cycle \u0394U = 0, so the heat exchanged equals the work, which is the area enclosed by the path.
The path is the upper semicircle ABC plus the diameter CA. Radius in volume = 100 cc = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3 and in pressure = 100 kPa = 10\u2075 Pa.
Area = \xBD \xD7 \u03C0 \xD7 (10\u207B\u2074)(10\u2075) = 5\u03C0 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"The work done in an adiabatic change in an ideal gas depends upon only",o:["change in its specific heat","change in its pressure","change in its volume","change in its temperature"],c:3,s:`In an adiabatic change Q = 0, so W = \u2212\u0394U.
For an ideal gas \u0394U = nC_V \u0394T, which depends only on the temperature change.
So W = nR(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) depends only on the change in temperature.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A poly-atomic molecule (C\u1D65 = 3R, C\u209A = 4R, where R is gas constant) goes from phase space point A (P = 10\u2075 Pa, V = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3) to point B (P = 5 \xD7 10\u2074 Pa, V = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3) to point C (P = 10\u2074 Pa, V = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3). A to B is an adiabatic path and B to C is an isothermal path. The net heat absorbed per unit mole by the system is",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Pressure-volume sketch: A on the 500 K isotherm, B and C on the 450 K isotherm; A to B steep adiabatic curve, B to C along the 450 K isotherm; volumes VA, VB, VC increase",d:[["l",50,180,310,180,"a"],["l",50,180,50,20,"a"],["p","M75 45 Q110 105 290 110","d"],["p","M105 100 Q140 132 290 136","d"],["p","M84 57 Q110 118 169 125","a"],["p","M169 125 Q200 130 222 132","a"],["l",84,57,84,180,"d"],["l",169,125,169,180,"d"],["l",222,132,222,180,"d"],["t",76,52,"A","e"],["t",169,144,"B","m"],["t",222,150,"C","m"],["t",294,112,"500 K","s"],["t",294,142,"450 K","s"],["t",84,196,"VA","ms"],["t",169,196,"VB","ms"],["t",222,196,"VC","ms"],["t",56,14,"Pressure","s"],["t",310,196,"Volume","es"]]},o:["500 R(ln 3 + ln 4)","400 R ln 4","450 R(ln 4 \u2212 ln 3)","500 R ln 2"],c:2,s:`A to B is adiabatic, so no heat is exchanged there.
B to C is isothermal at 450 K (B and C lie on the 450 K isotherm in the figure).
Q = W = RT ln(V_C/V_B) per mole = 450R ln({8\xD710\u207B\u2076;6\xD710\u207B\u2076}) = 450R ln({4;3}).
Q = 450R(ln 4 \u2212 ln 3).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A sample of gas at temperature T is adiabatically expanded to double its volume. Adiabatic constant for the gas is \u03B3 = {3;2}. The work done by the gas in the process is (\u03BC = 1 mole)",o:["RT[2\u221A2 \u2212 1]","RT[\u221A2 \u2212 2]","RT[2 \u2212 \u221A2]","RT[1 \u2212 2\u221A2]"],c:2,s:`Adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T\u2082 = T(V/2V)^(1/2) = T/\u221A2.
W = R(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = R(T \u2212 T/\u221A2)/0.5 = 2RT(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2).
W = RT(2 \u2212 \u221A2).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"The heat absorbed by a system in going through the given cyclic process is",fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"P-V graph of a circular cycle: circle touching P = 60 kPa and 340 kPa and V = 60 cc and 340 cc, with its centre marked",d:[["l",50,200,260,200,"a"],["l",50,200,50,20,"a"],["c",130,120,56],["c",130,120,2,"F"],["l",50,64,130,64,"d"],["l",50,176,130,176,"d"],["l",74,200,74,120,"d"],["l",186,200,186,120,"d"],["t",44,68,"340","e"],["t",44,180,"60","e"],["t",44,204,"0","e"],["t",74,214,"60","ms"],["t",186,214,"340","ms"],["t",56,14,"P (in kPa)","s"],["t",260,214,"V (in c.c)","es"]]},o:["19.6 J","61.6 J","431.2 J","616 J"],c:1,s:`For a cycle \u0394U = 0, so Q = W = area enclosed by the circle.
The circle spans 60 to 340 kPa and 60 to 340 cc, so its radius is 140 kPa = 1.4 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa along P and 140 cc = 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3 along V.
Area = \u03C0(1.4 \xD7 10\u2075)(1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = \u03C0 \xD7 19.6 \u2248 61.6 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"During an adiabatic process, if the pressure of a gas is found to be proportional to the cube of its absolute temperature, then the ratio of {C\u209A;C\u1D65} for the gas is",o:["{7;5}","{9;7}","{5;3}","{3;2}"],c:3,s:`For an adiabatic process P \u221D T^(\u03B3/(\u03B3\u22121)).
Given P \u221D T\xB3, so \u03B3/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = 3.
\u03B3 = 3\u03B3 \u2212 3, so 2\u03B3 = 3 and \u03B3 = {3;2}.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:`A total of 48 J heat is given to one mole of helium kept in a cylinder. The temperature of helium increases by 2\xB0C. The work done by the gas is
(Given, R = 8.3 J K\u207B\xB9mol\u207B\xB9.)`,o:["24.9 J","48 J","72.9 J","23.1 J"],c:3,s:`Helium is monoatomic: C_V = {3;2}R.
\u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = 1 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 2 = 24.9 J.
First law: W = Q \u2212 \u0394U = 48 \u2212 24.9 = 23.1 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Two different adiabatic paths for the same gas intersect two isothermal curves as shown in P-V diagram. The relation between the ratio {Va;Vd} and the ratio {Vb;Vc} is",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V diagram with two isothermal curves a-b (upper) and d-c (lower) crossed by two adiabatic curves a-d and b-c; volumes in order Va, Vd, Vb, Vc",d:[["l",50,190,300,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["p","M90 60 Q130 109.5 170 121"],["p","M130 120 Q187.5 146.5 245 153"],["p","M90 60 Q110 104 130 120"],["p","M170 121 Q207.5 144 245 153"],["l",90,60,90,190,"d"],["l",130,120,130,190,"d"],["l",170,121,170,190,"d"],["l",245,153,245,190,"d"],["t",84,56,"a","e"],["t",176,116,"b"],["t",122,130,"d","e"],["t",250,150,"c"],["t",90,206,"Va","ms"],["t",130,206,"Vd","ms"],["t",170,206,"Vb","ms"],["t",245,206,"Vc","ms"],["t",56,16,"P","s"],["t",300,206,"V","e"]]},o:["{Va;Vd} \u2260 {Vb;Vc}","{Va;Vd} = {Vb;Vc}","{Va;Vd} = ({Vb;Vc})^(\u22121)","{Va;Vd} = ({Vb;Vc})\xB2"],c:1,s:`Path a to d is adiabatic: T_a Va^(\u03B3\u22121) = T_d Vd^(\u03B3\u22121), so (Va/Vd)^(\u03B3\u22121) = T_d/T_a.
Path b to c is adiabatic: (Vb/Vc)^(\u03B3\u22121) = T_c/T_b.
Points a, b are on one isotherm and d, c on another, so T_a = T_b and T_d = T_c.
Hence T_d/T_a = T_c/T_b and Va/Vd = Vb/Vc.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"A diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 1.4) does 100 J of work in an isobaric expansion. The heat given to the gas is",o:["150 J","250 J","490 J","350 J"],c:3,s:`Isobaric: Q = nC_P\u0394T and W = nR\u0394T, so Q/W = C_P/R = \u03B3/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
Q/W = 1.4/0.4 = 3.5.
Q = 3.5 \xD7 100 = 350 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"The volume of an ideal gas (\u03B3 = 1.5) is changed adiabatically from 5 litres to 4 litres. The ratio of initial pressure to final pressure is",o:["{2;\u221A5}","{4;5}","{16;25}","{8;5\u221A5}"],c:3,s:`Adiabatic: P\u2081V\u2081^\u03B3 = P\u2082V\u2082^\u03B3.
P\u2081/P\u2082 = (V\u2082/V\u2081)^\u03B3 = (4/5)^1.5.
(4/5)^1.5 = {8;5\u221A5}, since 4^1.5 = 8 and 5^1.5 = 5\u221A5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A real gas within a closed chamber at 27\xB0C undergoes the cyclic process as shown in figure. The gas obeys PV\xB3 = RT equation for the path A to B. The net work done in the complete cycle is (assuming R = 8 J/mol K)",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V diagram: A at (2 m3, 20 N/m2), curve from A down to B at (4 m3, 10 N/m2), straight line from B to C at (2 m3, 10 N/m2), vertical line from C up to A",d:[["l",50,190,300,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,70,110,70,"d"],["l",50,130,230,130,"d"],["l",110,130,110,190,"d"],["l",230,130,230,190,"d"],["p","M110 70 Q170 120 230 130","a"],["l",230,130,110,130,"a"],["l",110,130,110,70,"a"],["t",44,74,"20","e"],["t",44,134,"10","e"],["t",110,206,"2","ms"],["t",230,206,"4","ms"],["t",56,14,"P (N/m\xB2)","s"],["t",300,206,"V (m\xB3)","es"],["t",106,64,"A","e"],["t",238,126,"B"],["t",104,146,"C","e"]]},o:["20 J","\u221220 J","225 J","205 J"],c:3,s:`Path A to B is at T = 300 K, with P = RT/V\xB3. W_AB = \u222BRT V\u207B\xB3 dV = {RT;2}({1;2\xB2} \u2212 {1;4\xB2}).
W_AB = {8\xD7300;2}({1;4} \u2212 {1;16}) = 1200 \xD7 {3;16} = 225 J.
B to C is at constant P = 10 N/m\xB2: W_BC = 10 \xD7 (2 \u2212 4) = \u221220 J.
C to A is at constant volume: W = 0.
Net W = 225 \u2212 20 = 205 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"The pressure and volume of an ideal gas are related as PV^(3/2) = K (constant). The work done when the gas is taken from state A (P\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081) to state B (P\u2082, V\u2082, T\u2082) is",o:["2(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081)","2(P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)","2(P\u2082\u221AV\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081\u221AV\u2081)","2(\u221AP\u2081 V\u2081 \u2212 \u221AP\u2082 V\u2082)"],c:1,s:`W = \u222BP dV = K\u222BV^(\u22123/2) dV = \u22122K(V\u2082^(\u22121/2) \u2212 V\u2081^(\u22121/2)).
So W = 2K({1;\u221AV\u2081} \u2212 {1;\u221AV\u2082}).
Use K = P\u2081V\u2081^(3/2) = P\u2082V\u2082^(3/2): K/\u221AV\u2081 = P\u2081V\u2081 and K/\u221AV\u2082 = P\u2082V\u2082.
W = 2(P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"0.08 kg air is heated at constant volume through 5\xB0C. The specific heat of air at constant volume is 0.17 kcal/kg\xB0C and J = 4.18 joule/cal. The change in its internal energy is approximately",o:["284 J","142 J","298 J","318 J"],c:0,s:`At constant volume no work is done, so \u0394U = Q = m c \u0394T.
Q = 0.08 \xD7 0.17 \xD7 5 = 0.068 kcal = 68 cal.
\u0394U = 68 \xD7 4.18 \u2248 284 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"Two thermodynamical processes are shown in the figure. The molar heat capacity for process A and B are C(A) and C(B). The molar heat capacity at constant pressure and constant volume are represented by C\u209A and C\u1D65, respectively. Choose the correct statement.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"Graph of log P against log V with two straight lines from near the origin: line A is steeper (angle tan inverse gamma), line B rises at 45 degrees",d:[["l",50,190,290,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,15,"a"],["l",72,152,125,46,"a"],["l",112,152,200,64,"a"],["l",72,152,50,190,"d"],["l",112,152,50,190,"d"],["c",72,152,2.5,"F"],["c",112,152,2.5,"F"],["t",78,150,"A"],["t",118,150,"B"],["t",132,44,"tan\u207B\xB9\u03B3","s"],["t",206,68,"45\xB0","s"],["t",44,204,"O","e"],["t",56,14,"log P","s"],["t",290,206,"log V","es"]]},o:["C(A) = 0 and C(B) = \u221E","C(A) > C\u209A > C\u1D65","C\u209A > C\u1D65 > C(A) = C(B)","C(B) = \u221E, C(A) = 0"],c:[0,3],s:`On a log P versus log V graph, a process PV\u207F = constant is a straight line whose slope has magnitude n.
Line A has slope \u03B3 (angle tan\u207B\xB9\u03B3): PV^\u03B3 = constant, an adiabatic process. Q = 0, so C(A) = 0.
Line B has slope 1 (45\xB0): PV = constant, an isothermal process. \u0394T = 0 but Q \u2260 0, so C = Q/(n\u0394T) = \u221E.
Options (a) and (d) both state C(A) = 0 and C(B) = \u221E.`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Choose the correct statement for processes A and B shown in figure.",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V graph: two curves A and B falling from upper left and meeting at one point on the right; B is the steeper curve",d:[["l",50,190,290,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["p","M90 70 Q120 135 230 150"],["p","M140 40 Q165 120 230 150"],["t",100,118,"A","e"],["t",160,66,"B"],["t",56,14,"P","s"],["t",290,206,"V","es"]]},o:["PV^\u03B3 = k for process B and PV = k for process A","{T^\u03B3;P^(\u03B3\u22121)} = k for process A and PV = k for process B","PV = k for process B and A","{P^(\u03B3\u22121);T^\u03B3} = k for process B and T = k for process A"],c:0,s:`The two curves meet at one point. There the adiabatic curve is steeper than the isothermal one, since the slopes are \u2212\u03B3P/V and \u2212P/V.
B is the steeper curve, so B is adiabatic: PV^\u03B3 = k.
A is the flatter curve, so A is isothermal: PV = k.
This matches option (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isobaric and isochoric processes",q:"The given figure represents two isobaric process for the same mass of an ideal gas, then",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"V against T graph with two straight lines through the origin: P2 is the steeper line and P1 the flatter line",d:[["l",50,190,290,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,190,230,50],["l",50,190,260,120],["t",236,50,"P\u2082"],["t",264,120,"P\u2081"],["t",44,204,"O","e"],["t",56,14,"V","s"],["t",290,206,"T","es"]]},o:["P\u2081 = P\u2082","P\u2082 \u2265 P\u2081","P\u2082 > P\u2081","P\u2081 > P\u2082"],c:3,s:`At constant pressure V = (nR/P)T, so the slope of the V-T line is nR/P.
A larger pressure means a smaller slope.
In the figure the line P\u2082 is steeper than the line P\u2081, so P\u2082 < P\u2081, that is P\u2081 > P\u2082.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A source supplies heat to a system at the rate of 1000 W. If the system performs work at a rate of 200 W. The rate at which internal energy of the system increases is",o:["600 W","800 W","1200 W","500 W"],c:1,s:`First law in rate form: dQ/dt = dU/dt + dW/dt.
dU/dt = 1000 \u2212 200 = 800 W.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"An air bubble of volume 1 cm\xB3 rises from the bottom of a lake 40 m deep to the surface at a temperature of 12\xB0C. The atmospheric pressure is 1 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, the density of water is 1000 kg/m\xB3 and g = 10 m/s\xB2. There is no difference of the temperature of water at the depth of 40 m and on the surface. The volume of air bubble when it reaches the surface will be",o:["4 cm\xB3","3 cm\xB3","5 cm\xB3","2 cm\xB3"],c:2,s:`Pressure at the bottom = P\u2080 + \u03C1gh = 1 \xD7 10\u2075 + 1000 \xD7 10 \xD7 40 = 5 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
The temperature is the same, so Boyle's law applies: P\u2081V\u2081 = P\u2082V\u2082.
V\u2082 = {5\xD710\u2075 \xD7 1;1\xD710\u2075} = 5 cm\xB3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Consider two containers A and B containing monoatomic gases at the same pressure (P), volume (V) and temperature (T). The gas in A is compressed isothermally to {1;8} of its initial volume while the gas in B is compressed adiabatically to {1;8} of its original volume. The ratio of final pressure of gas in B to that of gas in A is",o:["8^(3/2)","8","{1;8}","4"],c:3,s:`Gas A (isothermal): PV = constant, so P_A = 8P.
Gas B (adiabatic, \u03B3 = {5;3}): P_B = P \xD7 8^\u03B3 = P \xD7 8^(5/3) = 32P.
P_B/P_A = {32P;8P} = 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A gas is compressed adiabatically, which one of the following statement is NOT true.",o:["There is no heat supplied to the system.","There is no change in the internal energy.","The temperature of the gas increases.","The change in the internal energy is equal to the work done on the gas."],c:1,s:`In adiabatic compression Q = 0, so (a) is true.
Work is done on the gas, so \u0394U = \u2212W = positive: the internal energy rises and so does the temperature. (c) and (d) are true.
(b) is NOT true, because the internal energy changes.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:`1 kg of water at 100\xB0C is converted into steam at 100\xB0C by boiling at atmospheric pressure. The volume of water changes from 1.00 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 as a liquid to 1.671 m\xB3 as steam. The change in internal energy of the system during the process will be
(Given latent heat of vaporisation = 2257 kJ/kg, Atmospheric pressure = 1 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa)`,o:["+2090 kJ","\u22122090 kJ","\u22122426 kJ","+2476 kJ"],c:0,s:`Heat absorbed Q = mL = 1 \xD7 2257 = 2257 kJ.
Work done by the system W = P\u0394V = 10\u2075 \xD7 (1.671 \u2212 0.001) = 1.67 \xD7 10\u2075 J = 167 kJ.
\u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 2257 \u2212 167 = +2090 kJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"The thermodynamic process, in which internal energy of the system remains constant is",o:["isothermal","adiabatic","isochoric","isobaric"],c:0,s:`The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature.
It stays constant only when the temperature is constant, that is in an isothermal process.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Under isothermal condition, the pressure of a gas is given by P = aV\u207B\xB3, where a is a constant and V is the volume of the gas. The bulk modulus at constant temperature is equal to",o:["P","3P","2P","{P;2}"],c:1,s:`Bulk modulus B = \u2212V(dP/dV).
dP/dV = \u22123aV\u207B\u2074, so B = V \xD7 3aV\u207B\u2074 = 3aV\u207B\xB3.
Since P = aV\u207B\xB3, B = 3P.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`The initial pressure and volume of an ideal gas are P\u2080 and V\u2080. The final pressure of the gas when the gas is suddenly compressed to volume {V\u2080;4} will be
(Given \u03B3 = ratio of specific heats at constant pressure and at constant volume)`,o:["P\u2080","P\u2080(4)^\u03B3","P\u2080(4)^(1/\u03B3)","4P\u2080"],c:1,s:`Sudden compression is adiabatic: P\u2080V\u2080^\u03B3 = P(V\u2080/4)^\u03B3.
P = P\u2080 \xD7 4^\u03B3.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A thermodynamic system is taken through cyclic process. The total work done in the process is",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V triangle: C at (2 m3, 100 Pa), D at (2 m3, 400 Pa), E at (4 m3, 100 Pa); path C to D to E to C",d:[["l",50,190,290,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,50,130,50,"d"],["l",50,150,130,150,"d"],["l",130,150,130,190,"d"],["l",210,150,210,190,"d"],["l",130,150,130,50,"a"],["l",130,50,210,150,"a"],["l",210,150,130,150,"a"],["t",44,54,"400","e"],["t",44,154,"100","e"],["t",130,206,"2","ms"],["t",210,206,"4","ms"],["t",124,44,"D","e"],["t",122,166,"C","e"],["t",218,154,"E"],["t",56,14,"P (Pa)","s"],["t",290,206,"V (m\xB3)","es"]]},o:["300 J","200 J","Zero","100 J"],c:0,s:`Work in a cycle equals the area enclosed by the loop.
The loop is the triangle C (2, 100), D (2, 400), E (4, 100): base CE = 2 m\xB3, height = 400 \u2212 100 = 300 Pa.
Area = \xBD \xD7 2 \xD7 300 = 300 J.
The cycle C \u2192 D \u2192 E \u2192 C is clockwise, so the work is positive: 300 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"1 g of a liquid is converted to vapour at 3 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa pressure. If 10% of the heat supplied is used for increasing the volume by 1600 cm\xB3 during this phase change, then the increase in internal energy in the process will be",o:["4800 J","4320 J","4.32 \xD7 10\u2078 J","432000 J"],c:1,s:`Work done W = P\u0394V = 3 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 1600 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 480 J.
This is 10% of the heat supplied, so Q = 4800 J.
\u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 4800 \u2212 480 = 4320 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"In an isothermal change, the change in pressure and volume of a gas can be represented for three different temperature; T\u2083 > T\u2082 > T\u2081 as",fig:{w:340,h:252,alt:"Four small P-V graphs (a) to (d) with three curves each labelled T1, T2, T3: (a) hyperbolas with T1 nearest the axes and T3 farthest; (b) and (c) straight lines; (d) hyperbolas with T3 nearest the axes",d:[["l",15,101,150,101,"a"],["l",15,101,15,16,"a"],["t",11,22,"P","es"],["t",150,115,"V","es"],["p","M25.0 21.0 Q33.5 89.1 140.0 94.6"],["p","M40.0 21.0 Q56.7 74.3 140.0 85.0"],["p","M57.5 21.0 Q78.4 60.4 140.0 73.8"],["t",29,15,"T\u2081","ms"],["t",44,15,"T\u2082","ms"],["t",61.5,15,"T\u2083","ms"],["l",185,101,320,101,"a"],["l",185,101,185,16,"a"],["t",181,22,"P","es"],["t",320,115,"V","es"],["l",193,21,278,96],["l",207,21,292,96],["l",221,21,306,96],["t",193,15,"T\u2081","ms"],["t",207,15,"T\u2082","ms"],["t",221,15,"T\u2083","ms"],["l",15,221,150,221,"a"],["l",15,221,15,136,"a"],["t",11,142,"P","es"],["t",150,235,"V","es"],["l",23,141,108,216],["l",37,141,122,216],["l",51,141,136,216],["t",23,135,"T\u2083","ms"],["t",37,135,"T\u2082","ms"],["t",51,135,"T\u2081","ms"],["l",185,221,320,221,"a"],["l",185,221,185,136,"a"],["t",181,142,"P","es"],["t",320,235,"V","es"],["p","M195.0 141.0 Q203.5 209.1 310.0 214.6"],["p","M210.0 141.0 Q226.7 194.3 310.0 205.0"],["p","M227.5 141.0 Q248.4 180.4 310.0 193.8"],["t",199,135,"T\u2083","ms"],["t",214,135,"T\u2082","ms"],["t",231.5,135,"T\u2081","ms"],["t",82,120,"(a)","ms"],["t",252,120,"(b)","ms"],["t",82,240,"(c)","ms"],["t",252,240,"(d)","ms"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`At constant temperature PV = nRT is a rectangular hyperbola, not a straight line. This rules out (b) and (c).
A higher temperature gives a larger PV, so its hyperbola lies farther from the axes.
The order from the axes outward must be T\u2081, T\u2082, T\u2083. Graph (d) has the reverse order.
Only graph (a) is correct.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Heat is given to an ideal gas in an isothermal process.
A. Internal energy of the gas will decrease.
B. Internal energy of the gas will increase.
C. Internal energy of the gas will not change.
D. The gas will do positive work.
E. The gas will do negative work.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["C and D only","B and D only","A and E only","C and E only"],c:0,s:`Isothermal: T is constant, so \u0394U = 0. (C) is true; (A) and (B) are false.
First law: Q = \u0394U + W = W. Heat is given (Q > 0), so W > 0: the gas does positive work.
Correct: C and D only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"The pressure of a gas changes linearly with volume from A to B as shown in figure. If no heat is supplied to or extracted from the gas then change in the internal energy of the gas will be",fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V graph: straight line from A (200 cc, 10 kPa) up to B (50 cc, 50 kPa)",d:[["l",50,190,300,190,"a"],["l",50,190,50,15,"a"],["l",50,40,100,40,"d"],["l",50,160,250,160,"d"],["l",100,40,100,190,"d"],["l",250,160,250,190,"d"],["l",150,130,150,190,"d"],["l",200,190,200,160,"d"],["l",250,160,100,40,"a"],["t",44,44,"50","e"],["t",44,104,"30","e"],["t",44,134,"20","e"],["t",44,74,"40","e"],["t",44,164,"10","e"],["t",100,206,"50","ms"],["t",150,206,"100","ms"],["t",200,206,"150","ms"],["t",250,206,"200","ms"],["t",94,36,"B","e"],["t",256,164,"A"],["t",56,14,"P (kPa)","s"],["t",300,206,"V (cc)","es"]]},o:["4.5 J","\u22124.5 J","zero","6 J"],c:0,s:`Q = 0, so \u0394U = \u2212W.
Work from A to B = area under the line = average pressure \xD7 change in volume = {10+50;2} kPa \xD7 (50 \u2212 200) cc.
W = 30 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 (\u2212150 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = \u22124.5 J (the gas is compressed).
\u0394U = \u2212W = +4.5 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"Heat energy of 735 J is given to the gas allowing the gas to expand at constant pressure. Each gas molecule rotates around an internal axis but do not oscillate. The increase in the internal energy of the gas will be",o:["572 J","735 J","441 J","525 J"],c:3,s:`Molecules rotate but do not oscillate: a diatomic gas with 5 degrees of freedom, so C_V = {5;2}R and C_P = {7;2}R.
At constant pressure Q = nC_P\u0394T and \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T.
\u0394U = {C_V;C_P} \xD7 Q = {5;7} \xD7 735 = 525 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A hypothetical gas expands adiabatically such that its volume changes from 8 litres to 27 litres. If the ratio of final pressure of the gas to initial pressure of the gas is {16;81}. Then the ratio of {C\u209A;C\u1D65} will be",o:["{3;1}","{3;2}","{1;2}","{4;3}"],c:3,s:`Adiabatic: P\u2082/P\u2081 = (V\u2081/V\u2082)^\u03B3.
{16;81} = ({8;27})^\u03B3 = ({2;3})^(3\u03B3).
Also {16;81} = ({2;3})\u2074, so 3\u03B3 = 4.
\u03B3 = C_P/C_V = {4;3}.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"One mole of an ideal gas expands adiabatically from an initial state (T(A), V\u2080) to final state (T(f), 5V\u2080). Another mole of the same gas expands isothermally from a different initial state (T(B), V\u2080) to the same final state (T(f), 5V\u2080). The ratio of the specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume of this ideal gas is \u03B3. What is the ratio {T(A);T(B)}?",o:["5^(\u03B3\u22121)","5^(1\u2212\u03B3)","5^\u03B3","5^(1+\u03B3)"],c:0,s:`Isothermal expansion: the temperature does not change, so T(B) = T(f).
Adiabatic expansion: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T(A)V\u2080^(\u03B3\u22121) = T(f)(5V\u2080)^(\u03B3\u22121).
T(A)/T(f) = 5^(\u03B3\u22121).
Therefore T(A)/T(B) = 5^(\u03B3\u22121).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A certain amount of gas of volume V at 27\xB0C temperature and pressure 2 \xD7 10\u2077 N m\u207B\xB2 expands isothermally until its volume gets doubled. Later it expands adiabatically until its volume gets redoubled. The final pressure of the gas will be
(Use \u03B3 = 1.5)`,o:["3.536 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa","3.536 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa","1.25 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa","1.25 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa"],c:1,s:`Isothermal, volume V to 2V: P\u2081 = {2\xD710\u2077;2} = 10\u2077 Pa.
Adiabatic, volume 2V to 4V: P\u2082 = P\u2081({2V;4V})^1.5 = 10\u2077 \xD7 (0.5)^1.5.
(0.5)^1.5 = 0.3536, so P\u2082 = 3.536 \xD7 10\u2076 Pa.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A monoatomic gas at pressure P and volume V is suddenly compressed to one eighth of its original volume. The final pressure at constant entropy will be",o:["P","8P","32P","64P"],c:2,s:`Constant entropy with a sudden compression means an adiabatic process: PV^\u03B3 = constant.
For a monoatomic gas \u03B3 = {5;3}.
P_final = P \xD7 8^(5/3) = P \xD7 2\u2075 = 32P.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:`A thermodynamic system is taken from an original state D to an intermediate state E by the linear process shown in the figure. Its volume is then reduced to the original volume from E to F by an isobaric process.
The total work done by the gas from D to E to F will be`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph: straight line from D (2.0 m\xB3, 600 N/m\xB2) to E (5.0 m\xB3, 300 N/m\xB2), then a horizontal line back to F (2.0 m\xB3, 300 N/m\xB2).",d:[["l",50,160,320,160,"a"],["l",50,160,50,20,"a"],["t",320,175,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["t",56,28,"P (N/m\xB2)"],["l",50,60,130,60,"d"],["l",50,110,250,110,"d"],["l",130,60,130,160,"d"],["l",250,110,250,160,"d"],["l",130,60,250,110,"a"],["l",250,110,130,110,"a"],["t",44,64,"600","e"],["t",44,114,"300","e"],["t",130,176,"2.0","m"],["t",250,176,"5.0","m"],["t",50,176,"0","m"],["t",122,56,"D","e"],["t",256,106,"E"],["t",136,128,"F"]]},o:["\u2212450 J","450 J","900 J","1350 J"],c:1,s:`Work = area under the P\u2013V curve (positive for expansion, negative for compression).
D\u2192E: expansion from 2.0 to 5.0 m\xB3 with P falling linearly from 600 to 300 N/m\xB2. Area = \xBD(600 + 300) \xD7 (5.0 \u2212 2.0) = 1350 J.
E\u2192F: isobaric compression at 300 N/m\xB2 from 5.0 to 2.0 m\xB3. Work = \u2212300 \xD7 3 = \u2212900 J.
Total work = 1350 \u2212 900 = 450 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`A sample of an ideal gas is taken through the cyclic process ABCA as shown in figure. It absorbs 40 J of heat during the part AB, no heat during BC and rejects 60 J of heat during CA. A work of 50 J is done on the gas during the part BC. The internal energy of the gas at A is 1560 J.
The workdone by the gas during the part CA is`,fig:{w:340,h:180,alt:"P-V diagram of a triangle: A at bottom right, B directly above A, C at bottom left on the same level as A. Arrows go A to B (up), B to C (down-left), C to A (right).",d:[["l",50,160,310,160,"a"],["l",50,160,50,20,"a"],["t",310,175,"V","e"],["t",56,28,"P"],["l",120,130,230,130,"a"],["l",230,130,230,40,"a"],["l",230,40,120,130,"a"],["t",112,134,"C","e"],["t",236,136,"A"],["t",236,40,"B"]]},o:["20 J","30 J","\u221230 J","\u221260 J"],c:1,s:`For the full cycle \u0394U = 0. In the figure AB is at constant volume, so no work is done in AB.
AB: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 40 \u2212 0 = 40 J.
BC: Q = 0 and work done on the gas is 50 J, so \u0394U = +50 J.
CA: \u0394U = \u2212(40 + 50) = \u221290 J.
First law for CA: W = Q \u2212 \u0394U = \u221260 \u2212 (\u221290) = 30 J (done by the gas).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Starting with the same initial conditions, an ideal gas expands from volume V\u2081 to V\u2082 in three different ways. The work done by the gas is W\u2081 if the process is purely isothermal, W\u2082 if the process is purely adiabatic and W\u2083 if the process is purely isobaric. Then, choose the correct option.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph: from the same starting point at volume V1, a horizontal isobaric line stays on top, the isothermal curve falls below it and the adiabatic curve falls the most, up to volume V2.",d:[["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V","e"],["t",56,28,"P"],["l",100,60,250,60],["p","M100 60 L110 70 L120 78.3 L130 85.4 L140 91.4 L150 96.7 L160 101.3 L170 105.3 L180 108.9 L190 112.1 L200 115 L210 117.6 L220 120 L230 122.2 L240 124.2 L250 126"],["p","M100 60 L110 73.7 L120 84.8 L130 93.8 L140 101.3 L150 107.6 L160 113 L170 117.7 L180 121.7 L190 125.2 L200 128.3 L210 131.1 L220 133.5 L230 135.7 L240 137.7 L250 139.5"],["l",100,60,100,170,"d"],["l",250,60,250,170,"d"],["t",256,64,"Isobaric","s"],["t",256,124,"Isothermal","s"],["t",256,148,"Adiabatic","s"],["t",100,186,"V\u2081","m"],["t",250,186,"V\u2082","m"]]},o:["W\u2081 < W\u2082 < W\u2083","W\u2082 < W\u2083 < W\u2081","W\u2083 < W\u2081 < W\u2082","W\u2082 < W\u2081 < W\u2083"],c:3,s:`Work done by the gas = area under its curve on the P\u2013V graph from V\u2081 to V\u2082.
The isobaric line stays at the highest pressure, so its area (W\u2083) is the largest.
An adiabatic curve falls faster than an isothermal one (P \u221D V^(\u2212\u03B3) against P \u221D V\u207B\xB9), so its area (W\u2082) is the smallest.
Hence W\u2082 < W\u2081 < W\u2083.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A balloon carries a total load of 185 kg at normal pressure and temperature of 27\xB0C. What load will the balloon carry on rising to a height at which the barometric pressure is 45 cm of Hg and the temperature is \u22127\xB0C. Assuming the volume constant?",o:["214.15 kg","123.54 kg","219.07 kg","181.46 kg"],c:1,s:`The load the balloon can carry is fixed by the mass of air it displaces, since the volume is constant.
Mass of a fixed volume of air \u221D P/T (from PV = nRT).
Normal pressure = 76 cm of Hg. T\u2081 = 300 K, T\u2082 = 266 K.
Load\u2082 = 185 \xD7 (45/76) \xD7 (300/266) = 185 \xD7 0.5921 \xD7 1.1278.
Load\u2082 \u2248 123.54 kg.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"For an ideal gas, the instantaneous change in pressure p with volume v is given by the equation dp/dv = \u2212ap. If p = p\u2080 at v = 0 is the given boundary condition, then the maximum temperature one mole of a gas can attain is (Here R is the gas constant)",o:["{ap\u2080;eR}","infinity","0\xB0C","{p\u2080;aeR}"],c:3,s:`Integrate dp/p = \u2212a dv with p = p\u2080 at v = 0: p = p\u2080e^(\u2212av).
For one mole, T = pv/R = p\u2080 v e^(\u2212av)/R.
For maximum T: dT/dv = 0, so e^(\u2212av)(1 \u2212 av) = 0, giving v = 1/a.
T_max = p\u2080(1/a)e\u207B\xB9/R = p\u2080/(aeR).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A monoatomic ideal gas, initially at temperature T\u2081 is enclosed in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston. The gas is allowed to expand adiabatically to a temperature T\u2082 by releasing the piston suddenly. If l\u2081 and l\u2082 are the lengths of the gas column, before and after the expansion respectively, then the value of {T\u2081;T\u2082} will be",o:["({l\u2082;l\u2081})^(2/3)","{l\u2081;l\u2082}","({l\u2081;l\u2082})^(2/3)","{l\u2082;l\u2081}"],c:0,s:`Treat the expansion as adiabatic: T V^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant.
For a monoatomic gas \u03B3 = 5/3, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 2/3.
The volume is proportional to the length of the column (V = A l), so T\u2081 l\u2081^(2/3) = T\u2082 l\u2082^(2/3).
(T\u2081)/(T\u2082) = ((l\u2082)/(l\u2081))^(2/3).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`In the reported figure, there is a cyclic process ABCDA on a sample of 1 mol of a diatomic gas. The temperature of the gas during the process A \u2192 B and C \u2192 D are T\u2081 and T\u2082 (T\u2081 > T\u2082) respectively.
Choose the correct option out of the following for work-done if processes BC and DA are adiabatic.`,fig:{w:340,h:225,alt:"P-V diagram: A at the top left (volume V0, pressure 5P0); AB is the isotherm T1 down to B at 3.5V0; BC is an adiabatic fall to C at 5.5V0; CD is the isotherm T2 back to D at 1.5V0; DA is the adiabatic rise to A.",d:[["l",40,195,330,195,"a"],["l",40,195,40,20,"a"],["t",330,210,"V","e"],["t",46,28,"P"],["p","M80 45 L88.9 67.7 L97.9 84.5 L106.8 97.3 L115.7 107.5 L124.6 115.8 L133.6 122.6 L142.5 128.3 L151.4 133.2 L160.4 137.5 L169.3 141.2 L178.2 144.4 L187.1 147.3 L196.1 149.8 L205 152.1","a"],["p","M205 152.1 L212.1 158 L219.3 162.9 L226.4 166.9 L233.6 170.4 L240.7 173.3 L247.9 175.8 L255 177.9 L262.1 179.8 L269.3 181.4 L276.4 182.8 L283.6 184 L290.7 185.1 L297.9 186 L305 186.8","a"],["p","M305 186.8 L290.7 186.4 L276.4 185.9 L262.1 185.3 L247.9 184.7 L233.6 183.9 L219.3 183.1 L205 182.1 L190.7 181 L176.4 179.6 L162.1 178 L147.9 175.9 L133.6 173.3 L119.3 169.8 L105 165","a"],["p","M105 165 L103.2 162 L101.4 158.6 L99.6 154.7 L97.9 150.4 L96.1 145.4 L94.3 139.7 L92.5 133.1 L90.7 125.6 L88.9 116.9 L87.1 106.7 L85.4 94.9 L83.6 80.9 L81.8 64.5 L80 45","a"],["l",40,45,80,45,"d"],["l",40,165,105,165,"d"],["l",80,45,80,195,"d"],["l",105,165,105,195,"d"],["l",205,152.14285714285714,205,195,"d"],["l",305,186.8181818181818,305,195,"d"],["t",34,49,"5P\u2080","e"],["t",34,169,"P\u2080","e"],["t",72,209,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",111,221,"1.5V\u2080","ms"],["t",205,209,"3.5V\u2080","ms"],["t",305,221,"5.5V\u2080","ms"],["t",84,39,"A"],["t",209,146.14285714285714,"B"],["t",309,180.8181818181818,"C"],["t",111,161,"D"]]},o:["W_AD = W_BC","W_AB = W_DC","W_AB < W_CD","W_BC + W_DA > 0"],c:0,s:`In an adiabatic process W = nR(T\u1D62 \u2212 T_f)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1), which depends only on the end temperatures.
BC goes from T\u2081 to T\u2082, so W_BC = nR(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1).
AD (the reverse of DA) also goes from T\u2081 to T\u2082, so W_AD = nR(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = W_BC.
W_DA = \u2212W_AD, so W_BC + W_DA = 0, not positive.
For the isotherms, V_B/V_A = V_C/V_D, so W_AB = nRT\u2081 ln(V_B/V_A) is larger than W_DC (it has T\u2082 < T\u2081). Options (b) and (c) fail.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"In thermodynamics, heat and work are",o:["extensive thermodynamic state variables","point functions","path functions","intensive thermodynamic state variables."],c:2,s:`State variables (P, V, T, U) depend only on the state of the gas. They are point functions.
Heat and work depend on the route taken between two states: the same initial and final states can have different Q and W.
So heat and work are path functions.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Which one is the correct option for the two different thermodynamic processes?",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four small graphs (A) to (D) showing an isothermal and an adiabatic curve: (A) on P-V with a vertical adiabatic line and a falling isothermal curve, (B) on P-T with a horizontal isothermal line and a rising adiabatic curve, (C) on V-T with a vertical isothermal line and a falling adiabatic curve, (D) on P-T with a vertical isothermal line and a rising adiabatic curve.",d:[["t",2,10,"(A)"],["l",22,95,160,95,"a"],["l",22,95,22,18,"a"],["t",18,28,"P","e"],["t",160,109,"V","e"],["l",125,30,125,95],["t",121,30,"adiabatic","es"],["p","M32 24 Q38 88 150 90"],["t",58,56,"isothermal","s"],["t",172,10,"(B)"],["l",192,95,330,95,"a"],["l",192,95,192,18,"a"],["t",188,28,"P","e"],["t",330,109,"T","e"],["l",196,45,320,45],["t",200,40,"isothermal","s"],["p","M200 90 Q265 85 295 22"],["t",278,88,"adiabatic","s"],["t",2,135,"(C)"],["l",22,220,160,220,"a"],["l",22,220,22,143,"a"],["t",18,153,"V","e"],["t",160,234,"T","e"],["l",100,147,100,220],["t",104,155,"isothermal","s"],["p","M32 149 Q38 213 150 215"],["t",46,175,"adiabatic","s"],["t",172,135,"(D)"],["l",192,220,330,220,"a"],["l",192,220,192,143,"a"],["t",188,153,"P","e"],["t",330,234,"T","e"],["l",270,147,270,220],["t",266,155,"isothermal","es"],["p","M200 215 Q265 210 295 147"],["t",278,213,"adiabatic","s"]]},o:["(C) and (D)","(C) and (A)","(B) and (C)","(A) only"],c:0,s:`Isothermal: T is constant, so on a T-axis graph it is a vertical line, and PV = constant on a P\u2013V graph.
Adiabatic: PV\u1D5E = constant, TV\u1D5E\u207B\xB9 = constant, so V falls and P rises as T rises (P \u221D T^(\u03B3/(\u03B3\u22121)), V \u221D T^(\u22121/(\u03B3\u22121))).
(A): the adiabatic is drawn as a vertical line on P\u2013V, which is wrong. (B): the isothermal is drawn as a horizontal line on P\u2013T, which is wrong.
(C) and (D) show a vertical isothermal line and the correct adiabatic curves.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"The P-V diagram of a diatomic ideal gas system going under cyclic process as shown in figure. The work done during an adiabatic process CD is (use \u03B3 = 1.4)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram of a cycle A B C D: A (1 m\xB3, 200 N/m\xB2), B (1 m\xB3, 100 N/m\xB2), C (4 m\xB3, 100 N/m\xB2), D (3 m\xB3, 200 N/m\xB2). A to B down, B to C right, C to D an adiabatic curve up and left, D to A left.",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["t",66,28,"P"],["l",110,50,110,110,"a"],["l",110,110,260,110,"a"],["p","M260 110 Q218 110 210 50","a"],["l",210,50,110,50,"a"],["l",60,50,110,50,"d"],["l",60,110,110,110,"d"],["l",110,110,110,170,"d"],["l",210,50,210,170,"d"],["l",260,110,260,170,"d"],["t",54,54,"200 N/m\xB2","es"],["t",54,114,"100 N/m\xB2","es"],["t",110,185,"1","m"],["t",210,185,"3","m"],["t",260,185,"4","m"],["t",104,44,"A","e"],["t",104,124,"B","e"],["t",265,114,"C"],["t",212,44,"D"]]},o:["400 J","\u2212400 J","200 J","\u2212500 J"],c:3,s:`For an adiabatic process the work done by the gas is W = (P_f V_f \u2212 P_i V_i)/(1 \u2212 \u03B3).
From the figure (taking D at 3 m\xB3 and C at 4 m\xB3, as in the official solution): P_D V_D = 200 \xD7 3 = 600 J and P_C V_C = 100 \xD7 4 = 400 J.
W_CD = (P_D V_D \u2212 P_C V_C)/(1 \u2212 \u03B3) = (600 \u2212 400)/(1 \u2212 1.4) = 200/(\u22120.4).
W_CD = \u2212500 J (negative: the gas is compressed from C to D).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`n mole of a perfect gas undergoes a cyclic process ABCA (see figure) consisting of the following processes.
A \u2192 B : Isothermal expansion at temperature T so that the volume is doubled from V\u2081 to V\u2082 = 2V\u2081 and pressure changes from P\u2081 to P\u2082.
B \u2192 C : Isobaric compression at pressure P\u2082 to initial volume V\u2081.
C \u2192 A : Isochoric change leading to change of pressure from P\u2082 to P\u2081.
Total workdone in the complete cycle ABCA is`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram: A at (V1, P1), B at (2V1, P2) on an isotherm, C at (V1, P2). A to B along a curve, B to C horizontal to the left, C to A vertical upward.",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V","e"],["t",66,28,"P"],["p","M130 50 L138.6 58 L147.1 65 L155.7 71.2 L164.3 76.7 L172.9 81.6 L181.4 86 L190 90 L198.6 93.6 L207.1 97 L215.7 100 L224.3 102.8 L232.9 105.4 L241.4 107.8 L250 110","a"],["l",250,110,130,110,"a"],["l",130,110,130,50,"a"],["l",60,50,130,50,"d"],["l",60,110,250,110,"d"],["l",130,50,130,170,"d"],["l",250,110,250,170,"d"],["t",54,54,"P\u2081","e"],["t",54,114,"P\u2082","e"],["t",130,186,"V\u2081","m"],["t",250,186,"V\u2082 = 2V\u2081","m"],["t",122,46,"A","e"],["t",256,106,"B"],["t",122,126,"C","e"]]},o:["nRT(ln2 \u2212 {1;2})","0","nRT ln 2","nRT(ln2 + {1;2})"],c:0,s:`Total work = W_AB + W_BC + W_CA.
A\u2192B (isothermal): W = nRT ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) = nRT ln 2.
B\u2192C (isobaric): W = P\u2082(V\u2081 \u2212 V\u2082) = \u2212P\u2082V\u2081. With P\u2082V\u2082 = nRT and V\u2082 = 2V\u2081, P\u2082V\u2081 = nRT/2, so W = \u2212nRT/2.
C\u2192A (isochoric): W = 0.
Total W = nRT(ln2 \u2212 \xBD).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"If one mole of an ideal gas at (P\u2081, V\u2081) is allowed to expand reversibly and isothermally (A to B) its pressure is reduced to one-half of the original pressure (see figure). This is followed by a constant volume cooling till its pressure is reduced to one-fourth of the initial value (B \u2192 C). Then it is restored to its initial state by a reversible adiabatic compression (C to A). The net workdone by the gas is equal to",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram: A at (V1, P1); isothermal expansion to B at (2V1, P1/2); vertical drop to C at (2V1, P1/4); adiabatic compression back to A.",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V","e"],["t",66,28,"P"],["p","M130 50 L138.6 58 L147.1 65 L155.7 71.2 L164.3 76.7 L172.9 81.6 L181.4 86 L190 90 L198.6 93.6 L207.1 97 L215.7 100 L224.3 102.8 L232.9 105.4 L241.4 107.8 L250 110","a"],["l",250,110,250,140,"a"],["p","M250 140 Q150 138 130 50","a"],["l",60,50,130,50,"d"],["l",60,110,250,110,"d"],["l",60,140,250,140,"d"],["l",130,50,130,170,"d"],["l",250,110,250,170,"d"],["t",54,54,"P\u2081","e"],["t",54,114,"P\u2081/2","e"],["t",54,144,"P\u2081/4","e"],["t",130,186,"V\u2081","m"],["t",250,186,"2V\u2081","m"],["t",122,46,"A","e"],["t",256,106,"B"],["t",256,146,"C"]]},o:["0","\u2212{RT;2(\u03B3 \u2212 1)}","RT ln 2","RT(ln2 \u2212 {1;2(\u03B3 \u2212 1)})"],c:3,s:`Let the temperature of the isotherm be T, so P\u2081V\u2081 = RT.
A\u2192B (isothermal): W = RT ln(V_B/V_A) = RT ln 2, since P halves and V doubles (V_B = 2V\u2081).
B\u2192C (isochoric): W = 0.
C\u2192A (adiabatic): P_C V_C = (P\u2081/4)(2V\u2081) = RT/2, so W = (P_C V_C \u2212 P_A V_A)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = (RT/2 \u2212 RT)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = \u2212RT/(2(\u03B3 \u2212 1)).
Net work = RT(ln2 \u2212 (1)/(2(\u03B3 \u2212 1))).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"Thermodynamic process is shown below on a P-V diagram for one mole of an ideal gas. If V\u2082 = 2V\u2081, then the ratio of temperature {T\u2082;T\u2081} is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph of a falling curve PV^(1/2) = constant from (P1, V1, T1) at the top left to (P2, V2, T2) at the right.",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V","e"],["t",66,28,"P"],["p","M120 50 L128.6 54.1 L137.1 57.8 L145.7 61.1 L154.3 64.2 L162.9 67 L171.4 69.6 L180 72 L188.6 74.3 L197.1 76.4 L205.7 78.3 L214.3 80.2 L222.9 81.9 L231.4 83.6 L240 85.1","a"],["l",120,50,120,170,"d"],["l",240,85,240,170,"d"],["t",126,40,"(P\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081)","s"],["t",236,106,"(P\u2082, V\u2082, T\u2082)","es"],["t",190,56,"PV\xBD = constant","s"],["t",120,186,"V\u2081","m"],["t",240,186,"V\u2082","m"]]},o:["\xBD","{1;\u221A2}","2","\u221A2"],c:3,s:`For one mole, P = RT/V.
Substitute in PV^(1/2) = constant: (RT/V)V^(1/2) = constant, so T V^(\u22121/2) is constant, i.e. T \u221D V^(1/2).
(T\u2082)/(T\u2081) = \u221A((V\u2082)/(V\u2081)) = \u221A2.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"An ideal gas undergoes a four step cycle as shown in the P-V diagram. During this cycle, heat is absorbed by the gas in",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"P-V diagram of a rectangle: step 1 along the top to the right, step 2 down the right side, step 3 along the bottom to the left, step 4 up the left side.",d:[["l",50,165,310,165,"a"],["l",50,165,50,20,"a"],["t",310,180,"V","e"],["t",56,28,"P"],["l",110,50,250,50,"a"],["l",250,50,250,110,"a"],["l",250,110,110,110,"a"],["l",110,110,110,50,"a"],["t",180,42,"1","m"],["t",258,84,"2"],["t",180,126,"3","m"],["t",102,84,"4","e"]]},o:["steps 1 and 2","steps 1 and 3","steps 1 and 4","steps 2 and 4"],c:2,s:`Use \u0394U = Q \u2212 W for each step.
Step 1: isobaric expansion at high pressure, so W > 0 and T rises (\u0394U > 0). Q = \u0394U + W > 0: heat absorbed.
Step 2: volume constant, pressure falls, so T falls. Q < 0.
Step 3: isobaric compression, so W < 0 and T falls. Q < 0.
Step 4: volume constant, pressure rises, so T rises. Q > 0: heat absorbed.
Heat is absorbed in steps 1 and 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A balloon filled with helium (32\xB0C and 1.7 atm.) bursts. Immediately afterwards the expansion of helium can be considered as",o:["irreversible isothermal","irreversible adiabatic","reversible adiabatic","reversible isothermal."],c:1,s:`The balloon bursts suddenly, so the gas expands rapidly against a pressure difference, not in small equilibrium steps. The process is irreversible.
The expansion is so fast that there is no time for heat to flow in from the surroundings, so Q = 0: adiabatic.
It is an irreversible adiabatic expansion.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"In an adiabatic process, the density of a diatomic gas becomes 32 times its initial value. The final pressure of the gas is found to be n times the initial pressure. The value of n is",o:["32","326","128","{1;32}"],c:2,s:`In an adiabatic process PV\u1D5E = constant. Since density \u03C1 \u221D 1/V, this becomes P \u221D \u03C1\u03B3.
For a diatomic gas \u03B3 = 7/5.
n = (P\u2082)/(P\u2081) = ((\u03C1\u2082)/(\u03C1\u2081))^\u03B3 = 32^(7/5) = (2\u2075)^(7/5) = 2\u2077 = 128.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A litre of dry air at STP expands adiabatically to a volume of 3 litres. If \u03B3 = 1.40, the work done by air is (3^1.4 = 4.6555) [Take air to be an ideal gas]",o:["60.7 J","100.8 J","90.5 J","48 J"],c:2,s:`At STP, P\u2081 = 1.013 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, V\u2081 = 1 L = 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3, so P\u2081V\u2081 = 101.3 J.
Adiabatic: P\u2082 = P\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^\u03B3 = P\u2081/3^1.4 = P\u2081/4.6555.
P\u2082V\u2082 = 3P\u2081V\u2081/4.6555 = 0.644 P\u2081V\u2081 = 65.3 J.
W = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = (101.3 \u2212 65.3)/0.4 \u2248 90 J.
This matches option (c), 90.5 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A thermodynamic cycle xyzx is shown on a V-T diagram. The P-V diagram that best describes this cycle is (diagrams are schematic and not to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Left: a V-T diagram of the cycle xyzx with x on a dashed line through the origin, y up the line to the right, z level with y and directly above x. Right: four candidate P-V diagrams (a) to (d).",d:[["t",2,12,"V\u2013T diagram","s"],["l",14,225,108,225,"a"],["l",14,225,14,22,"a"],["t",108,240,"T","e"],["t",10,30,"V","e"],["l",14,225,50,175,"d"],["l",50,175,90,108,"a"],["l",90,108,50,108,"a"],["l",50,108,50,175,"a"],["t",56,182,"x"],["t",94,106,"y"],["t",40,106,"z","e"],["t",124,15,"(a)"],["l",134,105,222,105,"a"],["l",134,105,134,19,"a"],["t",222,117,"V","e"],["t",130,27,"P","e"],["l",204,35,156,35,"a"],["l",204,87,204,35,"a"],["p","M156 35 Q156 87 204 87","a"],["t",148,35,"x","e"],["t",208,35,"z"],["t",208,95,"y"],["t",234,15,"(b)"],["l",244,105,332,105,"a"],["l",244,105,244,19,"a"],["t",332,117,"V","e"],["t",240,27,"P","e"],["l",266,35,314,35,"a"],["l",314,35,314,87,"a"],["p","M314 87 Q266 87 266 35","a"],["t",258,35,"x","e"],["t",318,35,"y"],["t",318,95,"z"],["t",124,135,"(c)"],["l",134,225,222,225,"a"],["l",134,225,134,139,"a"],["t",222,237,"V","e"],["t",130,147,"P","e"],["l",156,155,156,207,"a"],["l",156,207,204,207,"a"],["l",204,207,156,155,"a"],["t",148,155,"x","e"],["t",150,215,"y","e"],["t",208,215,"z"],["t",234,135,"(d)"],["l",244,225,332,225,"a"],["l",244,225,244,139,"a"],["t",332,237,"V","e"],["t",240,147,"P","e"],["l",266,207,266,155,"a"],["l",266,207,314,207,"a"],["l",266,155,314,207,"a"],["t",258,155,"x","e"],["t",260,215,"z","e"],["t",318,215,"y"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`x\u2192y: V \u221D T along a line through the origin, so P is constant (isobaric expansion). In P\u2013V: a horizontal line from x to y, moving right.
y\u2192z: V is constant while T falls, so P falls (isochoric). In P\u2013V: a vertical line going down.
z\u2192x: T is constant while V falls: isothermal compression, with P rising along a hyperbola back to x.
Only graph (b) shows this: x top-left, horizontal to y, vertical down to z, then the curve back to x.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"Which of the following is an equivalent cyclic process corresponding to the thermodynamic cyclic given in the figure? where, 1 \u2192 2 is adiabatic. (Graphs are schematic and are not to scale)",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Left: a P-V diagram with 1 at the top left, an adiabatic curve to 2 at the lower right, a horizontal line back to 3 below 1, and a vertical line up to 1. Right (in (b) point 2 lies between 3 and 1 in T): four candidate V-T diagrams (a) to (d).",d:[["t",2,12,"P\u2013V diagram","s"],["l",12,215,108,215,"a"],["l",12,215,12,22,"a"],["t",108,230,"V","e"],["t",8,30,"P","e"],["p","M40 45 Q42 170 95 170","a"],["l",95,170,40,170,"a"],["l",40,170,40,45,"a"],["t",44,42,"1"],["t",98,168,"2"],["t",34,168,"3","e"],["t",124,15,"(a)"],["l",134,105,222,105,"a"],["l",134,105,134,19,"a"],["t",222,117,"T","e"],["t",130,27,"V","e"],["p","M212 91 Q196 85 190 30","a"],["l",190,30,156,91,"a"],["l",156,91,212,91,"a"],["t",190,25,"2","m"],["t",152,103,"3","e"],["t",216,95,"1"],["t",234,15,"(b)"],["l",244,105,332,105,"a"],["l",244,105,244,19,"a"],["t",332,117,"T","e"],["t",240,27,"V","e"],["l",262,91,324,91,"a"],["p","M324 91 Q298 85 294 30","a"],["l",294,30,262,91,"a"],["t",294,25,"2","m"],["t",256,95,"3","e"],["t",328,95,"1"],["t",124,135,"(c)"],["l",134,225,222,225,"a"],["l",134,225,134,139,"a"],["t",222,237,"T","e"],["t",130,147,"V","e"],["p","M216 211 Q190 205 184 155","a"],["l",184,155,150,155,"a"],["p","M150 155 Q158 205 216 211","a"],["t",188,151,"2"],["t",144,155,"3","e"],["t",220,215,"1"],["t",234,135,"(d)"],["l",244,225,332,225,"a"],["l",244,225,244,139,"a"],["t",332,237,"T","e"],["t",240,147,"V","e"],["l",262,211,324,211,"a"],["l",324,211,294,150,"a"],["l",294,150,262,211,"a"],["t",294,145,"2","m"],["t",256,215,"3","e"],["t",328,215,"1"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`1\u21922: adiabatic expansion, so V rises and T falls. 2\u21923: isobaric compression, so V and T both fall (V \u221D T, a straight line). 3\u21921: isochoric heating, so T rises at constant V.
This gives, on V\u2013T: 3\u21921 a horizontal line to the right (V\u2081 = V\u2083); 1\u21922 a falling curve up to the highest V; 2\u21923 a straight line back down to 3.
Also T\u2081 > T\u2082 > T\u2083, so point 2 lies between 3 and 1 in T.
Only graph (b) matches (in (d) the 1\u21922 path would have to be a straight line, which an adiabatic is not).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A thermally insulated vessel contains 150 g of water at 0\xB0C. Then the air from the vessel is pumped out adiabatically. A fraction of water turns into ice and the rest evaporates at 0\xB0C itself. The mass of evaporated water will be closest to (Latent heat of vaporization of water = 2.10 \xD7 10\u2076 J kg\u207B\xB9 and latent heat of fusion of water = 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075 J kg\u207B\xB9)",o:["20 g","35 g","130 g","150 g"],c:0,s:`The vessel is insulated, so the heat needed to evaporate some water comes from the freezing of the rest.
Let m be the mass evaporated; then (150 \u2212 m) g freezes.
Heat absorbed in evaporation = heat released in freezing: m \xD7 2.10 \xD7 10\u2076 = (150 \u2212 m) \xD7 3.36 \xD7 10\u2075.
21m = 3.36(150 \u2212 m), so 24.36m = 504.
m \u2248 20.7 g, which is closest to 20 g.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"The given diagram shows four processes i.e., isochoric, isobaric, isothermal and adiabatic. The correct assignment of the processes, in the same order is given by",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph: four curves leave one point at the top left: a horizontal line a, a gently falling curve b, a steeper falling curve c and a vertical line d going straight down.",d:[["l",60,170,320,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",320,185,"V","e"],["t",66,28,"P"],["l",60,40,300,40,"a"],["p","M60 40 L73.8 66 L87.5 83.3 L101.3 95.7 L115 105 L128.8 112.2 L142.5 118 L156.3 122.7 L170 126.7 L183.8 130 L197.5 132.9 L211.3 135.3 L225 137.5","a"],["p","M60 40 L67.3 60.9 L74.7 76.6 L82 88.8 L89.3 98.5 L96.7 106.4 L104 112.9 L111.3 118.3 L118.7 122.9 L126 126.9 L133.3 130.3 L140.7 133.3 L148 135.9","a"],["l",60,40,60,150,"a"],["t",304,44,"a"],["t",230,122,"b"],["t",148,150,"c"],["t",52,150,"d","e"]]},o:["d a c b","d a b c","a d b c","a d c b"],c:1,s:`Isochoric: V constant, a vertical line: d.
Isobaric: P constant, a horizontal line: a.
Isothermal (PV = constant) and adiabatic (PV\u1D5E = constant) both fall, but the adiabatic curve is steeper because \u03B3 > 1.
So b (gentler) is isothermal and c (steeper) is adiabatic.
In the order isochoric, isobaric, isothermal, adiabatic the answer is d a b c.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"Following figure shows two processes A and B for a gas. If \u0394Q_A and \u0394Q_B are the amount of heat absorbed by the system in two cases, and \u0394U_A and \u0394U_B are changes in internal energies, respectively, then",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"P-V graph: two curves A and B join the same initial point i (lower left) to the same final point f (upper right); curve A lies above curve B.",d:[["l",40,170,310,170,"a"],["l",40,170,40,20,"a"],["t",310,185,"V","e"],["t",46,28,"P"],["p","M110 140 Q125 55 220 50","a"],["p","M110 140 Q205 135 220 50","a"],["t",104,144,"i","e"],["t",224,48,"f"],["t",150,66,"A"],["t",200,118,"B"]]},o:["\u0394Q_A > \u0394Q_B, \u0394U_A > \u0394U_B","\u0394Q_A = \u0394Q_B, \u0394U_A = \u0394U_B","\u0394Q_A < \u0394Q_B, \u0394U_A < \u0394U_B","\u0394Q_A > \u0394Q_B, \u0394U_A = \u0394U_B"],c:3,s:`Internal energy is a state function. A and B join the same initial and final states, so \u0394U_A = \u0394U_B.
First law: \u0394Q = \u0394U + W.
Work = area under the P\u2013V curve. Curve A lies above curve B, so W_A > W_B.
Since \u0394U is equal, \u0394Q_A > \u0394Q_B.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"n moles of an ideal gas with constant volume heat capacity C\u1D65 undergo an isobaric expansion by certain volume. The ratio of the work done in the process, to the heat supplied is",o:["{nR;C\u1D65 \u2212 nR}","{4nR;C\u1D65 \u2212 nR}","{nR;C\u1D65 + nR}","{4nR;C\u1D65 + nR}"],c:2,s:`Isobaric process with temperature change \u0394T: W = P\u0394V = nR\u0394T.
Heat supplied Q = \u0394U + W = C\u1D65\u0394T + nR\u0394T = (C\u1D65 + nR)\u0394T, where C\u1D65 is the heat capacity of the whole gas.
(W)/(Q) = (nR)/(C\u1D65 + nR).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"One mole of an ideal gas passes through a process where pressure and volume obey the relation P = P\u2080[1 \u2212 \xBD({V\u2080;V})\xB2]. Here P\u2080 and V\u2080 are constants. Calculate the change in the temperature of the gas if its volume changes from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080.",o:["{1;4}{P\u2080V\u2080;R}","{5;4}{P\u2080V\u2080;R}","{1;2}{P\u2080V\u2080;R}","{3;4}{P\u2080V\u2080;R}"],c:1,s:`For one mole T = PV/R = (P\u2080/R)(V \u2212 V\u2080\xB2/(2V)).
At V = V\u2080: T\u2081 = (P\u2080/R)(V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080/2) = P\u2080V\u2080/(2R).
At V = 2V\u2080: T\u2082 = (P\u2080/R)(2V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080/4) = 7P\u2080V\u2080/(4R).
\u0394T = T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081 = (7/4 \u2212 1/2)P\u2080V\u2080/R = (5)/(4)(P\u2080V\u2080)/(R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A sample of an ideal gas is taken through the cyclic process abca as shown in the figure. The change in the internal energy of the gas along the path ca is \u2212180 J. The gas absorbs 250 J of heat along the path ab and 60 J along the path bc. The work done by the gas along the path abc is",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"P-V diagram: a is at the lower left, b is on the same level to the right, c is directly above b. a to b is a horizontal line, b to c is vertical, and a curve leads from c back to a.",d:[["l",40,170,310,170,"a"],["l",40,170,40,20,"a"],["t",310,185,"V","e"],["t",46,28,"P"],["l",100,130,220,130,"a"],["l",220,130,220,60,"a"],["p","M220 60 C190 48 165 58 150 82 C135 108 118 118 100 130","a"],["t",94,132,"a","e"],["t",226,136,"b"],["t",226,58,"c"]]},o:["120 J","130 J","140 J","100 J"],c:1,s:`For the whole cycle \u0394U = 0, so \u0394U_abc = \u2212\u0394U_ca = +180 J.
Heat absorbed along abc: Q = 250 + 60 = 310 J.
First law: W_abc = Q \u2212 \u0394U = 310 \u2212 180 = 130 J.
(Path bc is at constant volume, so all this work is done along ab.)`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"A diatomic gas with rigid molecules does 10 J of work when expanded at constant pressure. What would be the heat energy absorbed by the gas, in this process ?",o:["25 J","30 J","35 J","40 J"],c:2,s:`Rigid diatomic molecule: C\u209A = (7/2)R and W = nR\u0394T at constant pressure.
W = nR\u0394T = 10 J.
Q = nC\u209A\u0394T = (7/2)nR\u0394T = (7/2) \xD7 10 = 35 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A gas can be taken from A to B via two different processes ACB and ADB. When path ACB is used 60 J of heat flows into the system and 30 J of work is done by the system. If path ADB is used work done by the system is 10 J. The heat flow into the system in path ADB is",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"P-V diagram of a square: A at the bottom left, C above A, B at the top right, D at the bottom right. ACB goes up then right; ADB goes right then up.",d:[["l",50,150,310,150,"a"],["l",50,150,50,20,"a"],["t",310,165,"V","e"],["t",56,28,"P"],["l",110,110,110,40],["l",110,40,220,40],["l",110,110,220,110],["l",220,110,220,40],["t",104,114,"A","e"],["t",104,38,"C","e"],["t",226,38,"B"],["t",226,118,"D"]]},o:["100 J","80 J","40 J","20 J"],c:2,s:`Internal energy is a state function, so \u0394U is the same along ACB and ADB.
ACB: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 60 \u2212 30 = 30 J.
ADB: Q = \u0394U + W = 30 + 10 = 40 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:"Half mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is heated at constant pressure of 1 atm from 20\xB0C to 90\xB0C. Work done by gas is close to (Gas constant R = 8.31 J/mol K)",o:["291 J","73 J","581 J","146 J"],c:0,s:`At constant pressure W = P\u0394V = nR\u0394T.
n = 0.5 mol and \u0394T = 90 \u2212 20 = 70 K.
W = 0.5 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 70 = 290.85 J \u2248 291 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A rigid diatomic ideal gas undergoes an adiabatic process at room temperature. The relation between temperature and volume for this process is TV\u02E3 = constant, then x is",o:["{2;3}","{3;5}","{2;5}","{5;3}"],c:2,s:`For an adiabatic process TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so x = \u03B3 \u2212 1.
A rigid diatomic gas has \u03B3 = 7/5.
x = 7/5 \u2212 1 = 2/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"In a process, temperature and volume of one mole of an ideal monoatomic gas are varied according to the relation VT = K, where K is a constant. In this process, the temperature of the gas is increased by \u0394T. The amount of heat absorbed by gas is (R is gas constant)",o:["{2K;3}\u0394T","\xBDR\u0394T","{3;2}R\u0394T","\xBDKR\u0394T"],c:1,s:`VT = K gives V = K/T, so dV = \u2212K dT/T\xB2.
For one mole P = RT/V = RT\xB2/K.
Work dW = P dV = (RT\xB2/K)(\u2212K/T\xB2)dT = \u2212R dT, so W = \u2212R\u0394T.
For a monoatomic gas \u0394U = (3/2)R\u0394T.
Q = \u0394U + W = (3/2)R\u0394T \u2212 R\u0394T = \xBDR\u0394T.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"For the given cyclic process CAB as shown for a gas, the work done is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"P-V graph with V in m\xB3 and P in Pa: C at (1, 6), A at (5, 6), B at (5, 1). C to A is horizontal to the right, A to B is vertical down, B to C is a straight line back.",d:[["l",50,180,320,180,"a"],["l",50,180,50,30,"a"],["t",320,195,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["t",44,16,"P (Pa)"],["l",95,48,275,48,"a"],["l",275,48,275,158,"a"],["l",275,158,95,48,"a"],["l",95,180,95,185],["t",95,198,"1","m"],["l",140,180,140,185],["t",140,198,"2","m"],["l",185,180,185,185],["t",185,198,"3","m"],["l",230,180,230,185],["t",230,198,"4","m"],["l",275,180,275,185],["t",275,198,"5","m"],["l",46,158,50,158],["t",42,162,"1","e"],["l",46,136,50,136],["t",42,140,"2","e"],["l",46,114,50,114],["t",42,118,"3","e"],["l",46,92,50,92],["t",42,96,"4","e"],["l",46,70,50,70],["t",42,74,"5","e"],["l",46,48,50,48],["t",42,52,"6","e"],["t",91,42,"C","e"],["t",279,42,"A"],["t",279,162,"B"]]},o:["10 J","1 J","5 J","30 J"],c:0,s:`The work in a cycle is the area enclosed on the P\u2013V graph.
The enclosed shape is a right triangle: base CA = 5 \u2212 1 = 4 m\xB3, height AB = 6 \u2212 1 = 5 Pa.
Area = \xBD \xD7 4 \xD7 5 = 10 J.
The path goes C\u2192A (top, expansion) and returns lower, so the cycle is clockwise and the work is positive: +10 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Two moles of an ideal monoatomic gas occupies a volume V at 27\xB0C. The gas expands adiabatically to a volume 2V. Calculate (i) the final temperature of the gas and (ii) change in its internal energy.",o:["(i) 198 K  (ii) 2.7 kJ","(i) 195 K  (ii) \u22122.7 kJ","(i) 189 K  (ii) \u22122.7 kJ","(i) 195 K  (ii) 2.7 kJ"],c:2,s:`T\u2081 = 300 K, \u03B3 = 5/3. Adiabatic: T\u2081V^(\u03B3\u22121) = T\u2082(2V)^(\u03B3\u22121).
T\u2082 = 300 \xD7 (\xBD)^(2/3) = 300 \xD7 0.63 \u2248 189 K.
\u0394U = nC\u1D65\u0394T = 2 \xD7 (3/2) \xD7 8.31 \xD7 (189 \u2212 300) \u2248 \u22122.77 \xD7 10\xB3 J.
This is about \u22122.7 kJ (the gas cools as it does work).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is compressed isothermally in a rigid vessel to double its pressure at room temperature, 27\xB0C. The work done on the gas will be",o:["300 R","300 R ln 2","300 R ln 6","300 R ln 7"],c:1,s:`Isothermal at T = 300 K: P\u2081V\u2081 = P\u2082V\u2082, so doubling the pressure halves the volume.
Work done on the gas = nRT ln(V\u2081/V\u2082) = nRT ln(P\u2082/P\u2081).
W = 1 \xD7 R \xD7 300 \xD7 ln 2 = 300 R ln 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:`One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is taken along the path ABCA as shown in the PV diagram.
The maximum temperature attained by the gas along the path BC is given by`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"P-V diagram: A at (V0, P0), B at (V0, 3P0) above A, C at (2V0, P0). A to B vertical, B to C a straight sloping line, C to A horizontal.",d:[["l",50,180,320,180,"a"],["l",50,180,50,15,"a"],["t",320,195,"V","e"],["t",56,23,"P"],["l",150,130,150,30,"a"],["l",150,30,250,130,"a"],["l",250,130,150,130,"a"],["l",50,30,150,30,"d"],["l",50,130,150,130,"d"],["l",150,130,150,180,"d"],["l",250,130,250,180,"d"],["t",44,34,"3P\u2080","e"],["t",44,134,"P\u2080","e"],["t",150,196,"V\u2080","m"],["t",250,196,"2V\u2080","m"],["t",145,26,"B","e"],["t",145,144,"A","e"],["t",255,134,"C"]]},o:["{5P\u2080V\u2080;8R}","{25P\u2080V\u2080;8R}","{25P\u2080V\u2080;4R}","{25P\u2080V\u2080;16R}"],c:1,s:`Line BC passes through B(V\u2080, 3P\u2080) and C(2V\u2080, P\u2080): slope = \u22122P\u2080/V\u2080, so P = 5P\u2080 \u2212 2P\u2080V/V\u2080.
For one mole T = PV/R = (5P\u2080V \u2212 2P\u2080V\xB2/V\u2080)/R.
dT/dV = 0 gives 5P\u2080 \u2212 4P\u2080V/V\u2080 = 0, so V = 5V\u2080/4 (it lies between V\u2080 and 2V\u2080).
Then P = 5P\u2080 \u2212 2.5P\u2080 = 2.5P\u2080, and T_max = (2.5P\u2080)(1.25V\u2080)/R = (25P\u2080V\u2080)/(8R).`},{y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"For the P-V diagram given for an ideal gas, out of the following which one correctly represents the T-P diagram?",fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Left: a P-V graph of the hyperbola P = constant/V from point 1 at the top left to point 2 at the lower right. Right: four candidate T-P graphs (a) to (d).",d:[["t",2,12,"P\u2013V diagram","s"],["l",12,215,108,215,"a"],["l",12,215,12,22,"a"],["t",108,230,"V","e"],["t",8,30,"P","e"],["p","M30 50 L34.1 76.1 L38.3 95 L42.4 109.4 L46.5 120.7 L50.6 129.8 L54.8 137.4 L58.9 143.6 L63 149 L67.1 153.6 L71.3 157.6 L75.4 161.1 L79.5 164.2 L83.6 167 L87.8 169.5 L91.9 171.7 L96 173.8","a"],["t",34,48,"1"],["t",70,160,"2"],["t",30,108,"P = Constant/V","s"],["t",124,15,"(a)"],["l",134,105,222,105,"a"],["l",134,105,134,19,"a"],["t",222,117,"P","e"],["t",130,27,"T","e"],["l",182,37,182,77],["c",182,37,2,"F"],["c",182,77,2,"F"],["t",188,39,"2"],["t",188,85,"1"],["t",234,15,"(b)"],["l",244,105,332,105,"a"],["l",244,105,244,19,"a"],["t",332,117,"P","e"],["t",240,27,"T","e"],["l",266,50,316,50],["c",266,50,2,"F"],["c",316,50,2,"F"],["t",266,67,"1","m"],["t",316,67,"2","m"],["t",124,135,"(c)"],["l",134,225,222,225,"a"],["l",134,225,134,139,"a"],["t",222,237,"P","e"],["t",130,147,"T","e"],["l",156,170,206,170],["c",156,170,2,"F"],["c",206,170,2,"F"],["t",156,187,"2","m"],["t",206,187,"1","m"],["t",234,135,"(d)"],["l",244,225,332,225,"a"],["l",244,225,244,139,"a"],["t",332,237,"P","e"],["t",240,147,"T","e"],["l",264,200,320,163],["c",264,200,2,"F"],["c",320,163,2,"F"],["t",264,215,"1","m"],["t",326,159,"2"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:2,s:`P = constant/V means PV = constant: the process is isothermal, so T does not change.
On a T\u2013P graph it is therefore a horizontal line (a vertical line or slanted line would mean T changes).
On the P\u2013V graph the gas goes from 1 (high P) to 2 (low P), so on the T\u2013P graph the point 1 is at larger P than point 2.
The horizontal line with 2 on the left and 1 on the right is graph (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"An ideal gas undergoes a quasi static, reversible process in which its molar heat capacity C remains constant. If during this process the relation of pressure P and volume V is given by PV\u207F = constant, then n is given by (Here C\u209A and C\u1D65 are molar specific heat at constant pressure and constant volume, respectively)",o:["n = {C\u209A;C\u1D65}","n = {C \u2212 C\u209A;C \u2212 C\u1D65}","n = {C\u209A \u2212 C;C \u2212 C\u1D65}","n = {C \u2212 C\u1D65;C \u2212 C\u209A}"],c:1,s:`For a process with PV\u207F = constant, the molar heat capacity is C = C\u1D65 + R/(1 \u2212 n).
So C \u2212 C\u1D65 = R/(1 \u2212 n) and 1 \u2212 n = R/(C \u2212 C\u1D65).
Using R = C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65: 1 \u2212 n = (C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65)/(C \u2212 C\u1D65).
n = 1 \u2212 (C\u209A \u2212 C\u1D65)/(C \u2212 C\u1D65) = (C \u2212 C\u209A)/(C \u2212 C\u1D65).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"\u2018n\u2019 moles of an ideal gas undergoes a process A \u2192 B as shown in the figure. The maximum temperature of the gas during the process will be",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"P-V graph: a straight line from A at (V0, 2P0) down to B at (2V0, P0).",d:[["l",50,180,320,180,"a"],["l",50,180,50,15,"a"],["t",320,195,"V","e"],["t",56,23,"P"],["l",130,60,210,120,"a"],["l",50,60,130,60,"d"],["l",50,120,210,120,"d"],["l",130,60,130,180,"d"],["l",210,120,210,180,"d"],["t",44,64,"2P\u2080","e"],["t",44,124,"P\u2080","e"],["t",130,196,"V\u2080","m"],["t",210,196,"2V\u2080","m"],["t",134,54,"A"],["t",215,116,"B"]]},o:["{9P\u2080V\u2080;4nR}","{3P\u2080V\u2080;2nR}","{9P\u2080V\u2080;2nR}","{9P\u2080V\u2080;nR}"],c:0,s:`The line through A(V\u2080, 2P\u2080) and B(2V\u2080, P\u2080) is P = 3P\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V/V\u2080.
T = PV/(nR) = (3P\u2080V \u2212 P\u2080V\xB2/V\u2080)/(nR).
dT/dV = 0 gives 3P\u2080 \u2212 2P\u2080V/V\u2080 = 0, so V = 3V\u2080/2 (between V\u2080 and 2V\u2080).
Then P = 3P\u2080 \u2212 1.5P\u2080 = 1.5P\u2080, and T_max = (1.5P\u2080)(1.5V\u2080)/(nR) = (9P\u2080V\u2080)/(4nR).`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"The ratio of work done by an ideal monoatomic gas to the heat supplied to it in an isobaric process is",o:["{2;5}","{3;2}","{3;5}","{2;3}"],c:0,s:`Isobaric process: W = nR\u0394T.
Monoatomic gas: C\u209A = (5/2)R, so Q = nC\u209A\u0394T = (5/2)nR\u0394T.
(W)/(Q) = (nR\u0394T)/((5/2)nR\u0394T) = 2/5.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"200 g water is heated from 40\xB0C to 60\xB0C. Ignoring the slight expansion of water, the change in its internal energy is close to (Given specific heat of water = 4184 J/kg/K)",o:["167.4 kJ","8.4 kJ","4.2 kJ","16.7 kJ"],c:3,s:`Water barely expands, so the work done is negligible (W \u2248 0) and \u0394U \u2248 Q.
Q = mc\u0394T = 0.2 kg \xD7 4184 J/kg/K \xD7 20 K = 16 736 J.
\u0394U \u2248 16.7 kJ.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A gas is enclosed in a cylinder with a movable frictionless piston. Its initial thermodynamic state at pressure P\u1D62 = 10\u2075 Pa and volume V\u1D62 = 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 changes to a final state at P_f = (1/32) \xD7 10\u2075 Pa and V_f = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 in an adiabatic quasi-static process, such that P\xB3V\u2075 = constant. Consider another thermodynamic process that brings the system from the same initial state to the same final state in two steps: an isobaric expansion at P\u1D62 followed by an isochoric (isovolumetric) process at volume V_f. The amount of heat supplied to the system in the two-step process is approximately",o:["112 J","294 J","588 J","813 J"],c:2,s:`P\xB3V\u2075 = constant means PV^(5/3) = constant, so \u03B3 = 5/3.
\u0394U depends only on the end states: \u0394U = (P_f V_f \u2212 P\u1D62 V\u1D62)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1). P_f V_f = (10\u2075/32)(8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 25 J and P\u1D62V\u1D62 = 100 J.
\u0394U = (25 \u2212 100)/(2/3) = \u2212112.5 J.
Work in the two-step path (only the isobaric step does work): W = P\u1D62(V_f \u2212 V\u1D62) = 10\u2075 \xD7 7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 700 J.
Q = \u0394U + W = \u2212112.5 + 700 \u2248 588 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Consider a spherical shell of radius R at temperature T. The black body radiation inside it can be considered as an ideal gas of photons with internal energy per unit volume u = {U;V} \u221D T\u2074 and pressure p = {1;3}({U;V}). If the shell now undergoes an adiabatic expansion the relation between T and R is",o:["T \u221D {1;R}","T \u221D {1;R\xB3}","T \u221D e^(\u2212R)","T \u221D e^(\u22123R)"],c:0,s:`Write U = aT\u2074V. Adiabatic: dU = \u2212p dV, with p = U/(3V) = aT\u2074/3.
d(aT\u2074V) = aT\u2074dV + 4aT\xB3V dT = \u2212(aT\u2074/3)dV.
4aT\xB3V dT = \u2212(4/3)aT\u2074 dV, so dT/T = \u2212(1/3) dV/V.
T \u221D V^(\u22121/3). Since V \u221D R\xB3, T \u221D 1/R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`An ideal gas goes through a reversible cycle a \u2192 b \u2192 c \u2192 d has the V-T diagram shown below. Process d \u2192 a and b \u2192 c are adiabatic.
The corresponding P-V diagram for the process is (all figures are schematic and not drawn to scale)`,fig:{w:340,h:245,alt:"Left: a V-T diagram of the cycle a b c d with two straight sloping lines (a to b and c to d) and two adiabatic curves (b to c and d to a). Right: four candidate P-V diagrams (a) to (d).",d:[["t",2,12,"V\u2013T diagram","s"],["l",12,215,108,215,"a"],["l",12,215,12,22,"a"],["t",108,230,"T","e"],["t",8,30,"V","e"],["p","M40 172 Q55 180 75 180","a"],["l",75,180,100,166,"a"],["p","M100 166 Q70 160 60 141","a"],["l",60,141,40,172,"a"],["t",70,194,"a"],["t",104,166,"b"],["t",58,136,"c"],["t",34,172,"d","e"],["t",124,15,"(a)"],["l",134,105,222,105,"a"],["l",134,105,134,19,"a"],["t",222,117,"V","e"],["t",130,27,"P","e"],["l",152,35,202,35,"a"],["p","M202,35 Q202,87 208,87","a"],["l",208,87,156,87,"a"],["p","M156,87 Q152,87 152,35","a"],["t",148,31,"a","e"],["t",206,31,"b"],["t",212,99,"c"],["t",152,99,"d","e"],["t",234,15,"(b)"],["l",244,105,332,105,"a"],["l",244,105,244,19,"a"],["t",332,117,"V","e"],["t",240,27,"P","e"],["l",266,87,318,87,"a"],["p","M318,87 Q312,87 312,35","a"],["l",312,35,262,35,"a"],["p","M262,35 Q262,87 266,87","a"],["t",258,31,"d","e"],["t",316,31,"c"],["t",322,99,"b"],["t",262,99,"a","e"],["t",124,135,"(c)"],["l",134,225,222,225,"a"],["l",134,225,134,139,"a"],["t",222,237,"V","e"],["t",130,147,"P","e"],["l",152,155,202,155,"a"],["p","M202,155 Q208,155 208,207","a"],["l",208,207,156,207,"a"],["p","M156,207 Q156,155 152,155","a"],["t",148,151,"a","e"],["t",206,151,"b"],["t",212,219,"c"],["t",152,219,"d","e"],["t",234,135,"(d)"],["l",244,225,332,225,"a"],["l",244,225,244,139,"a"],["t",332,237,"V","e"],["t",240,147,"P","e"],["l",266,207,318,207,"a"],["p","M318,207 Q318,155 312,155","a"],["l",312,155,262,155,"a"],["p","M262,155 Q266,155 266,207","a"],["t",258,151,"d","e"],["t",316,151,"c"],["t",322,219,"b"],["t",262,219,"a","e"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`a\u2192b and c\u2192d are straight lines through the origin on a V\u2013T graph, so P is constant on each: isobaric. The line a\u2192b is less steep than c\u2192d, so P_ab > P_cd.
a\u2192b: isobaric expansion at the higher pressure, so a horizontal line at the top going right.
b\u2192c: adiabatic expansion (T falls, V rises), so P falls steeply.
c\u2192d: isobaric compression at the lower pressure: a horizontal line at the bottom going left.
d\u2192a: adiabatic compression, P rises steeply. Graph (a) shows this clockwise cycle with a, b on top and d, c below.`},{y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:"The equation of state for a gas is given by PV = nRT + \u03B1V, where n is the number of moles and \u03B1 is a positive constant. The initial temperature and pressure of one mole of the gas contained in a cylinder are T\u2080 and P\u2080 respectively. The work done by the gas when its temperature doubles isobarically will be",o:["{P\u2080T\u2080R;P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1}","{P\u2080T\u2080R;P\u2080 + \u03B1}","P\u2080T\u2080R ln 2","P\u2080T\u2080R"],c:0,s:`For n = 1 and constant pressure P\u2080: P\u2080V = RT + \u03B1V, so V(P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1) = RT and V = RT/(P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1).
When T goes from T\u2080 to 2T\u2080: \u0394V = R(2T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080)/(P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1) = RT\u2080/(P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1).
W = P\u2080\u0394V = (P\u2080T\u2080R)/(P\u2080 \u2212 \u03B1).`},{y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`One mole of diatomic ideal gas undergoes a cyclic process ABC as shown in figure. The process BC is adiabatic. The temperatures at A, B and C are 400 K, 800 K and 600 K respectively.
Choose the correct statement.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram: A at the bottom left (400 K), B directly above A (800 K), C at the bottom right on the same level as A (600 K). A to B vertical up, B to C an adiabatic curve down to the right, C to A horizontal to the left.",d:[["l",50,175,320,175,"a"],["l",50,175,50,25,"a"],["t",320,190,"V","e"],["t",56,33,"P"],["l",100,140,100,45,"a"],["p","M100 45 Q104 140 240 140","a"],["l",240,140,100,140,"a"],["t",94,148,"A","e"],["t",94,44,"B","e"],["t",246,144,"C"],["t",108,150,"400 K","s"],["t",108,40,"800 K","s"],["t",190,126,"600 K","s"]]},o:["The change in internal energy in the process BC is \u2212500 R.","The change in internal energy in whole cyclic process is 250 R.","The change in internal energy in the process CA is 700 R.","The change in internal energy in the process AB is \u2212350 R."],c:0,s:`For a diatomic gas C\u1D65 = (5/2)R and \u0394U = nC\u1D65\u0394T.
BC: \u0394U = (5/2)R(600 \u2212 800) = \u2212500R. So (a) is correct.
Whole cycle: \u0394U = 0 (internal energy is a state function), so (b) is wrong.
CA: \u0394U = (5/2)R(400 \u2212 600) = \u2212500R, not 700R. AB: \u0394U = (5/2)R(800 \u2212 400) = +1000R, not \u2212350R.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"Given that 1 g of water in liquid phase has volume 1 cm\xB3 and in vapour phase 1671 cm\xB3 at atmospheric pressure and the latent heat of vaporization of water is 2256 J/g; the change in the internal energy (in Joules) for 1 g of water at 373 K when it changes from liquid phase to vapour phase at the same temperature is",o:["2256","167","2089","1"],c:2,s:`Heat supplied Q = mL = 1 g \xD7 2256 J/g = 2256 J, at constant atmospheric pressure (P \u2248 10\u2075 Pa).
Work done by the system on expansion: W = P\u0394V = 10\u2075 \xD7 (1671 \u2212 1) \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u2248 167 J.
First law: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 2256 \u2212 167 = 2089 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:"The pressure of an ideal gas varies with volume as P = \u03B1V, where \u03B1 is a constant. One mole of the gas is allowed to undergo expansion such that its volume becomes \u2018m\u2019 times its initial volume. The work done by the gas in the process is",o:["{\u03B1V;2}(m\xB2\u22121)","{\u03B1\xB2V\xB2;2}(m\xB2\u22121)","{\u03B1;2V}(m\xB2\u22121)","{\u03B1V\xB2;2}(m\xB2\u22121)"],c:3,s:`Work done by the gas is W = \u222BP dV.
Here P = \u03B1V, so W = \u03B1\u222BV dV from V to mV.
W = {\u03B1;2}[(mV)\xB2 \u2212 V\xB2] = {\u03B1V\xB2;2}(m\xB2 \u2212 1).
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"ptp",st:"Isobaric and isochoric processes",q:"Two moles of ideal helium gas are in a rubber balloon at 30\xB0C. The balloon is fully expandable and can be assumed to require no energy in its expansion. The temperature of the gas in the balloon is slowly changed to 35\xB0C. The amount of heat required in raising the temperature is nearly (Take R = 8.31 J/mol.K)",o:["62 J","104 J","124 J","208 J"],c:3,s:`The balloon expands freely, so the gas stays at constant pressure (isobaric).
Helium is monatomic: C\u209A = {5;2}R.
Q = nC\u209A\u0394T = 2 \xD7 {5;2} \xD7 8.31 \xD7 (35 \u2212 30).
Q = 2 \xD7 20.775 \xD7 5 \u2248 208 J.
Answer: option (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"5.6 litre of helium gas at STP is adiabatically compressed to 0.7 litre. Taking the initial temperature to be T\u2081, the work done in the process is",o:["{9;8}RT\u2081","{3;2}RT\u2081","{15;8}RT\u2081","{9;2}RT\u2081"],c:0,s:`Moles of gas: n = 5.6/22.4 = 0.25. Helium: \u03B3 = 5/3.
Volume ratio V\u2081/V\u2082 = 5.6/0.7 = 8.
Adiabatic: T\u2081V\u2081^(\u03B3\u22121) = T\u2082V\u2082^(\u03B3\u22121), so T\u2082 = T\u2081 \xD7 8^(2/3) = 4T\u2081.
Work done on the gas = nR(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = 0.25R \xD7 3T\u2081 \xF7 (2/3) = {9;8}RT\u2081.
(The gas itself does \u2212{9;8}RT\u2081; the book's answer gives the magnitude.)
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"100 g of water is heated from 30\xB0C to 50\xB0C. Ignoring the slight expansion of the water, the change in its internal energy is (specific heat of water is 4184 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["4.2 kJ","8.4 kJ","84 kJ","2.1 kJ"],c:1,s:`Expansion is ignored, so no work is done and \u0394U = Q.
Q = mc\u0394T = 0.1 kg \xD7 4184 \xD7 20 K = 8368 J.
\u0394U \u2248 8.4 kJ.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"An insulated container of gas has two chambers separated by an insulating partition. One of the chambers has volume V\u2081 and contains ideal gas at pressure P\u2081 and temperature T\u2081. The other chamber has volume V\u2082 and contains ideal gas at pressure P\u2082 and temperature T\u2082. If the partition is removed without doing any work on the gas, the final equilibrium temperature of the gas in the container will be",o:["{T\u2081T\u2082(P\u2081V\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082);P\u2081V\u2081T\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082T\u2082}","{T\u2081T\u2082(P\u2081V\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082);P\u2081V\u2081T\u2082+P\u2082V\u2082T\u2081}","{P\u2081V\u2081T\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082T\u2082;P\u2081V\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082}","{P\u2081V\u2081T\u2082+P\u2082V\u2082T\u2081;P\u2081V\u2081+P\u2082V\u2082}"],c:1,s:`No heat and no work: the total internal energy stays the same, so n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 = (n\u2081 + n\u2082)T.
Moles: n\u2081 = P\u2081V\u2081/(RT\u2081), n\u2082 = P\u2082V\u2082/(RT\u2082).
T = (P\u2081V\u2081 + P\u2082V\u2082) \xF7 (P\u2081V\u2081/T\u2081 + P\u2082V\u2082/T\u2082).
Multiply top and bottom by T\u2081T\u2082: T = T\u2081T\u2082(P\u2081V\u2081 + P\u2082V\u2082) \xF7 (P\u2081V\u2081T\u2082 + P\u2082V\u2082T\u2081).
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"When a system is taken from state i to state f along the path iaf, it is found that Q = 50 cal and W = 20 cal. Along the path ibf, Q = 36 cal. W along the path ibf is",fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"Rectangle on a state diagram with corners i (bottom left), b (bottom right), a (top left) and f (top right); arrows run i to a, a to f, i to b and b to f.",d:[["r",100,30,140,90],["l",100,75,100,60,"a"],["l",240,75,240,60,"a"],["l",150,30,175,30,"a"],["l",150,120,175,120,"a"],["t",90,26,"a","e"],["t",250,26,"f"],["t",90,134,"i","e"],["t",250,134,"b"]]},o:["14 cal","6 cal","16 cal","66 cal"],c:1,s:`Internal energy is a state function, so \u0394U is the same for both paths.
Path iaf: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 50 \u2212 20 = 30 cal.
Path ibf: W = Q \u2212 \u0394U = 36 \u2212 30 = 6 cal.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:"The work of 146 kJ is performed in order to compress one kilo mole of gas adiabatically and in this process the temperature of the gas increases by 7\xB0C. The gas is (R = 8.3 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["monoatomic","diatomic","triatomic","a mixture of monoatomic and diatomic."],c:1,s:`Adiabatic: Q = 0, so the work done on the gas goes into internal energy: \u0394U = 146000 J.
n = 1 kilo mole = 1000 mol, \u0394T = 7 K.
\u0394U = nC_V\u0394T gives C_V = 146000/(1000 \xD7 7) \u2248 20.9 J/mol K.
C_V/R = 20.9/8.3 \u2248 2.5 = {5;2}, so f = 5: the gas is diatomic.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A system goes from A to B via two processes I and II as shown in figure. If \u0394U\u2081 and \u0394U\u2082 are the changes in internal energies in the processes I and II respectively, then",fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"P\u2013V diagram with two paths from A on the left to B on the right; path II is the upper curve and path I the lower curve.",d:[["l",40,150,310,150,"a"],["l",40,150,40,20,"a"],["t",310,164,"V","e"],["t",44,28,"P"],["p","M100 85 Q190 25 280 85","a"],["p","M100 85 Q190 145 280 85","a"],["t",92,89,"A","e"],["t",288,89,"B"],["t",190,40,"II","m"],["t",190,138,"I","m"]]},o:["\u0394U\u2082 > \u0394U\u2081","\u0394U\u2082 < \u0394U\u2081","\u0394U\u2081 = \u0394U\u2082","relation between \u0394U\u2081 and \u0394U\u2082 cannot be determined"],c:2,s:`Internal energy is a state function: it depends only on the initial and final states, not on the path.
Both processes go from A to B, so \u0394U\u2081 = \u0394U\u2082.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Which of the following is incorrect regarding the first law of thermodynamics?",o:["It introduces the concept of the internal energy.","It introduces the concept of entropy.","It is not applicable to any cyclic process.","It is a restatement of the principle of conservation of energy."],c:[1,2],s:`The first law is \u0394Q = \u0394U + \u0394W. It introduces the internal energy and is the law of conservation of energy for heat and work, so (a) and (d) are correct.
(b) is incorrect: entropy comes from the second law, not the first.
(c) is incorrect: the first law applies to every process, including cyclic ones (where \u0394U = 0 and Q = W).
Answer: options (b) and (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"pts",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Which of the following statements is correct for any thermodynamic system?",o:["The internal energy changes in all processes.","Internal energy and entropy are state functions.","The change in entropy can never be zero.","The work done in an adiabatic process is always zero."],c:1,s:`(a) is wrong: internal energy does not change in an isothermal process of an ideal gas.
(b) is correct: internal energy and entropy depend only on the state of the system.
(c) is wrong: entropy change is zero in a reversible adiabatic process.
(d) is wrong: in an adiabatic process W = \u2212\u0394U, which is generally not zero.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"An ideal gas initially at P\u2081, V\u2081 is expanded to P\u2082, V\u2082 and then compressed adiabatically to the same volume V\u2081 and pressure P\u2083. If W is the net work done by the gas in the complete process which of the following is true?",o:["W > 0; P\u2083 > P\u2081","W < 0; P\u2083 > P\u2081","W > 0; P\u2083 < P\u2081","W < 0; P\u2083 < P\u2081"],c:1,s:`Take the first step as an expansion along an isotherm (the picture intended in the book), then an adiabatic compression back to V\u2081.
On a P\u2013V graph an adiabatic curve is steeper than an isotherm, so the adiabatic compression returns to a higher pressure: P\u2083 > P\u2081.
At every volume between V\u2081 and V\u2082 the pressure during the compression is higher than during the expansion, so the work done on the gas exceeds the work done by it.
Net work done by the gas W < 0.
Answer: option (b): W < 0, P\u2083 > P\u2081.`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"Which of the following parameters does not characterize the thermodynamic state of matter?",o:["temperature","pressure","work","volume"],c:2,s:`State variables such as temperature, pressure and volume describe the state of the system.
Work is a path-dependent quantity: it describes a process, not a state.
So work does not characterize the state.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"The PT diagram for an ideal gas is shown in the figure, where AC is an adiabatic process. Find the corresponding PV diagram.",fig:{w:340,h:240,alt:"Top left: the given P\u2013T diagram with A above B on a vertical line, B to C along a horizontal line, and a curve from A to C. Then four P\u2013V diagrams (a) to (d), each showing a point A at the top, a horizontal line between points B and C at the bottom, and curves from A.",d:[["t",58,10,"Given: P\u2013T","ms"],["l",14,106,106,106,"a"],["l",14,106,14,18,"a"],["t",106,120,"T","e"],["t",18,26,"P"],["l",44,34,44,92],["l",44,92,92,92],["p","M44 34 Q50 88 92 92"],["t",40,38,"A","e"],["t",42,106,"B","e"],["t",96,96,"C"],["t",171,10,"(a)","ms"],["l",127,106,219,106,"a"],["l",127,106,127,18,"a"],["t",219,120,"V","e"],["t",131,26,"P"],["l",161,92,205,92],["p","M153 32 Q155 86 161 92"],["p","M153 32 Q161 88 205 92"],["t",149,30,"A","e"],["t",161,106,"B","m"],["t",208,106,"C","m"],["t",284,10,"(b)","ms"],["l",240,106,332,106,"a"],["l",240,106,240,18,"a"],["t",332,120,"V","e"],["t",244,26,"P"],["l",274,92,318,92],["p","M266 32 Q268 86 274 92"],["p","M266 32 Q274 88 318 92"],["t",262,30,"A","e"],["t",274,106,"C","m"],["t",321,106,"B","m"],["t",58,130,"(c)","ms"],["l",14,226,106,226,"a"],["l",14,226,14,138,"a"],["t",106,240,"V","e"],["t",18,146,"P"],["l",40,152,40,212],["l",40,212,92,212],["p","M40 152 Q46 204 92 212"],["t",36,150,"A","e"],["t",36,226,"B","e"],["t",95,226,"C","m"],["t",171,130,"(d)","ms"],["l",127,226,219,226,"a"],["l",127,226,127,138,"a"],["t",219,240,"V","e"],["t",131,146,"P"],["l",153,152,153,212],["l",153,212,205,212],["p","M153 152 Q159 204 205 212"],["t",149,150,"A","e"],["t",149,226,"C","e"],["t",208,226,"B","m"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`AB is a vertical line on the P\u2013T graph: temperature is constant, so AB is an isothermal. BC is a horizontal line: pressure is constant, so BC is an isobar. AC is the adiabatic.
On a P\u2013V graph the adiabatic through A is steeper than the isotherm through A.
So the curve to C (adiabatic) falls more steeply and ends at a smaller volume, while the isotherm AB ends further right at B. The isobar joins C and B horizontally.
This matches graph (b): the steep curve A to C on the left and the flatter A to B on the right.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Which of the following graphs correctly represents the variation of \u03B2 = \u2212{dV/dP;V} with P for an ideal gas at constant temperature?",fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Four graphs of \u03B2 against P: (a) a falling hyperbola that flattens at large P; (b) a flat start that then curves sharply down; (c) a curve rising steeply then flattening; (d) an S-shaped falling curve.",d:[["t",75,10,"(a)","ms"],["l",20,105,150,105,"a"],["l",20,105,20,17,"a"],["t",150,119,"P","e"],["t",24,25,"\u03B2"],["p","M30 30 Q40 95 140 100"],["t",245,10,"(b)","ms"],["l",190,105,320,105,"a"],["l",190,105,190,17,"a"],["t",320,119,"P","e"],["t",194,25,"\u03B2"],["p","M200 35 Q235 33 245 62 L250 100"],["t",75,135,"(c)","ms"],["l",20,230,150,230,"a"],["l",20,230,20,142,"a"],["t",150,244,"P","e"],["t",24,150,"\u03B2"],["p","M30 220 Q40 160 100 155"],["t",245,135,"(d)","ms"],["l",190,230,320,230,"a"],["l",190,230,190,142,"a"],["t",320,244,"P","e"],["t",194,150,"\u03B2"],["p","M200 160 Q210 160 225 185 Q240 215 285 220"]]},o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`At constant temperature PV = constant, so P dV + V dP = 0.
Then dV/dP = \u2212V/P, so \u03B2 = \u2212(1/V)(dV/dP) = 1/P.
\u03B2 varies inversely with P, so \u03B2 against P is a rectangular hyperbola that falls steeply at small P and flattens at large P.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"An ideal gas is taken through the cycle A \u2192 B \u2192 C \u2192 A, as shown in the figure. If the net heat supplied to the gas in the cycle is 5 J, the work done by the gas in the process C \u2192 A is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"V\u2013P diagram of a triangle: A at P = 10, V = 1; B directly above A at V = 2; C to the left of B at V = 2. Arrows go A to B (up), B to C (left), C to A (diagonal down).",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",310,184,"P (N/m\xB2)","e"],["t",64,28,"V (m\xB3)"],["l",220,170,220,164],["t",220,184,"10","m"],["l",60,120,66,120],["t",54,124,"1","e"],["l",60,70,66,70],["t",54,74,"2","e"],["l",220,120,220,76,"a"],["l",220,70,150,70,"a"],["l",150,70,214,118,"a"],["t",228,124,"A"],["t",226,66,"B"],["t",146,64,"C","e"],["l",220,120,220,170,"d"],["l",60,70,150,70,"d"]]},o:["\u22125 J","\u221210 J","\u221215 J","\u221220 J"],c:0,s:`Over a cycle \u0394U = 0, so the net work done equals the net heat: W_cycle = 5 J.
A \u2192 B: pressure stays at 10 N/m\xB2 while V goes from 1 to 2 m\xB3: W = 10 \xD7 1 = 10 J.
B \u2192 C: both points are at V = 2 m\xB3 (a horizontal line on this graph), so volume is constant and W = 0.
W_AB + W_BC + W_CA = 5 gives 10 + 0 + W_CA = 5, so W_CA = \u22125 J.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"P-V plots for two gases during adiabatic processes are shown in the figure. Plots 1 and 2 should correspond respectively to",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"P\u2013V graph with two falling adiabatic curves starting close together; curve 1 is the upper, flatter one and curve 2 is the lower, steeper one.",d:[["l",40,165,310,165,"a"],["l",40,165,40,25,"a"],["t",310,179,"V","e"],["t",44,33,"P"],["p","M90 30 Q110 130 280 140"],["p","M90 30 Q100 150 250 158"],["t",284,138,"1"],["t",254,160,"2"]]},o:["He and O\u2082","O\u2082 and He","He and Ar","O\u2082 and N\u2082"],c:1,s:`In an adiabatic process PV^\u03B3 = constant, so the P\u2013V curve is steeper for a larger \u03B3.
He is monatomic: \u03B3 = 5/3 \u2248 1.67. O\u2082 is diatomic: \u03B3 = 7/5 = 1.4.
Curve 1 is less steep, so it has the smaller \u03B3 and is O\u2082. Curve 2 is steeper, so it is He.
(O\u2082 and N\u2082 have the same \u03B3, so option (d) cannot give two different curves.)
Answer: option (b): O\u2082 and He.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"In a given process on an ideal gas dW = 0 and dQ < 0. Then for the gas",o:["the temperature will decrease","the volume will increase","the pressure will remain constant","the temperature will increase."],c:0,s:`First law: dQ = dU + dW. With dW = 0 this gives dU = dQ < 0.
For an ideal gas dU = nC_V dT, so dT < 0.
The temperature decreases. (Volume is constant since dW = 0, so it cannot increase, and the pressure falls with T.)
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"An ideal monoatomic gas is taken round the cycle ABCDA as shown in the P-V diagram (see figure). The workdone during the cycle is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P\u2013V diagram of a rectangular cycle: A (P, V) bottom left, B (2P, V) top left, C (2P, 2V) top right, D (P, 2V) bottom right, traversed A to B to C to D to A.",d:[["l",50,175,310,175,"a"],["l",50,175,50,25,"a"],["t",310,189,"V","e"],["t",54,33,"P"],["l",130,115,130,60,"a"],["l",130,55,205,55,"a"],["l",210,60,210,110,"a"],["l",210,115,135,115,"a"],["r",130,55,80,60],["t",122,50,"B (2P, V)","e"],["t",216,50,"C (2P, 2V)"],["t",122,130,"A (P, V)","e"],["t",216,130,"D (P, 2V)"]]},o:["PV","2PV","{PV;2}","zero."],c:0,s:`Work done in a cycle equals the area enclosed by the loop on the P\u2013V graph.
The loop is a rectangle with sides \u0394P = 2P \u2212 P = P and \u0394V = 2V \u2212 V = V.
The cycle A \u2192 B \u2192 C \u2192 D \u2192 A runs clockwise, so the work done by the gas is positive.
W = P \xD7 V = PV.
Answer: option (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`In the given P-V diagram, a monoatomic gas (\u03B3 = {5;3}) is first compressed adiabatically from state A to state B. Then it expands isothermally from state B to state C.
[Given: ({1;3})^(0.6) \u2248 0.5, ln 2 \u2248 0.7].
Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P\u2013V diagram: A at V = 0.80 m\xB3 and 100 kPa; B at about V = 0.40 m\xB3 and 300 kPa reached by an adiabatic curve from A; C directly above A at V = 0.80 m\xB3 reached from B by an isothermal curve; vertical line C to A.",d:[["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["t",320,184,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["t",54,28,"P (kPa)"],["l",50,50,150,50,"d"],["l",50,130,250,130,"d"],["l",250,170,250,50,"d"],["l",150,50,150,170,"d"],["t",46,54,"300","e"],["t",46,134,"100","e"],["t",250,184,"0.80","m"],["t",150,184,"0.40","m"],["p","M250 130 Q170 100 150 50","a"],["p","M150 50 Q190 100 250 112","a"],["l",250,112,250,128],["t",256,110,"C"],["t",256,140,"A"],["t",144,44,"B","e"]]},o:["The magnitude of the total work done in the process A \u2192 B \u2192 C is 144 kJ.","The magnitude of the work done in the process B \u2192 C is 84 kJ.","The magnitude of the work done in the process A \u2192 B is 60 kJ.","The magnitude of the work done in the process C \u2192 A is zero."],c:[1,2,3],s:`From the graph: A = (0.80 m\xB3, 100 kPa), B is at 300 kPa. Adiabatic A \u2192 B: P_A V_A^\u03B3 = P_B V_B^\u03B3, so V_B = 0.8 \xD7 (1/3)^(3/5) = 0.8 \xD7 0.5 = 0.4 m\xB3.
W_AB = (P_B V_B \u2212 P_A V_A)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) in magnitude = (300\xD70.4 \u2212 100\xD70.8) kJ \xF7 (2/3) = 40 \xD7 1.5 = 60 kJ (done on the gas). So (c) is true.
B \u2192 C is isothermal with V_C = 0.8 m\xB3: W_BC = P_B V_B ln(V_C/V_B) = 120 kJ \xD7 ln 2 = 120 \xD7 0.7 = 84 kJ. So (b) is true.
C \u2192 A is at constant volume, so W = 0 and (d) is true.
Net work A \u2192 B \u2192 C = \u221260 + 84 = +24 kJ, not 144 kJ, so (a) is false.
Answer: (b), (c), (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A bubble has surface tension S. The ideal gas inside the bubble has ratio of specific heats \u03B3 = {5;3}. The bubble is exposed to the atmosphere and it always retains its spherical shape. When the atmospheric pressure is P\u2090\u2081, the radius of the bubble is found to be r\u2081 and the temperature of the enclosed gas is T\u2081. When the atmospheric pressure is P\u2090\u2082, the radius of the bubble and the temperature of the enclosed gas are r\u2082 and T\u2082, respectively.
Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,o:["If the surface of the bubble is a perfect heat insulator, then (r\u2081/r\u2082)\u2075 = (P\u2090\u2082 + 2S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 2S/r\u2081).","If the surface of the bubble is a perfect heat insulator, then the total internal energy of the bubble including its surface energy does not change with the external atmospheric pressure.","If the surface of the bubble is a perfect heat conductor and the change in atmospheric temperature is negligible, then (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB3 = (P\u2090\u2082 + 4S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 4S/r\u2081).","If the surface of the bubble is a perfect heat insulator, then (T\u2082/T\u2081)^(5/2) = (P\u2090\u2082 + 4S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 4S/r\u2081)."],c:[2,3],s:`A bubble has two surfaces, so the gas pressure is P = P\u2090 + 4S/r. Its volume is proportional to r\xB3.
(c) Isothermal (conducting surface, T unchanged): PV = constant, so P r\xB3 = constant and (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB3 = (P\u2090\u2082 + 4S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 4S/r\u2081). True.
(a) Adiabatic: PV^\u03B3 = constant gives P r\u2075 = constant, so (r\u2081/r\u2082)\u2075 = (P\u2090\u2082 + 4S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 4S/r\u2081). The statement uses 2S instead of 4S, so it is false.
(d) Adiabatic: P^(1\u2212\u03B3)T^\u03B3 = constant, so P^(\u22122/3)T^(5/3) = constant and (T\u2082/T\u2081)^(5/2) = P\u2082/P\u2081 = (P\u2090\u2082 + 4S/r\u2082)/(P\u2090\u2081 + 4S/r\u2081). True.
(b) The atmosphere does work on the bubble when its pressure changes, so the total energy of the bubble does change. False.
Answer: (c) and (d).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`An ideal gas of density \u03C1 = 0.2 kg m\u207B\xB3 enters a chimney of height h at the rate of \u03B1 = 0.8 kg s\u207B\xB9 from its lower end, and escapes through the upper end as shown in the figure. The cross-sectional area of the lower end is A\u2081 = 0.1 m\xB2 and the upper end is A\u2082 = 0.4 m\xB2. The pressure and the temperature of the gas at the lower end are 600 Pa and 300 K, respectively, while its temperature at the upper end is 150 K. The chimney is heat insulated so that the gas undergoes adiabatic expansion. Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2 and the ratio of specific heats of the gas \u03B3 = 2. Ignore atmospheric pressure.
Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Funnel-shaped chimney of height h: narrow lower end of area A\u2081 where gas enters, widening to a larger upper end of area A\u2082 where gas leaves; arrows point upward.",d:[["p","M130 190 V150 L70 50"],["p","M170 190 V150 L230 50"],["l",70,50,230,50,"d"],["t",150,40,"A\u2082","m"],["t",150,205,"A\u2081","m"],["l",140,185,140,160,"a"],["l",150,185,150,160,"a"],["l",160,185,160,160,"a"],["l",115,110,115,80,"a"],["l",150,110,150,80,"a"],["l",185,110,185,80,"a"],["l",262,50,262,190,"b"],["t",270,125,"h"],["t",180,198,"P\u2081 = 600 Pa, T\u2081 = 300 K","s"],["t",240,40,"T\u2082 = 150 K","s"]]},o:["The pressure of the gas at the upper end of the chimney is 300 Pa.","The velocity of the gas at the lower end of the chimney is 40 m s\u207B\xB9 and at the upper end is 20 m s\u207B\xB9.","The height of the chimney is 590 m.","The density of the gas at the upper end is 0.05 kg m\u207B\xB3."],c:1,s:`Mass flow \u03B1 = \u03C1Av. Lower end: v\u2081 = 0.8/(0.2 \xD7 0.1) = 40 m/s.
Adiabatic: P^(1\u2212\u03B3)T^\u03B3 = constant, so P\u2082 = P\u2081(T\u2082/T\u2081)^(\u03B3/(\u03B3\u22121)) = 600 \xD7 (1/2)\xB2 = 150 Pa. So (a) is false.
Density \u03C1 \u221D P/T, so \u03C1\u2082 = 0.2 \xD7 (150/600) \xD7 (300/150) = 0.1 kg/m\xB3, so (d) is false. Then v\u2082 = 0.8/(0.1 \xD7 0.4) = 20 m/s, so (b) is true.
Energy per kg of flowing gas: v\xB2/2 + gz + C_pT is constant, with C_pT = {\u03B3;\u03B3\u22121}(P/\u03C1) = 2 \xD7 3000 = 6000 J/kg at the bottom and 3000 J/kg at the top.
800 + 6000 = 200 + 3000 + 10h, so h = 360 m, not 590 m. So (c) is false.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas undergoes a cyclic process as shown in the figure (where V is the volume and T is the temperature). Which of the following statements is (are) true?",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"T\u2013V diagram of a closed cycle with four sides: I on the left going up, II on top going right, III on the right going down, IV on the bottom going left. Top and bottom sides are horizontal; left and right sides are slightly slanted curves.",d:[["l",40,175,310,175,"a"],["l",40,175,40,25,"a"],["t",310,189,"V","e"],["t",44,33,"T"],["l",95,140,82,60,"a"],["l",82,60,228,60,"a"],["l",228,60,241,140,"a"],["l",241,140,95,140,"a"],["l",40,60,82,60,"d"],["l",40,140,95,140,"d"],["t",72,102,"I","e"],["t",155,52,"II","m"],["t",248,102,"III"],["t",168,156,"IV","m"]]},o:["Process I is an isochoric process","In process II, gas absorbs heat","In process IV, gas releases heat","Processes I and III are not isobaric"],c:[1,2,3],s:`Read the graph: II and IV are horizontal (T constant), so they are isotherms. I and III are slanted lines in which T changes together with V.
(a) In process I the volume is decreasing while T rises, so it is not isochoric. False.
(b) Process II is isothermal expansion: \u0394U = 0 and W > 0, so Q = W > 0 and the gas absorbs heat. True.
(c) Process IV is isothermal compression: \u0394U = 0 and W < 0, so Q < 0 and the gas releases heat. True.
(d) An isobaric process would be a straight line through the origin of the V\u2013T graph (V \u221D T). I and III are not, so they are not isobaric. True.
Answer: (b), (c), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"One mole of an ideal gas in initial state A undergoes a cyclic process ABCA, as shown in the figure. Its pressure at A is P\u2080. Choose the correct option(s) from the following.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"V\u2013T diagram: A at (T\u2080, V\u2080), B directly above A at 4V\u2080, C on the V\u2080 level to the left of A. Arrows go A to B, B to C along a straight line, and C to A along the V\u2080 level.",d:[["l",50,175,310,175,"a"],["l",50,175,50,15,"a"],["t",310,189,"T","e"],["t",54,23,"V"],["l",50,50,230,50,"d"],["l",50,140,130,140,"d"],["l",230,140,230,175,"d"],["l",230,138,230,56,"a"],["l",230,50,134,134,"a"],["l",130,140,226,140,"a"],["t",44,54,"4V\u2080","e"],["t",44,144,"V\u2080","e"],["t",230,190,"T\u2080","m"],["t",238,50,"B"],["t",238,144,"A"],["t",124,156,"C","e"]]},o:["Internal energies at A and B are the same.","Work done by the gas in process AB is P\u2080V\u2080 ln 4.","Pressure at C is {P\u2080;4}.","Temperature at C is {T\u2080;4}."],c:[0,1],s:`A and B lie on the vertical line T = T\u2080, so AB is isothermal and T_A = T_B. For an ideal gas U depends only on T, so U_A = U_B. (a) is true.
At A: P\u2080V\u2080 = RT\u2080 (one mole).
Isothermal work A \u2192 B: W = RT\u2080 ln(V_B/V_A) = RT\u2080 ln 4 = P\u2080V\u2080 ln 4. (b) is true.
The line BC is straight but does not pass through the origin of the V\u2013T graph, so V/T is not constant there: BC is not an isobar. Nothing in the data fixes P_C = P\u2080/4 or T_C = T\u2080/4, and the point C in the figure is not at T\u2080/4. So (c) and (d) are not correct.
Answer: (a) and (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"The figure shows the P-V plot of an ideal gas taken through a cycle ABCDA. The part ABC is a semi-circle and CDA is half of an ellipse. Then,",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"P\u2013V diagram: points A (V = 2, P = 3), B (V = 3, P = 2), C (V = 2, P = 1) on a semicircle on the right, and a half ellipse on the left through D (P = 2) joining C back to A; the cycle runs clockwise.",d:[["l",85,180,85,40,"d"],["l",40,135,200,135,"d"],["l",130,180,130,40,"d"],["l",40,90,200,90,"d"],["l",175,180,175,40,"d"],["l",40,45,200,45,"d"],["l",40,180,210,180,"a"],["l",40,180,40,30,"a"],["t",210,194,"V","e"],["t",44,38,"P"],["p","M130 45 A45 45 0 0 1 130 135"],["p","M130 135 A60 45 0 0 1 130 45"],["l",124,45,136,45,"a"],["l",175,84,175,96,"a"],["l",136,135,124,135,"a"],["l",70,96,70,84,"a"],["t",130,38,"A","m"],["t",182,94,"B"],["t",130,150,"C","m"],["t",62,92,"D","e"],["t",85,194,"1","ms"],["t",34,139,"1","es"],["t",130,194,"2","ms"],["t",34,94,"2","es"],["t",175,194,"3","ms"],["t",34,49,"3","es"]]},o:["the process during path A \u2192 B is isothermal","heat flows out of the gas during the path B \u2192 C \u2192 D","work done during the path A \u2192 B \u2192 C is zero","positive work is done by the gas in the cycle ABCDA"],c:[1,3],s:`Read from the figure: A = (V 2, P 3), B = (3, 2), C = (2, 1), D is on the left at P = 2 with V < 3.
(a) PV is 6 at both A and B, but halfway along the arc (V \u2248 2.7, P \u2248 2.7) PV \u2248 7.3. T is not constant along the arc, so A \u2192 B is not isothermal. False.
(b) B \u2192 C \u2192 D: volume decreases, so W < 0. P is the same (2) at B and D but V_D < V_B, so T_D < T_B and \u0394U < 0. Q = \u0394U + W < 0, heat flows out. True.
(c) The area under the arc ABC is not zero, so the work is not zero. False.
(d) The cycle runs clockwise, so the net work done by the gas (area of the loop) is positive. True.
Answer: (b) and (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A given quantity of an ideal gas is at pressure P and absolute temperature T. The isothermal bulk modulus of the gas is",o:["{2;3}P","P","{3;2}P","2P."],c:1,s:`Bulk modulus K = \u2212\u0394P/(\u0394V/V) = \u2212V (dP/dV).
Isothermal: PV = constant, so P dV + V dP = 0 and dP/dV = \u2212P/V.
K = \u2212V \xD7 (\u2212P/V) = P.
Answer: option (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"Two identical containers A and B with frictionless pistons contain the same ideal gas at the same temperature and the same volume V. The mass of the gas in A is m_A, and that in B is m_B. The gas in each cylinder is now allowed to expand isothermally to the same final volume 2V. The changes in the pressure in A and B are found to be \u0394P and 1.5 \u0394P respectively. Then",o:["4m_A = 9m_B","2m_A = 3m_B","3m_A = 2m_B","9m_A = 4m_B."],c:2,s:`Initially P = mRT/(MV). Isothermal expansion to 2V halves the pressure, so the pressure falls by \u0394P = P/2 = mRT/(2MV).
So the pressure drop is proportional to the mass of gas: \u0394P \u221D m.
\u0394P_A/\u0394P_B = m_A/m_B, so \u0394P/(1.5\u0394P) = m_A/m_B = 2/3.
That gives 3m_A = 2m_B.
Answer: option (c).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"ptp",st:"P\u2013V graphs and polytropic processes",q:"An ideal gas is taken from the state A (pressure P, volume V) to the state B (pressure P/2, volume 2V) along a straight line path in the P-V diagram. Select the correct statement(s) from the following.",o:["The workdone by the gas in the process A to B exceeds the work that would be done by it if the system were taken from A to B along the isotherm.","In the T-V diagram, the path AB becomes a part of a parabola.","In the P-T diagram, the path AB becomes a part of a hyperbola.","In going from A to B, the temperature T of the gas first increases to a maximum value and then decreases."],c:[0,1,3],s:`Let P\u2032 and V\u2032 be the running pressure and volume. The straight line is P\u2032 = {3P;2} \u2212 {P;2V}V\u2032, and PV (hence T) has the same value at A and B.
(a) Work along the line = area of the trapezoid = {P + P/2;2} \xD7 V = 0.75PV. Work along the isotherm = PV ln 2 \u2248 0.69PV. So the line gives more work. True.
(b) T \u221D P\u2032V\u2032 = {3P;2}V\u2032 \u2212 {P;2V}V\u2032\xB2, a parabola in V\u2032. True.
(c) In terms of P\u2032 the volume is V\u2032 = V(3 \u2212 2P\u2032/P), so T \u221D P\u2032V\u2032 is quadratic in P\u2032: a parabola, not a hyperbola. False.
(d) T \u221D P\u2032V\u2032 is the same at A and B and larger in between, so T first rises to a maximum and then falls. True.
Answer: (a), (b), (d).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"For an ideal gas",o:["the change in internal energy in a constant pressure process from temperature T\u2081 to T\u2082 is equal to nC_V(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081), where C_V is the molar specific heat at constant volume and n the number of moles of the gas.","the change in internal energy of the gas and the work done by the gas are equal in magnitude in an adiabatic process.","the internal energy does not change in an isothermal process.","no heat is added or removed in an adiabatic process."],c:[0,1,2,3],s:`(a) At constant pressure \u0394U = nC_V(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081) always, because U of an ideal gas depends only on T. True.
(b) Adiabatic: Q = 0, so 0 = \u0394U + W and |\u0394U| = |W|. True.
(c) In an isothermal process \u0394T = 0, so \u0394U = nC_V\u0394T = 0. True.
(d) Adiabatic means no heat is added or removed. True.
Answer: all four, (a), (b), (c) and (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Heat capacities and degrees of freedom",q:`The internal energy of air in 4 m \xD7 4 m \xD7 3 m sized room at 1 atmosphere pressure will be ___ \xD7 10\u2076 J.
(Consider air as diatomic molecule)`,n:12,s:`Diatomic gas at ordinary temperature has 5 degrees of freedom, so U = {5;2}nRT = {5;2}PV.
P = 1 atm \u2248 10\u2075 Pa, V = 4 \xD7 4 \xD7 3 = 48 m\xB3.
U = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 48 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u2077 J = 12 \xD7 10\u2076 J.
Answer: 12.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`An ideal gas has undergone through the cyclic process as shown in the figure. Work done by the gas in the entire cycle is ___ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 J.
(Take \u03C0 = 3.14)`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"P\u2013V graph of a circular cycle: the circle spans pressure from 300 to 500 kPa and volume from 150 to 350 cm\xB3.",d:[["l",60,175,270,175,"a"],["l",60,175,60,25,"a"],["t",330,200,"P (kPa)","e"],["t",64,33,"V (cm\xB3)"],["c",180,90,50],["l",60,40,180,40,"d"],["l",60,140,180,140,"d"],["l",60,90,130,90,"d"],["l",130,90,130,175,"d"],["l",180,40,180,175,"d"],["l",230,90,230,175,"d"],["t",54,44,"350","es"],["t",54,94,"250","es"],["t",54,144,"150","es"],["t",130,188,"300","ms"],["t",180,188,"400","ms"],["t",230,188,"500","ms"]]},n:314,s:`Work in a cycle = area enclosed on the P\u2013V graph. The loop is a circle.
Its diameter along P is 500 \u2212 300 = 200 kPa, so the radius is 100 kPa = 10\u2075 Pa.
Its diameter along V is 350 \u2212 150 = 200 cm\xB3, so the radius is 100 cm\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB3.
Area = \u03C0 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 10\u03C0 = 31.4 J = 314 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 J.
Answer: 314.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"An ideal gas initially at 0\xB0C temperature, is compressed suddenly to one fourth of its volume. If the ratio of specific heat at constant pressure to that at constant volume is 3/2, the change in temperature due to the thermodynamic process is ___ K.",n:273,s:`Sudden compression means adiabatic: T\u2081V\u2081^(\u03B3\u22121) = T\u2082V\u2082^(\u03B3\u22121).
T\u2081 = 273 K, V\u2082 = V\u2081/4, \u03B3 \u2212 1 = \xBD.
T\u2082 = 273 \xD7 4^(1/2) = 546 K.
Change in temperature = 546 \u2212 273 = 273 K.
Answer: 273.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isobaric and isochoric processes",q:"A container of fixed volume contains a gas at 27\xB0C. To double the pressure of the gas, the temperature of gas should be raised to ___ \xB0C.",n:327,s:`Volume is fixed, so P/T is constant (T in kelvin): P\u2082/P\u2081 = T\u2082/T\u2081.
T\u2081 = 27 + 273 = 300 K, P\u2082 = 2P\u2081.
T\u2082 = 2 \xD7 300 = 600 K = 600 \u2212 273 = 327 \xB0C.
Answer: 327.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"An ideal monoatomic gas of n moles is taken through a cycle WXYZW consisting of consecutive adiabatic and isobaric quasi-static processes, as shown in the schematic V-T diagram. The volume of the gas at W, X and Y points are, 64 cm\xB3, 125 cm\xB3 and 250 cm\xB3, respectively. If the absolute temperature of the gas T_W at the point W is such that nRT_W = 1 J (R is the universal gas constant), then the amount of heat absorbed (in J) by the gas along the path XY is ___.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"V\u2013T diagram of the cycle W X Y Z W: W to X an adiabatic curve, X to Y an isobaric straight line, Y to Z an adiabatic curve, Z to W an isobaric straight line; volumes at W, X, Y are 64, 125, 250 cm\xB3.",d:[["l",40,170,300,170,"a"],["l",40,170,40,30,"a"],["t",300,184,"T","e"],["t",44,38,"V"],["l",40,170,180,70,"d"],["l",40,170,259,119,"d"],["p","M149 144 Q124 135 110 120","a"],["l",110,120,180,70,"a"],["p","M180 70 Q210 102 259 119","a"],["l",259,119,153,143,"a"],["l",110,120,110,170,"d"],["l",149,144,149,170,"d"],["l",40,120,110,120,"d"],["l",40,144,149,144,"d"],["l",40,70,180,70,"d"],["t",36,74,"250","es"],["t",36,123,"125","es"],["t",36,148,"64","es"],["t",104,126,"X","e"],["t",152,158,"W"],["t",180,62,"Y","m"],["t",266,119,"Z"]]},n:1.6,s:`W \u2192 X is adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant with \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 2/3.
T_W \xD7 64^(2/3) = T_X \xD7 125^(2/3), so T_W \xD7 16 = T_X \xD7 25 and T_X = {16;25}T_W.
X \u2192 Y is isobaric: V/T is constant, so T_Y = T_X \xD7 {250;125} = 2T_X.
Q = nC_p\u0394T = n \xD7 {5;2}R \xD7 (T_Y \u2212 T_X) = {5;2}nRT_X.
Q = {5;2} \xD7 {16;25} \xD7 nRT_W = {5;2} \xD7 {16;25} \xD7 1 = 1.6 J.
Answer: 1.6.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"One mole of an ideal gas undergoes two different cyclic processes I and II, as shown in the P-V diagrams below. In cycle I, processes a, b, c and d are isobaric, isothermal, isobaric and isochoric, respectively. In cycle II, processes a\u2032, b\u2032, c\u2032 and d\u2032 are isothermal, isochoric, isobaric and isochoric, respectively. The total work done during cycle I is W_I and that during cycle II is W_II. The ratio W_I/W_II is ___.",fig:{w:340,h:185,alt:"Two P\u2013V diagrams. Cycle I: a isobaric at 4P\u2080 from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080, b isothermal from 2V\u2080 to 4V\u2080 (pressure falls to 2P\u2080), c isobaric at 2P\u2080 back to V\u2080, d isochoric up at V\u2080. Cycle II: a\u2032 isothermal from V\u2080 at 4P\u2080 to 2V\u2080 at 2P\u2080, b\u2032 isochoric down to P\u2080, c\u2032 isobaric back to V\u2080 at P\u2080, d\u2032 isochoric up at V\u2080.",d:[["l",30,160,150,160,"a"],["l",30,160,30,20,"a"],["t",150,174,"V","e"],["t",34,28,"P"],["l",190,160,310,160,"a"],["l",190,160,190,20,"a"],["t",310,174,"V","e"],["t",194,28,"P"],["t",80,16,"(I)","ms"],["t",250,16,"(II)","ms"],["l",55,40,78,40,"a"],["p","M80 40 Q100 62 130 100","a"],["l",130,100,57,100,"a"],["l",55,100,55,43,"a"],["t",66,34,"a","m"],["t",116,62,"b"],["t",92,114,"c","m"],["t",48,72,"d","e"],["l",30,40,55,40,"d"],["l",30,100,130,100,"d"],["l",55,160,55,100,"d"],["l",80,160,80,40,"d"],["l",130,160,130,100,"d"],["t",26,44,"4P\u2080","es"],["t",26,104,"2P\u2080","es"],["t",55,174,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",80,174,"2V\u2080","ms"],["t",130,174,"4V\u2080","ms"],["p","M215 42 Q230 70 240 100","a"],["l",240,100,240,127,"a"],["l",240,130,217,130,"a"],["l",215,130,215,45,"a"],["t",232,60,"a\u2032"],["t",246,118,"b\u2032"],["t",228,144,"c\u2032","m"],["t",208,88,"d\u2032","e"],["l",190,40,215,40,"d"],["l",190,100,240,100,"d"],["l",190,130,215,130,"d"],["l",215,160,215,130,"d"],["l",240,160,240,100,"d"],["t",186,44,"4P\u2080","es"],["t",186,104,"2P\u2080","es"],["t",186,134,"P\u2080","es"],["t",215,174,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",240,174,"2V\u2080","ms"]]},n:2,s:`Cycle I: a at 4P\u2080 from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080: W = 4P\u2080V\u2080. b isothermal at nRT = 4P\u2080 \xD7 2V\u2080 = 8P\u2080V\u2080 from 2V\u2080 to 4V\u2080: W = 8P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2. c at 2P\u2080 from 4V\u2080 to V\u2080: W = 2P\u2080 \xD7 (\u22123V\u2080) = \u22126P\u2080V\u2080. d isochoric: 0.
W_I = 8P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2 \u2212 2P\u2080V\u2080.
Cycle II: a\u2032 isothermal from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080 with nRT = 4P\u2080V\u2080: W = 4P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2. b\u2032 and d\u2032 are isochoric: 0. c\u2032 at P\u2080 from 2V\u2080 to V\u2080: W = \u2212P\u2080V\u2080.
W_II = 4P\u2080V\u2080 ln 2 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080.
Ratio = (8 ln 2 \u2212 2)/(4 ln 2 \u2212 1) = 2.
Answer: 2.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2023",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`An incompressible liquid is kept in a container having a weightless piston with a hole. A capillary tube of inner radius 0.1 mm is dipped vertically into the liquid through the airtight piston hole, as shown in the figure. The air in the container is isothermally compressed from its original volume V\u2080 to {100;101}V\u2080 with the movable piston. Considering air as an ideal gas, the height (h) of the liquid column in the capillary above the liquid level in cm is ___.
[Given: Surface tension of the liquid is 0.075 N m\u207B\xB9, atmospheric pressure is 10\u2075 N m\u207B\xB2, acceleration due to gravity (g) is 10 m s\u207B\xB2, density of the liquid is 10\xB3 kg m\u207B\xB3 and contact angle of capillary surface with the liquid is zero]`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"A closed container with a movable piston holding air above a liquid; a thin capillary tube passes through an airtight hole in the piston into the liquid, and the liquid column of height h in the capillary is marked above the level of the liquid in the container.",d:[["r",60,95,200,75,"f"],["l",60,95,260,95,"B"],["l",144,40,144,160],["l",156,40,156,160],["l",144,40,156,40],["l",60,95,60,175],["l",260,95,260,175],["l",60,175,260,175],["t",80,70,"air","s"],["t",80,115,"air","s"],["t",100,155,"Liquid","s"],["t",190,60,"Piston","s"],["l",190,64,175,92,"a"],["l",170,95,170,125,"b"],["t",176,112,"h"]]},n:25,s:`Isothermal compression: P\u2080V\u2080 = P \xD7 {100;101}V\u2080, so the air pressure above the liquid is P = 1.01P\u2080 = 1.01 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
The top of the capillary is open to the atmosphere at P\u2080. Just below the concave meniscus the pressure is P\u2080 \u2212 2S/r, with 2S/r = 2 \xD7 0.075/10\u207B\u2074 = 1500 Pa.
At the level of the liquid in the container the pressure is P, so P\u2080 \u2212 2S/r + \u03C1gh = P.
\u03C1gh = 1500 + 0.01 \xD7 10\u2075 = 2500 Pa.
h = 2500/(10\xB3 \xD7 10) = 0.25 m = 25 cm.
Answer: 25.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`The pressure P\u2081 and density d\u2081 of diatomic gas (\u03B3 = {7;5}) changes suddenly to P\u2082 (> P\u2081) and d\u2082 respectively during an adiabatic process. The temperature of the gas increases and becomes ___ times of its initial temperature.
(given {d\u2082;d\u2081} = 32)`,n:4,s:`Adiabatic: P/d^\u03B3 is constant, so P\u2082/P\u2081 = (d\u2082/d\u2081)^\u03B3 = 32^(7/5) = 2\u2077 = 128.
Ideal gas: P = dRT/M, so T \u221D P/d.
T\u2082/T\u2081 = (P\u2082/P\u2081) \xD7 (d\u2081/d\u2082) = 128/32 = 4.
Answer: 4.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isobaric and isochoric processes",q:"A diatomic gas (\u03B3 = 1.4) does 400 J of work when it is expanded isobarically. The heat given to the gas in the process is ___ J.",n:1400,s:`Isobaric work W = P\u0394V = nR\u0394T = 400 J.
Heat Q = nC_p\u0394T with C_p = {\u03B3;\u03B3\u22121}R, so Q = {\u03B3;\u03B3\u22121} \xD7 nR\u0394T = {1.4;0.4} \xD7 400.
Q = 3.5 \xD7 400 = 1400 J.
Answer: 1400.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A sample of gas with \u03B3 = 1.5 is taken through an adiabatic process in which the volume is compressed from 1200 cm\xB3 to 300 cm\xB3. If the initial pressure is 200 kPa. The absolute value of the workdone by the gas in the process = ___ J.",n:480,s:`Adiabatic: P\u2082 = P\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^\u03B3 = 200 kPa \xD7 4^1.5 = 200 \xD7 8 = 1600 kPa.
P\u2081V\u2081 = 200 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 1200 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 240 J. P\u2082V\u2082 = 1600 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 300 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 480 J.
|W| = |P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081|/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = 240/0.5 = 480 J.
Answer: 480.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"In the reported figure, heat energy absorbed by a system in going through a cyclic process is ___ \u03C0 J.",fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"P\u2013V graph of a circular cycle: the circle spans volume from 20 to 40 litre and pressure from 20 to 40 kPa (centre at 30 litre, 30 kPa).",d:[["l",50,175,310,175,"a"],["l",50,175,50,15,"a"],["t",310,189,"V (litre)","e"],["t",54,23,"P (kPa)"],["c",170,50,40],["l",50,10,170,10,"d"],["l",50,90,170,90,"d"],["l",130,50,130,175,"d"],["l",210,50,210,175,"d"],["t",44,14,"40","es"],["t",44,94,"20","es"],["t",130,190,"20","ms"],["t",210,190,"40","ms"]]},n:100,s:`Over a complete cycle \u0394U = 0, so the heat absorbed equals the work done, which is the area of the loop.
The loop is a circle. Radius along P: (40 \u2212 20)/2 = 10 kPa = 10\u2074 Pa. Radius along V: (40 \u2212 20)/2 = 10 litre = 10\u207B\xB2 m\xB3.
Area = \u03C0 \xD7 10\u2074 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 = 100\u03C0 J.
Answer: 100.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:`One mole of an ideal gas at 27\xB0C is taken from A to B as shown in the given PV indicator diagram. The work done by the system will be ___ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 J.
[Given: R = 8.3 J/mole K, ln 2 = 0.6931] (Round off to nearest integer)`,fig:{w:340,h:205,alt:"P\u2013V indicator diagram: a falling curve from A (V = 2 m\xB3, P = 200 N/m\xB2) to B (V = 4 m\xB3, P = 100 N/m\xB2).",d:[["l",50,175,300,175,"a"],["l",50,175,50,25,"a"],["t",300,189,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["t",54,33,"P (N/m\xB2)"],["l",50,50,130,50,"d"],["l",50,110,210,110,"d"],["l",130,50,130,175,"d"],["l",210,110,210,175,"d"],["p","M130 50 Q160 85 210 110"],["t",44,54,"200","es"],["t",44,114,"100","es"],["t",130,190,"2","ms"],["t",210,190,"4","ms"],["t",124,44,"A(P\u2081,V\u2081)","e"],["t",216,108,"B(P\u2082,V\u2082)"]]},n:17258,s:`At A: P\u2081V\u2081 = 200 \xD7 2 = 400. At B: P\u2082V\u2082 = 100 \xD7 4 = 400. They are equal, so A \u2192 B is an isothermal at T = 300 K.
W = nRT ln(V\u2082/V\u2081) = 1 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300 \xD7 ln 2.
W = 2490 \xD7 0.6931 = 1725.8 J \u2248 17258 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 J.
Answer: 17258.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Work done by a gas",q:"In a certain thermodynamical process, the pressure of a gas depends on its volume as kV\xB3. The work done when the temperature changes from 100\xB0C to 300\xB0C will be ___ nR, where n denotes number of moles of a gas.",n:50,s:`P = kV\xB3 and PV = nRT give kV\u2074 = nRT.
Differentiate: 4kV\xB3 dV = nR dT, so P dV = kV\xB3 dV = {nR;4} dT.
W = \u222BP dV = {nR;4} \xD7 \u0394T = {nR;4} \xD7 200 = 50nR.
(\u0394T is the same in \xB0C and K.)
Answer: 50.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"A closed vessel contains 0.1 mole of a monoatomic ideal gas at 200 K. If 0.05 mole of the same gas at 400 K is added to it, the final equilibrium temperature (in K) of the gas in the vessel will be close to ___.",n:267,s:`The vessel is closed and no heat is exchanged, so the total internal energy is conserved: n\u2081T\u2081 + n\u2082T\u2082 = (n\u2081 + n\u2082)T.
0.1 \xD7 200 + 0.05 \xD7 400 = 0.15 T.
20 + 20 = 0.15 T, so T = 40/0.15 \u2248 266.7 K.
Answer: 267.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"The change in the magnitude of the volume of an ideal gas when a small additional pressure \u0394P is applied at a constant temperature, is the same as the change when the temperature is reduced by a small quantity \u0394T at constant pressure. The initial temperature and pressure of the gas were 300 K and 2 atm. respectively. If |\u0394T| = C|\u0394P| then value of C in (K/atm) is ___.",n:150,s:`Constant T: PV = constant, so |\u0394V| = V|\u0394P|/P.
Constant P: V/T = constant, so |\u0394V| = V|\u0394T|/T.
Equal changes: |\u0394P|/P = |\u0394T|/T, so |\u0394T| = (T/P)|\u0394P|.
C = T/P = 300/2 = 150 K/atm.
Answer: 150.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:"Initially a gas of diatomic molecules is contained in a cylinder of volume V\u2081 at a pressure P\u2081 and temperature 250 K. Assuming that 25% of the molecules get dissociated causing a change in number of moles. The pressure of the resulting gas at temperature 2000 K, when contained in a volume 2V\u2081 is given by P\u2082. The ratio P\u2082/P\u2081 is ___.",n:5,s:`Let the initial number of moles be n. Each dissociated diatomic molecule gives two atoms, so 25% dissociation gives n \xD7 (0.75 + 2 \xD7 0.25) = 1.25n moles.
Initially: P\u2081V\u2081 = nR \xD7 250.
Finally: P\u2082 \xD7 2V\u2081 = 1.25n \xD7 R \xD7 2000.
Divide: {P\u2082 \xD7 2;P\u2081} = {1.25 \xD7 2000;250} = 10, so P\u2082/P\u2081 = 5.
Answer: 5.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A soft plastic bottle, filled with water of density 1 gm cc\u207B\xB9, carries an inverted glass test-tube with some air (ideal gas) trapped as shown in the figure. The test-tube has a mass of 5 gm, and it is made of a thick glass of density 2.5 gm cc\u207B\xB9. Initially the bottle is sealed at atmospheric pressure p\u2080 = 10\u2075 Pa so that the volume of the trapped air is v\u2080 = 3.3 cc. When the bottle is squeezed from outside at constant temperature, the pressure inside rises and the volume of the trapped air reduces. It is found that the test tube begins to sink at pressure p\u2080 + \u0394p without changing its orientation. At this pressure, the volume of the trapped air is v\u2080 \u2212 \u0394v.
Let \u0394v = X cc and \u0394p = Y \xD7 10\xB3 Pa.
(i) The value of X is ___.
(ii) The value of Y is ___.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A soft plastic bottle full of water with a sealed top; inside the neck region an inverted glass test-tube holds trapped air.",d:[["p","M100 180 V70 Q100 58 118 52 L132 46 V20 H188 V46 L202 52 Q220 58 220 70 V180 Z"],["l",132,20,188,20,"B"],["p","M148 50 V100 M172 50 V100 M148 50 H172"],["t",230,78,"inverted test-tube","s"],["t",230,90,"with trapped air","s"],["t",160,150,"water","ms"]]},a:"(i) 0.3   (ii) 10",s:`Glass volume = mass/density = 5/2.5 = 2 cc.
The tube is about to sink when the buoyant force equals its weight: water displaced by (glass + trapped air) has mass 5 g, so 1 \xD7 (2 + v) = 5 and v = 3 cc.
(i) \u0394v = 3.3 \u2212 3 = 0.3 cc, so X = 0.3.
(ii) Isothermal: p\u2080v\u2080 = (p\u2080 + \u0394p) \xD7 3. So p\u2080 + \u0394p = 10\u2075 \xD7 3.3/3 = 1.1 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
\u0394p = 0.1 \xD7 10\u2075 = 10 \xD7 10\xB3 Pa, so Y = 10.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:"A spherical bubble inside water has radius R. Take the pressure inside the bubble and the water pressure to be p\u2080. The bubble now gets compressed radially in an adiabatic manner so that its radius becomes (R \u2212 a). For a << R the magnitude of the work done in the process is given by (4\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2)X, where X is a constant and \u03B3 = {C_p;C_v} = {41;30}. The value of X is ___.",n:2.05,s:`Let x = a/R. The gas is compressed adiabatically from volume V = {4;3}\u03C0R\xB3 to V(1 \u2212 x)\xB3, so its pressure becomes p\u2080(1 \u2212 x)^(\u22123\u03B3).
Work done on the gas = \u0394U = (p\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 p\u2080V)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = p\u2080V[(1 \u2212 x)^(\u2212k) \u2212 1]/(\u03B3 \u2212 1), where k = 3(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = 1.1.
Expand to second order: (1 \u2212 x)^(\u2212k) \u2212 1 \u2248 kx + {k(k+1);2}x\xB2. This gives \u0394U \u2248 4\u03C0p\u2080R\xB2a + 4.2\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2.
The water at constant pressure p\u2080 supplies p\u2080 \xD7 (volume decrease) = p\u2080 \xD7 (4\u03C0R\xB2a \u2212 4\u03C0Ra\xB2) of this. The remaining work done in the process (beyond that of the constant pressure p\u2080) is
[4\u03C0p\u2080R\xB2a + 4.2\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2] \u2212 [4\u03C0p\u2080R\xB2a \u2212 4\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2] = 8.2\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2 = (4\u03C0p\u2080Ra\xB2) \xD7 2.05.
Answer: X = 2.05.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"A thermodynamic system is taken from an initial state i with internal energy U(i) = 100 J to the final state f along two different paths iaf and ibf, as schematically shown in the figure. The work done by the system along the paths af, ib and bf are W(af) = 200 J, W(ib) = 50 J and W(bf) = 100 J respectively. The heat supplied to the system along the path iaf, ib and bf are Q(iaf), Q(ib) and Q(bf) respectively. If the internal energy of the system in the state b is U(b) = 200 J and Q(iaf) = 500 J, the ratio Q(bf)/Q(ib) is ______.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram with two paths from i to f: i up to a then right to f, and i right to b then curving up to f",d:[["l",40,170,40,20,"a"],["l",40,170,320,170,"a"],["t",34,26,"P","e"],["t",320,186,"V","e"],["l",80,140,80,50],["l",80,50,280,50],["p","M80 140H190C250 140 272 110 280 50"],["t",72,152,"i","e"],["t",72,46,"a","e"],["t",286,46,"f"],["t",190,158,"b","m"]]},n:2,s:`Path ia is at constant volume, so no work is done on it. Hence the work along iaf is W = W(af) = 200 J.
First law on iaf: Q(iaf) = \u0394U(if) + W, so 500 = \u0394U(if) + 200 and \u0394U(if) = 300 J. So U(f) = 100 + 300 = 400 J.
Along ib: \u0394U(ib) = 200 \u2212 100 = 100 J, so Q(ib) = 100 + 50 = 150 J.
Along bf: \u0394U(bf) = 400 \u2212 200 = 200 J, so Q(bf) = 200 + 100 = 300 J.
Ratio Q(bf)/Q(ib) = 300/150 = 2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match the List-I with List-II.
List-I: A. Isobaric   B. Isochoric   C. Adiabatic   D. Isothermal
List-II: (I) \u0394Q = \u0394W   (II) \u0394Q = \u0394U   (III) \u0394Q = zero   (IV) \u0394Q = \u0394U + P\u0394V
\u0394Q = Heat supplied, \u0394W = Work done by the system, \u0394U = Change in internal energy, P = Pressure of the system, \u0394V = Change in volume of the system
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-(IV), B-(I), C-(III), D-(II)","A-(IV), B-(II), C-(III), D-(I)","A-(II), B-(IV), C-(III), D-(I)","A-(IV), B-(III), C-(II), D-(I)"],c:1,s:`First law: \u0394Q = \u0394U + \u0394W.
Isobaric: work is P\u0394V, so \u0394Q = \u0394U + P\u0394V, which is (IV).
Isochoric: \u0394V = 0, so no work and \u0394Q = \u0394U, which is (II).
Adiabatic: no heat flows, \u0394Q = 0, which is (III).
Isothermal: \u0394U = 0 for an ideal gas, so \u0394Q = \u0394W, which is (I).
So A-(IV), B-(II), C-(III), D-(I).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List - I with List - II.
List-I: (A) Isothermal   (B) Adiabatic   (C) Isobaric   (D) Isochoric
List-II: (I) \u0394W (work done) = 0   (II) \u0394Q (supplied heat) = 0   (III) \u0394U (change in internal energy) \u2260 0   (IV) \u0394U = 0
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"],c:2,s:`Isothermal: temperature is constant, so for an ideal gas \u0394U = 0, which is (IV).
Adiabatic: no heat exchange, \u0394Q = 0, which is (II).
Isobaric: temperature changes, so \u0394U \u2260 0, which is (III).
Isochoric: volume is constant, so work done is zero, which is (I).
So (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match the List-I with List-II.
List-I: A. Pressure varies inversely with volume of an ideal gas   B. Heat absorbed goes partly to increase internal energy and partly to do work.   C. Heat is neither absorbed nor released by a system   D. No work is done on or by a gas.
List-II: I. Adiabatic process   II. Isochoric process   III. Isothermal process   IV. Isobaric process
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],c:2,s:`A: P \u221D 1/V means PV is constant, which is an isothermal process (III).
B: heat goes partly into internal energy and partly into work, as in expansion at constant pressure: isobaric (IV).
C: no heat exchange is the adiabatic process (I).
D: no work means \u0394V = 0, the isochoric process (II).
So A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`One mole of monoatomic ideal gas undergoes the cyclic process J\u2192K\u2192L\u2192M\u2192J, as shown in the P\u2013T diagram. Match the quantities mentioned in List-I with their values in List-II and choose the correct option. [R is the gas constant.]
List-I: (P) Work done in the complete cyclic process   (Q) Change in the internal energy of the gas in the process JK   (R) Heat given to the gas in the process KL   (S) Change in the internal energy of the gas in the process MJ
List-II: 1. RT\u2080 \u2212 4RT\u2080 ln2   2. 0   3. 3RT\u2080   4. \u22122RT\u2080 ln2   5. \u22123RT\u2080 ln2`,fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"P-T diagram of the cycle J to K to L to M to J, a rectangle with J at (T0,P0), K at (3T0,P0), L at (3T0,2P0), M at (T0,2P0)",d:[["l",50,180,50,20,"a"],["l",50,180,320,180,"a"],["t",44,26,"P","e"],["t",320,196,"T","e"],["l",50,150,100,150,"d"],["l",50,60,100,60,"d"],["l",100,150,100,180,"d"],["l",270,150,270,180,"d"],["l",100,150,270,150,"a"],["l",270,150,270,60,"a"],["l",270,60,100,60,"a"],["l",100,60,100,150,"a"],["t",44,154,"P\u2080","e"],["t",44,64,"2P\u2080","e"],["t",100,196,"T\u2080","m"],["t",270,196,"3T\u2080","m"],["t",94,166,"J","e"],["t",276,166,"K"],["t",276,54,"L"],["t",94,54,"M","e"]]},o:["P \u2192 1; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 5; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 4; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 5; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 4; Q \u2192 1; R \u2192 2; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 2; Q \u2192 5; R \u2192 3; S \u2192 4"],c:1,s:`From the diagram: JK is at constant P0 (T\u2080 to 3T\u2080), KL is at constant T = 3T\u2080 (P\u2080 to 2P\u2080), LM is at constant 2P\u2080 (3T\u2080 to T\u2080), MJ is at constant T\u2080 (2P\u2080 to P\u2080).
Q: \u0394U(JK) = (3/2)R(3T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080) = 3RT\u2080, so Q \u2192 3.
R: KL is isothermal compression with volume halved, so heat = work = RT\xB7ln(V_L/V_K) = 3RT\u2080 ln(1/2) = \u22123RT\u2080 ln2, so R \u2192 5.
S: MJ is isothermal, \u0394U = 0, so S \u2192 2.
P: JK work = R(2T\u2080) = 2RT\u2080; KL work = \u22123RT\u2080 ln2; LM work = R(T\u2080 \u2212 3T\u2080) = \u22122RT\u2080; MJ work = RT\u2080 ln2. Sum = \u22122RT\u2080 ln2, so P \u2192 4.
So P\u21924, Q\u21923, R\u21925, S\u21922.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I: A. Isothermal process   B. Adiabatic process   C. Isochoric process   D. Isobaric process
List II: I. Work done by the gas decreases internal energy   II. No change in internal energy   III. The heat absorbed goes partly to increase internal energy and partly to do work   IV. No work is done on or by the gas
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],c:1,s:`Isothermal: T is constant, so there is no change in internal energy (II).
Adiabatic: no heat flows, so the work done by the gas comes out of its internal energy, which therefore decreases (I).
Isochoric: volume is constant, so no work is done (IV).
Isobaric: heat goes partly into internal energy and partly into work (III).
So A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (A) Isothermal   (B) Isochoric   (C) Adiabatic   (D) Isobaric
List-II: (i) Pressure constant   (ii) Temperature constant   (iii) Volume constant   (iv) Heat content is constant
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(i), (B)-(iii), (C)-(ii), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(iii), (D)-(i)"],c:2,s:`Isothermal means constant temperature: (ii).
Isochoric means constant volume: (iii).
Adiabatic means no heat enters or leaves, so the heat content stays constant: (iv).
Isobaric means constant pressure: (i).
So (A)-(ii), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Match the thermodynamic processes taking place in a system with the correct conditions. In the table: \u0394Q is the heat supplied, \u0394W is the work done and \u0394U is change in internal energy of the system.
Process: (I) Adiabatic   (II) Isothermal   (III) Isochoric   (IV) Isobaric
Condition: (A) \u0394W = 0   (B) \u0394Q = 0   (C) \u0394U \u2260 0, \u0394W \u2260 0, \u0394Q \u2260 0   (D) \u0394U = 0`,o:["(I)-(B), (II)-(D), (III)-(A), (IV)-(C)","(I)-(A), (II)-(A), (III)-(B), (IV)-(C)","(I)-(A), (II)-(B), (III)-(D), (IV)-(D)","(I)-(B), (II)-(A), (III)-(D), (IV)-(C)"],c:0,s:`Adiabatic: no heat exchange, \u0394Q = 0, so (I)-(B).
Isothermal: \u0394U = 0 for an ideal gas, so (II)-(D).
Isochoric: volume fixed, \u0394W = 0, so (III)-(A).
Isobaric: temperature changes and work is done, so \u0394U, \u0394W and \u0394Q are all non-zero: (IV)-(C).
This is option (a).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas undergoes four thermodynamic processes as shown schematically in the PV-diagram. Among these four processes, one is isobaric, one is isochoric, one is isothermal and one is adiabatic. Match the processes mentioned in List-I with the corresponding statements in List-II.
List-I: P. In process I   Q. In process II   R. In process III   S. In process IV
List-II: 1. Work done by the gas is zero   2. Temperature of the gas remains unchanged   3. No heat is exchanged between the gas and its surroundings   4. Work done by the gas is 6P\u2080V\u2080`,fig:{w:340,h:215,alt:"P-V diagram with four processes from the state at V0,3P0: I a steep falling curve, II horizontal line at 3P0 to 3V0, III vertical line down at 3V0, IV a shallower falling curve",d:[["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,190,320,190,"a"],["t",44,26,"P","e"],["t",320,206,"V","e"],["l",50,150,200,150,"d"],["l",50,70,100,70,"d"],["l",100,70,100,190,"d"],["l",200,70,200,190,"d"],["l",100,70,200,70,"a"],["l",200,70,200,150,"a"],["p","M100 70L112 94L125 110L150 130L175 142L200 150","a"],["p","M100 70L112 107L125 129L150 152L175 164L200 171","a"],["t",44,154,"P\u2080","e"],["t",44,74,"3P\u2080","e"],["t",100,206,"V\u2080","m"],["t",200,206,"3V\u2080","m"],["t",150,62,"II","m"],["t",208,112,"III"],["t",158,124,"IV"],["t",134,172,"I"]]},o:["P \u2192 4; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 1; Q \u2192 3; R \u2192 2; S \u2192 4","P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 4; R \u2192 1; S \u2192 2","P \u2192 3; Q \u2192 4; R \u2192 2; S \u2192 1"],c:2,s:`Process II is the horizontal line at 3P\u2080 from V\u2080 to 3V\u2080: isobaric. Work = 3P\u2080 \xD7 (3V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080) = 6P\u2080V\u2080, so Q \u2192 4.
Process III is the vertical line (V constant): isochoric, no work, so R \u2192 1.
Processes I and IV are the two curves. The isothermal one is the less steep (IV), along which the temperature does not change, so S \u2192 2.
The steeper curve I is the adiabatic one, where no heat is exchanged, so P \u2192 3.
So P\u21923, Q\u21924, R\u21921, S\u21922.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Answer Q. 148, Q. 149 and Q. 150 by appropriately matching the information given in the three columns of the following table.
An ideal gas is undergoing a cyclic thermodynamic process in different ways as shown in the corresponding P\u2013V diagrams in column 3 of the table. Consider only the path from state 1 to state 2. W denotes the corresponding work done on the system. The equations and plots in the table have standard notations as used in thermodynamic processes. Here \u03B3 is the ratio of heat capacities at constant pressure and constant volume. The number of moles in the gas is n.
Column 1: (I) W(1\u21922) = {1;\u03B3\u22121}(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081)   (II) W(1\u21922) = \u2212PV\u2082 + PV\u2081   (III) W(1\u21922) = 0   (IV) W(1\u21922) = \u2212nRT ln{V\u2082;V\u2081}
Column 2: (i) Isothermal   (ii) Isochoric   (iii) Isobaric   (iv) Adiabatic
Column 3: (P), (Q), (R), (S) are the four P\u2013V diagrams in the figure (the path 1\u21922 is the bold one).
Which one of the following options correctly represents a thermodynamic process that is used as a correction in the determination of the speed of sound in an ideal gas?`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Four small P-V cycle diagrams P, Q, R, S, each with the path from state 1 to state 2 in bold: P is a horizontal line, Q a steep falling curve, R a gently falling curve, S a vertical line downward",d:[["l",15,88,15,8,"a"],["l",15,88,155,88,"a"],["t",10,16,"P","e"],["t",156,98,"V","e"],["t",90,12,"(P)","m"],["p","M35 30 L95 30","Ba"],["p","M95 30 Q110 52 122 60"],["p","M122 60 Q80 66 35 62"],["p","M35 62 L35 30"],["t",28,26,"1","s"],["t",98,26,"2","s"],["l",185,88,185,8,"a"],["l",185,88,325,88,"a"],["t",180,16,"P","e"],["t",326,98,"V","e"],["t",260,12,"(Q)","m"],["p","M280 35 Q286 55 295 64","Ba"],["p","M295 64 Q255 62 220 55"],["p","M220 55 L220 32"],["p","M220 32 L280 35"],["t",282,30,"1","s"],["t",298,74,"2","s"],["l",15,188,15,108,"a"],["l",15,188,155,188,"a"],["t",10,116,"P","e"],["t",156,198,"V","e"],["t",90,112,"(R)","m"],["p","M45 132 Q70 144 105 148","Ba"],["p","M105 148 Q118 156 125 164"],["p","M125 164 Q85 162 50 156"],["p","M50 156 L45 132"],["t",36,130,"1","s"],["t",108,144,"2","s"],["l",185,188,185,108,"a"],["l",185,188,325,188,"a"],["t",180,116,"P","e"],["t",326,198,"V","e"],["t",260,112,"(S)","m"],["p","M210 128 L210 160","Ba"],["p","M210 160 L280 160"],["p","M280 160 L280 128"],["p","M280 128 L210 128"],["t",200,130,"1","s"],["t",200,164,"2","s"]]},o:["(IV) (ii) (R)","(I) (ii) (Q)","(I) (iv) (Q)","(III) (iv) (R)"],c:2,s:`Newton assumed the compressions in a sound wave are isothermal. Laplace corrected this: they are so quick that no heat flows, so the process is adiabatic (iv).
Work done on the system in an adiabatic process is (P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1), which is item (I).
The adiabatic path 1\u21922 is the steep falling curve, graph (Q).
So the combination is (I) (iv) (Q).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Answer Q. 148, Q. 149 and Q. 150 by appropriately matching the information given in the three columns of the following table.
An ideal gas is undergoing a cyclic thermodynamic process in different ways as shown in the corresponding P\u2013V diagrams in column 3 of the table. Consider only the path from state 1 to state 2. W denotes the corresponding work done on the system. The equations and plots in the table have standard notations as used in thermodynamic processes. Here \u03B3 is the ratio of heat capacities at constant pressure and constant volume. The number of moles in the gas is n.
Column 1: (I) W(1\u21922) = {1;\u03B3\u22121}(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081)   (II) W(1\u21922) = \u2212PV\u2082 + PV\u2081   (III) W(1\u21922) = 0   (IV) W(1\u21922) = \u2212nRT ln{V\u2082;V\u2081}
Column 2: (i) Isothermal   (ii) Isochoric   (iii) Isobaric   (iv) Adiabatic
Column 3: (P), (Q), (R), (S) are the four P\u2013V diagrams in the figure (the path 1\u21922 is the bold one).
Which one of the following options is the only correct representation of a process in which \u0394U = \u0394Q \u2212 P\u0394V?`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Four small P-V cycle diagrams P, Q, R, S, each with the path from state 1 to state 2 in bold: P is a horizontal line, Q a steep falling curve, R a gently falling curve, S a vertical line downward",d:[["l",15,88,15,8,"a"],["l",15,88,155,88,"a"],["t",10,16,"P","e"],["t",156,98,"V","e"],["t",90,12,"(P)","m"],["p","M35 30 L95 30","Ba"],["p","M95 30 Q110 52 122 60"],["p","M122 60 Q80 66 35 62"],["p","M35 62 L35 30"],["t",28,26,"1","s"],["t",98,26,"2","s"],["l",185,88,185,8,"a"],["l",185,88,325,88,"a"],["t",180,16,"P","e"],["t",326,98,"V","e"],["t",260,12,"(Q)","m"],["p","M280 35 Q286 55 295 64","Ba"],["p","M295 64 Q255 62 220 55"],["p","M220 55 L220 32"],["p","M220 32 L280 35"],["t",282,30,"1","s"],["t",298,74,"2","s"],["l",15,188,15,108,"a"],["l",15,188,155,188,"a"],["t",10,116,"P","e"],["t",156,198,"V","e"],["t",90,112,"(R)","m"],["p","M45 132 Q70 144 105 148","Ba"],["p","M105 148 Q118 156 125 164"],["p","M125 164 Q85 162 50 156"],["p","M50 156 L45 132"],["t",36,130,"1","s"],["t",108,144,"2","s"],["l",185,188,185,108,"a"],["l",185,188,325,188,"a"],["t",180,116,"P","e"],["t",326,198,"V","e"],["t",260,112,"(S)","m"],["p","M210 128 L210 160","Ba"],["p","M210 160 L280 160"],["p","M280 160 L280 128"],["p","M280 128 L210 128"],["t",200,130,"1","s"],["t",200,164,"2","s"]]},o:["(II) (iii) (S)","(II) (iii) (P)","(III) (iii) (P)","(II) (iv) (R)"],c:1,s:`\u0394U = \u0394Q \u2212 P\u0394V is the first law with the work done by the gas written as P\u0394V, with P constant. So the process is isobaric (iii).
Work done on the system is \u2212P\u0394V = \u2212P(V\u2082 \u2212 V\u2081) = \u2212PV\u2082 + PV\u2081, which is item (II).
An isobaric path is a horizontal line on the P\u2013V diagram, graph (P).
So the combination is (II) (iii) (P).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Answer Q. 148, Q. 149 and Q. 150 by appropriately matching the information given in the three columns of the following table.
An ideal gas is undergoing a cyclic thermodynamic process in different ways as shown in the corresponding P\u2013V diagrams in column 3 of the table. Consider only the path from state 1 to state 2. W denotes the corresponding work done on the system. The equations and plots in the table have standard notations as used in thermodynamic processes. Here \u03B3 is the ratio of heat capacities at constant pressure and constant volume. The number of moles in the gas is n.
Column 1: (I) W(1\u21922) = {1;\u03B3\u22121}(P\u2082V\u2082 \u2212 P\u2081V\u2081)   (II) W(1\u21922) = \u2212PV\u2082 + PV\u2081   (III) W(1\u21922) = 0   (IV) W(1\u21922) = \u2212nRT ln{V\u2082;V\u2081}
Column 2: (i) Isothermal   (ii) Isochoric   (iii) Isobaric   (iv) Adiabatic
Column 3: (P), (Q), (R), (S) are the four P\u2013V diagrams in the figure (the path 1\u21922 is the bold one).
Which one of the following options is the correct combination?`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Four small P-V cycle diagrams P, Q, R, S, each with the path from state 1 to state 2 in bold: P is a horizontal line, Q a steep falling curve, R a gently falling curve, S a vertical line downward",d:[["l",15,88,15,8,"a"],["l",15,88,155,88,"a"],["t",10,16,"P","e"],["t",156,98,"V","e"],["t",90,12,"(P)","m"],["p","M35 30 L95 30","Ba"],["p","M95 30 Q110 52 122 60"],["p","M122 60 Q80 66 35 62"],["p","M35 62 L35 30"],["t",28,26,"1","s"],["t",98,26,"2","s"],["l",185,88,185,8,"a"],["l",185,88,325,88,"a"],["t",180,16,"P","e"],["t",326,98,"V","e"],["t",260,12,"(Q)","m"],["p","M280 35 Q286 55 295 64","Ba"],["p","M295 64 Q255 62 220 55"],["p","M220 55 L220 32"],["p","M220 32 L280 35"],["t",282,30,"1","s"],["t",298,74,"2","s"],["l",15,188,15,108,"a"],["l",15,188,155,188,"a"],["t",10,116,"P","e"],["t",156,198,"V","e"],["t",90,112,"(R)","m"],["p","M45 132 Q70 144 105 148","Ba"],["p","M105 148 Q118 156 125 164"],["p","M125 164 Q85 162 50 156"],["p","M50 156 L45 132"],["t",36,130,"1","s"],["t",108,144,"2","s"],["l",185,188,185,108,"a"],["l",185,188,325,188,"a"],["t",180,116,"P","e"],["t",326,198,"V","e"],["t",260,112,"(S)","m"],["p","M210 128 L210 160","Ba"],["p","M210 160 L280 160"],["p","M280 160 L280 128"],["p","M280 128 L210 128"],["t",200,130,"1","s"],["t",200,164,"2","s"]]},o:["(II) (iv) (P)","(III) (ii) (S)","(II) (iv) (R)","(IV) (ii) (S)"],c:1,s:`An isochoric process (ii) has constant volume, so no work is done: W = 0, which is item (III).
On the P\u2013V diagram it is a vertical line, graph (S).
The other options fail: (II) is the isobaric work formula, not adiabatic; (IV) is the isothermal work formula, not isochoric.
So the correct combination is (III) (ii) (S).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas is taken through a cycle ABCDA as shown in the P\u2013V diagram. Column II gives the characteristics involved in the cycle. Match them with each of the processes given in column I.
Column I: (A) Process A\u2192B   (B) Process B\u2192C   (C) Process C\u2192D   (D) Process D\u2192A
Column II: (p) Internal energy decreases.   (q) Internal energy increases.   (r) Heat is lost.   (s) Heat is gained.   (t) Work is done on the gas.`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram of cycle A to B to C to D to A: A at (3V,3P), B at (V,3P), C at (V,P), D at (9V,P) with curve D to A",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,300,170,"a"],["t",44,26,"P","e"],["t",300,186,"V","e"],["l",50,50,110,50,"d"],["l",50,130,230,130,"d"],["l",70,130,70,170,"d"],["l",110,50,110,170,"d"],["l",230,130,230,170,"d"],["l",110,50,70,50,"a"],["l",70,50,70,130,"a"],["l",70,130,230,130,"a"],["p","M230 130L190 110L150 90L122 70L110 50","a"],["t",44,54,"3P","e"],["t",44,134,"1P","e"],["t",70,186,"1V","m"],["t",110,186,"3V","m"],["t",230,186,"9V","m"],["t",116,44,"A"],["t",64,44,"B","e"],["t",64,146,"C","e"],["t",236,146,"D"]]},a:"A\u2192(p,r,t); B\u2192(p,r); C\u2192(q,s); D\u2192(r,t)",s:`From the diagram: A=(3V, 3P), B=(V, 3P), C=(V, P), D=(9V, P). At A and D the product PV is the same (3V \xD7 3P = 9V \xD7 P), so D\u2192A is isothermal.
A\u2192B: isobaric compression. T falls, so U decreases (p); heat is lost (r); work is done on the gas (t).
B\u2192C: isochoric, pressure falls, T falls: U decreases (p), heat is lost (r), no work.
C\u2192D: isobaric expansion, T rises: U increases (q), heat is gained (s).
D\u2192A: isothermal compression: \u0394U = 0, work is done on the gas (t), and that energy leaves as heat (r).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Column I contains a list of processes involving expansion of an ideal gas. Match this with Column II describing the thermodynamic change during this process.
Column I:
(a) An insulated container has two chambers separated by a valve. Chamber I contains an ideal gas and Chamber II has vacuum. The valve is opened.
(b) An ideal monoatomic gas expands to twice its original volume such that its pressure P \u221D 1/V\xB2, where V is the volume of the gas.
(c) An ideal monoatomic gas expands to twice its original volume such that its pressure P \u221D 1/V^(4/3), where V is its volume.
(d) An ideal monoatomic gas expands such that its pressure P and volume V follows the behaviour shown in the graph (P rises as V goes from V\u2081 to 2V\u2081).
Column II:
(p) The temperature of the gas decreases
(q) The temperature of the gas increases or remains constant
(r) The gas loses heat
(s) The gas gains heat`,fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"P-V graph where pressure rises with volume from V1 to 2V1 along a gently curved line",d:[["l",40,140,40,20,"a"],["l",40,140,240,140,"a"],["t",34,26,"P","e"],["t",240,156,"V","e"],["l",100,140,100,100,"d"],["l",180,140,180,45,"d"],["p","M100 100Q150 92 180 45"],["t",100,156,"V\u2081","m"],["t",180,156,"2V\u2081","m"]]},a:"(a)\u2192(q), (b)\u2192(p,r), (c)\u2192(p,s), (d)\u2192(q,s)",s:`(a) Free expansion into vacuum: Q = 0 and W = 0, so \u0394U = 0 and T stays constant: (q).
(b) PV\xB2 = constant, so T \u221D PV \u221D 1/V falls as V doubles: (p). Molar heat capacity C = 3R/2 + R/(1 \u2212 2) = R/2 > 0, \u0394T < 0, so Q = nC\u0394T < 0: heat is lost (r).
(c) P V^(4/3) = constant, so T \u221D V^(\u22121/3) falls: (p). Here C = 3R/2 + R/(1 \u2212 4/3) = \u22123R/2 < 0 and \u0394T < 0, so Q > 0: heat is gained (s).
(d) P and V both increase, so T \u221D PV increases (q). Work by the gas and \u0394U are both positive, so Q = \u0394U + W > 0: heat is gained (s).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Heat given to process is positive, match the following option of column I with the corresponding option of column II.
Column I: (i) JK   (ii) KL   (iii) LM   (iv) MJ
Column II: (p) \u0394W > 0   (q) \u0394Q < 0   (r) \u0394W < 0   (s) \u0394Q > 0`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram with P in atm and V in cubic metres: J at (10,30), K at (10,10), L at (20,10), M at (20,20); path J to K to L to M to J",d:[["l",30,170,30,20,"a"],["l",30,170,280,170,"a"],["t",70,14,"P (atm)","e"],["t",280,186,"V (m\xB3)","e"],["l",30,50,110,50,"d"],["l",30,90,190,90,"d"],["l",30,130,190,130,"d"],["l",110,130,110,170,"d"],["l",190,90,190,170,"d"],["l",110,50,110,130,"a"],["l",110,130,190,130,"a"],["l",190,130,190,90,"a"],["l",190,90,110,50,"a"],["t",24,54,"30","e"],["t",24,94,"20","e"],["t",24,134,"10","e"],["t",110,186,"10","m"],["t",190,186,"20","m"],["t",104,46,"J","e"],["t",104,146,"K","e"],["t",196,146,"L"],["t",196,86,"M"]]},a:"(i)\u2192(q), (ii)\u2192(p,s), (iii)\u2192(s), (iv)\u2192(q,r)",s:`Read the points: J=(10, 30), K=(10, 10), L=(20, 10), M=(20, 20) in (m\xB3, atm).
JK: volume constant, pressure falls, so T falls. W = 0, \u0394U < 0, so \u0394Q < 0: (q).
KL: isobaric expansion, so \u0394W > 0 (p). T doubles, \u0394U > 0, so \u0394Q > 0 (s).
LM: volume constant, pressure rises, T rises. W = 0 and \u0394U > 0, so \u0394Q > 0: (s).
MJ: volume decreases, so \u0394W < 0 (r). PV goes from 400 to 300, so T falls, \u0394U < 0 and \u0394Q = \u0394U + W < 0 (q).`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A thermally insulating cylinder has a thermally insulating and frictionless movable partition in the middle, as shown in the figure. On each side of the partition, there is one mole of an ideal gas, with specific heat at constant volume, C\u1D65 = 2R. Here, R is the gas constant. Initially, each side has a volume V\u2080 and temperature T\u2080. The left side has an electric heater, which is turned on at very low power to transfer heat Q to the gas on the left side. As a result the partition moves slowly towards the right reducing the right side volume to V\u2080/2. Consequently, the gas temperatures on the left and the right sides become T(L) and T(R), respectively. Ignore the changes in the temperatures of the cylinder, heater and the partition.
The value of T(R)/T\u2080 is`,fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Insulated cylinder split by a movable partition into two halves, each with gas at T0 and V0; heater on the left side",d:[["r",30,30,280,70],["l",170,30,170,100,"B"],["r",14,52,16,26,"f"],["t",22,46,"heater","ms"],["t",100,70,"T\u2080, V\u2080","m"],["t",240,70,"T\u2080, V\u2080","m"],["t",100,24,"left","ms"],["t",240,24,"right","ms"],["t",8,98,"Q","s"]]},o:["\u221A2","\u221A3","2","3"],c:0,s:`Right gas: no heat enters it (insulated) and it is compressed slowly, so it is adiabatic. \u03B3 = (C\u1D65 + R)/C\u1D65 = 3R/2R = 3/2.
TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant: T(R) (V\u2080/2)^(1/2) = T\u2080 V\u2080^(1/2), so T(R) = \u221A2 T\u2080.
So T(R)/T\u2080 = \u221A2.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"ptp",st:"Isothermal and adiabatic processes",q:`A thermally insulating cylinder has a thermally insulating and frictionless movable partition in the middle, as shown in the figure. On each side of the partition, there is one mole of an ideal gas, with specific heat at constant volume, C\u1D65 = 2R. Here, R is the gas constant. Initially, each side has a volume V\u2080 and temperature T\u2080. The left side has an electric heater, which is turned on at very low power to transfer heat Q to the gas on the left side. As a result the partition moves slowly towards the right reducing the right side volume to V\u2080/2. Consequently, the gas temperatures on the left and the right sides become T(L) and T(R), respectively. Ignore the changes in the temperatures of the cylinder, heater and the partition.
The value of Q/RT\u2080 is`,fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Insulated cylinder split by a movable partition into two halves, each with gas at T0 and V0; heater on the left side",d:[["r",30,30,280,70],["l",170,30,170,100,"B"],["r",14,52,16,26,"f"],["t",22,46,"heater","ms"],["t",100,70,"T\u2080, V\u2080","m"],["t",240,70,"T\u2080, V\u2080","m"],["t",100,24,"left","ms"],["t",240,24,"right","ms"],["t",8,98,"Q","s"]]},o:["4(2\u221A2 + 1)","4(2\u221A2 \u2212 1)","(5\u221A2 + 1)","(5\u221A2 \u2212 1)"],c:1,s:`Right gas: no heat enters it (insulated) and it is compressed slowly, so it is adiabatic. \u03B3 = (C\u1D65 + R)/C\u1D65 = 3R/2R = 3/2.
TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant: T(R) (V\u2080/2)^(1/2) = T\u2080 V\u2080^(1/2), so T(R) = \u221A2 T\u2080.
Right side finally has pressure P = R T(R)/(V\u2080/2) = 2\u221A2 RT\u2080/V\u2080. The left side has volume 2V\u2080 \u2212 V\u2080/2 = 3V\u2080/2 and the same pressure, so T(L) = P(3V\u2080/2)/R = 3\u221A2 T\u2080.
The whole cylinder is insulated and its total volume is fixed, so the heater's Q all goes into internal energy: Q = C\u1D65[(T(L) \u2212 T\u2080) + (T(R) \u2212 T\u2080)] = 2R[(3\u221A2 \u2212 1) + (\u221A2 \u2212 1)]T\u2080 = 2R(4\u221A2 \u2212 2)T\u2080.
Q/RT\u2080 = 4(2\u221A2 \u2212 1).`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`Two moles of helium gas are taken over the cycle ABCDA, as shown in the P\u2013T diagram.
Assuming the gas to be ideal the work done by the gas in taking it from A to B is`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-T diagram of cycle ABCDA: A at (300 K, 2\xD710\u2075 Pa), B at (500 K, 2\xD710\u2075), C at (500 K, 10\u2075), D at (300 K, 10\u2075)",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["t",44,26,"P (Pa)","e"],["t",320,186,"T","e"],["l",50,70,100,70,"d"],["l",50,120,100,120,"d"],["l",100,120,100,170,"d"],["l",250,120,250,170,"d"],["l",100,70,250,70,"a"],["l",250,70,250,120,"a"],["l",250,120,100,120,"a"],["l",100,120,100,70,"a"],["t",44,74,"2\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",44,124,"1\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",100,188,"300 K","m"],["t",250,188,"500 K","m"],["t",94,64,"A","e"],["t",256,64,"B"],["t",256,136,"C"],["t",94,136,"D","e"]]},o:["200R","300R","400R","500R"],c:2,s:`A\u2192B is at constant pressure 2 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa while T rises from 300 K to 500 K, so the gas expands.
For an isobaric process the work done by the gas is P\u0394V = nR\u0394T = 2 \xD7 R \xD7 (500 \u2212 300) = 400R.
(The work done on the gas would be \u2212400R; the options list the magnitude.)`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`Two moles of helium gas are taken over the cycle ABCDA, as shown in the P\u2013T diagram.
The work done on the gas in taking it from D to A is`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-T diagram of cycle ABCDA: A at (300 K, 2\xD710\u2075 Pa), B at (500 K, 2\xD710\u2075), C at (500 K, 10\u2075), D at (300 K, 10\u2075)",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["t",44,26,"P (Pa)","e"],["t",320,186,"T","e"],["l",50,70,100,70,"d"],["l",50,120,100,120,"d"],["l",100,120,100,170,"d"],["l",250,120,250,170,"d"],["l",100,70,250,70,"a"],["l",250,70,250,120,"a"],["l",250,120,100,120,"a"],["l",100,120,100,70,"a"],["t",44,74,"2\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",44,124,"1\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",100,188,"300 K","m"],["t",250,188,"500 K","m"],["t",94,64,"A","e"],["t",256,64,"B"],["t",256,136,"C"],["t",94,136,"D","e"]]},o:["\u2212414R","+414R","\u2212690R","+690R"],c:1,s:`D\u2192A is at constant T = 300 K, with pressure rising from 10\u2075 to 2 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa, so the gas is compressed isothermally to half its volume.
Work done on the gas = nRT ln(V_D/V_A) = nRT ln(P_A/P_D) = 2 \xD7 R \xD7 300 \xD7 ln2.
With ln2 = 0.69 this is 600R \xD7 0.69 = 414R.
The gas is compressed, so the work done on it is positive: +414R.`},{y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:`Two moles of helium gas are taken over the cycle ABCDA, as shown in the P\u2013T diagram.
The net work done by the gas in the cycle ABCDA is`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-T diagram of cycle ABCDA: A at (300 K, 2\xD710\u2075 Pa), B at (500 K, 2\xD710\u2075), C at (500 K, 10\u2075), D at (300 K, 10\u2075)",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["t",44,26,"P (Pa)","e"],["t",320,186,"T","e"],["l",50,70,100,70,"d"],["l",50,120,100,120,"d"],["l",100,120,100,170,"d"],["l",250,120,250,170,"d"],["l",100,70,250,70,"a"],["l",250,70,250,120,"a"],["l",250,120,100,120,"a"],["l",100,120,100,70,"a"],["t",44,74,"2\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",44,124,"1\xD710\u2075","e"],["t",100,188,"300 K","m"],["t",250,188,"500 K","m"],["t",94,64,"A","e"],["t",256,64,"B"],["t",256,136,"C"],["t",94,136,"D","e"]]},o:["zero","276R","1076R","1904R"],c:1,s:`Add the four steps (work by the gas):
A\u2192B isobaric expansion: nR\u0394T = 400R.
B\u2192C isothermal expansion at 500 K, volume doubles: 2R \xD7 500 \xD7 ln2 = 690R.
C\u2192D isobaric compression at 10\u2075 Pa: \u22122R \xD7 200 = \u2212400R.
D\u2192A isothermal compression at 300 K: \u22122R \xD7 300 \xD7 ln2 = \u2212414R.
Net work done by the gas = 400 + 690 \u2212 400 \u2212 414 = 276R (the work done on the gas is \u2212276R; the options list the magnitude).`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): With the increase in the pressure of an ideal gas, the volume falls off more rapidly in an isothermal process in comparison to the adiabatic process.
Reason (R): In isothermal process, PV = constant, while in adiabatic process PV^\u03B3 = constant. Here \u03B3 is the ratio of specific heats, P is the pressure and V is the volume of the ideal gas.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:1,s:`Isothermal: PV = k gives dV/dP = \u2212V/P.
Adiabatic: PV^\u03B3 = k gives dV/dP = \u2212V/(\u03B3P), which is smaller in size because \u03B3 > 1.
So for the same rise in pressure the volume falls more in the isothermal case. (A) is true.
(R) states the two laws correctly, and these laws are exactly what give the result in (A). So (R) explains (A).`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: If heat is added to a system, its temperature must increase.
Statement II: If positive work is done by a system in a thermodynamic process, its volume must increase.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:3,s:`Statement I is false: in an isothermal expansion heat is added but the temperature stays constant (also during a phase change).
Statement II is true: work by the gas is W = \u222BP dV, and with P > 0 the work is positive only if dV > 0, so the volume must increase.
So Statement I is false and Statement II is true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): If dQ and dW represent the heat supplied to the system and the work done on the system respectively. Then according to the first law of thermodynamics dQ = dU \u2212 dW.
Reason (R): First law of thermodynamics is based on law of conservation of energy.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct.","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct.","Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)."],c:3,s:`Energy supplied to the system as heat dQ plus work dW done on it goes into its internal energy: dU = dQ + dW.
So dQ = dU \u2212 dW, and (A) is correct.
This statement is just conservation of energy applied to the system, so (R) is correct and it explains (A).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: When \u03BC amounts of an ideal gas undergoes adiabatic change from state (P\u2081, V\u2081, T\u2081) to state (P\u2082, V\u2082, T\u2082), then work done is W = {\u03BCR(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081);1 \u2212 \u03B3}, where \u03B3 = {C\u209A;C\u1D65} and R = universal gas constant.
Statement-II: In the above case, when work is done on the gas, the temperature of the gas shall rise.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both statement-I and statement-II are true.","Both statement-I and statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but statement-II is true."],c:0,s:`Adiabatic work done by the gas: W = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = \u03BCR(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = \u03BCR(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)/(1 \u2212 \u03B3). So Statement-I is true.
In an adiabatic process Q = 0, so \u0394U = \u2212W where W is the work done by the gas. When work is done on the gas, W is negative, so \u0394U > 0 and the temperature rises. Statement-II is true.
Both are true.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"ptf",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`A cylinder of mass 1 kg is given heat of 20,000 J at atmospheric pressure. If initially the temperature of cylinder is 20\xB0C, find
(a) final temperature of the cylinder,
(b) work done by the cylinder,
(c) change in internal energy of the cylinder.
(Given that specific heat of cylinder = 400 J kg\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, coefficient of volume expansion = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xB0C\u207B\xB9, atmospheric pressure = 10\u2075 N/m\xB2 and density of cylinder = 9000 kg m\u207B\xB3).`,a:"(a) 70 \xB0C   (b) 0.05 J   (c) 19999.95 J",s:`(a) Q = mc\u0394T, so \u0394T = 20000/(1 \xD7 400) = 50 \xB0C. Final temperature = 20 + 50 = 70 \xB0C.
(b) Initial volume V = m/\u03C1 = 1/9000 m\xB3. Expansion \u0394V = V\u03B3 \u0394T = (1/9000)(9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)(50) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 m\xB3.
Work done against the atmosphere W = P\u0394V = 10\u2075 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 0.05 J.
(c) First law: \u0394U = Q \u2212 W = 20000 \u2212 0.05 = 19999.95 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two moles of helium gas undergo a cyclic process as shown in figure. Assuming the gas to be ideal, calculate the following quantities in this process:
(a) The net change in the heat energy
(b) The net work done
(c) The net change in internal energy`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-T diagram of cycle ABCDA: A at (300 K, 2 atm), B at (400 K, 2 atm), C at (400 K, 1 atm), D at (300 K, 1 atm)",d:[["l",50,170,50,20,"a"],["l",50,170,320,170,"a"],["t",44,26,"P","e"],["t",320,186,"T","e"],["l",50,70,100,70,"d"],["l",50,120,100,120,"d"],["l",100,120,100,170,"d"],["l",250,120,250,170,"d"],["l",100,70,250,70,"a"],["l",250,70,250,120,"a"],["l",250,120,100,120,"a"],["l",100,120,100,70,"a"],["t",44,74,"2 atm","e"],["t",44,124,"1 atm","e"],["t",100,188,"300 K","m"],["t",250,188,"400 K","m"],["t",94,64,"A","e"],["t",256,64,"B"],["t",256,136,"C"],["t",94,136,"D","e"]]},a:"(a) 1152 J   (b) 1152 J   (c) zero",s:`After a full cycle the gas is back in its initial state, so (c) the net change in internal energy is zero.
The straight steps A\u2192B and C\u2192D are at constant pressure; B\u2192C (400 K) and D\u2192A (300 K) are isothermal.
Work: AB = nR\u0394T = 2R(100) = 200R; CD = \u2212200R; these cancel.
BC = 2R(400) ln2 (expansion, since pressure falls 2 atm to 1 atm); DA = \u22122R(300) ln2.
Net work = 2R(400 \u2212 300) ln2 = 200 \xD7 8.31 \xD7 0.693 = 1152 J.
First law for the cycle: net heat = net work + \u0394U = 1152 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Three moles of an ideal gas (C\u209A = {7;2}R) at pressure P\u2090 and temperature T\u2090 are isothermally expanded to twice the initial volume. The gas is then compressed at constant pressure to its original volume. Finally the gas is compressed at constant volume to its original pressure P\u2090.
(a) Sketch P\u2013V and P\u2013T diagrams for the complete process.
(b) Calculate the net work done by the gas, and the net heat supplied to the gas during the complete process.`,a:"(b) 0.58 RT\u2090, 0.58 RT\u2090",s:`Steps: A\u2192B isothermal expansion (V to 2V, pressure P\u2090 to P\u2090/2). B\u2192C isobaric compression at P\u2090/2 (2V back to V, temperature T\u2090 to T\u2090/2). C\u2192A isochoric heating (pressure P\u2090/2 back to P\u2090).
P\u2013V diagram: a falling curve A\u2192B, a horizontal line leftwards B\u2192C, a vertical line up C\u2192A.
P\u2013T diagram: a vertical line down at T = T\u2090 for A\u2192B, a horizontal line leftwards at P\u2090/2 for B\u2192C, and a straight line through the origin up to A for C\u2192A.
Work: AB = 3RT\u2090 ln2 = 2.08RT\u2090. BC = (P\u2090/2)(V \u2212 2V) = \u2212P\u2090V/2 = \u22121.5RT\u2090 (since P\u2090V = 3RT\u2090). CA = 0.
Net work = 2.08RT\u2090 \u2212 1.5RT\u2090 = 0.58RT\u2090.
In a full cycle \u0394U = 0, so the net heat supplied = net work = 0.58RT\u2090.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Two moles of helium gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) are initially at temperature 27\xB0C and occupy a volume of 20 litres. The gas is first expanded at constant pressure until the volume is doubled. Then it undergoes an adiabatic change until the temperature returns to its initial value.
(i) Sketch the process on a P\u2013V diagram.
(ii) What are the final volume and pressure of the gas?
(iii) What is the workdone by gas?`,a:"(ii) 113 litre, 0.44 \xD7 10\u2075 N/m\xB2   (iii) 12450 J",s:`(i) P\u2013V diagram: a horizontal line from V = 20 L to 40 L at P\u2081, then a falling adiabatic curve to V\u2083 at the original temperature.
(ii) T\u2081 = 300 K, P\u2081 = nRT\u2081/V\u2081 = 2 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300/0.020 = 2.49 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa. After the isobaric step V\u2082 = 40 L, T\u2082 = 600 K.
Adiabatic: T V^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so 600 \xD7 40^(2/3) = 300 \xD7 V\u2083^(2/3), V\u2083 = 40 \xD7 2^(3/2) = 113 L. P\u2083 = nRT/V = 2 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300/0.113 = 0.44 \xD7 10\u2075 Pa.
(iii) Isobaric work = P\u2081\u0394V = 2.49 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 0.020 = 4980 J. Adiabatic work = nC\u1D65(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2083) = 2 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 8.3 \xD7 300 = 7470 J.
Total work = 4980 + 7470 = 12450 J.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The rectangular box shown in figure has a partition which can slide without friction along the length of the box. Initially each of the two chambers of the box has one mole of a mono-atomic ideal gas (\u03B3 = 5/3) at a pressure P\u2080, volume V\u2080 and temperature T\u2080. The chamber on the left is slowly heated by an electric heater. The walls of the box and the partition are thermally insulated. Heat loss through the lead wires of the heater is negligible. The gas in the left chamber expands pushing the partition until the final pressure in both chambers becomes 243P\u2080/32. Determine (i) the final temperature of the gas in each chamber and (ii) the workdone by the gas in the right chamber.",fig:{w:340,h:130,alt:"Rectangular insulated box with a sliding partition; each chamber has one mole of gas at P0, V0, T0; heater in the left chamber",d:[["r",30,30,280,70],["l",170,30,170,100,"B"],["r",14,52,16,26,"f"],["t",22,46,"heater","ms"],["t",100,70,"P\u2080, V\u2080, T\u2080","m"],["t",240,70,"P\u2080, V\u2080, T\u2080","m"]]},a:"(i) 2.25 T\u2080 in the right chamber (about 12.9 T\u2080 in the left chamber)   (ii) \u221215.58 T\u2080 joule",s:`Right chamber: insulated and compressed slowly, so adiabatic: P\u2080V\u2080^\u03B3 = (243P\u2080/32)V\u2082^\u03B3. So (V\u2082/V\u2080)^(5/3) = 32/243 = (2/3)\u2075, giving V\u2082 = (8/27)V\u2080.
Temperature: T\u2082 = (P\u2082V\u2082)/(P\u2080V\u2080) \xD7 T\u2080 = (243/32)(8/27)T\u2080 = 2.25T\u2080.
Left chamber: total volume is 2V\u2080, so V\u2081 = 2V\u2080 \u2212 8V\u2080/27 = 46V\u2080/27 and T\u2081 = (243/32)(46/27)T\u2080 \u2248 12.9T\u2080.
(ii) Work by the right gas = nC\u1D65(T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2082) = 1.5R(1 \u2212 2.25)T\u2080 = \u22121.875RT\u2080 = \u221215.58T\u2080 J (R = 8.31).
It is negative because the gas is compressed.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"Calculate the work done when one mole of a perfect gas is compressed adiabatically. The initial pressure and volume of the gas are 10\u2075 N/m\xB2 and 6 litre respectively. The final volume of the gas is 2 litre. Molar specific heat of the gas at constant volume is 3R/2.",a:"\u2212973.1 J",s:`C\u209A = C\u1D65 + R = 5R/2, so \u03B3 = 5/3 \u2248 1.67.
Adiabatic: P\u2082 = P\u2081(V\u2081/V\u2082)^\u03B3 = 10\u2075 \xD7 3^1.67 \u2248 6.26 \xD7 10\u2075 N/m\xB2.
Work done by the gas W = (P\u2081V\u2081 \u2212 P\u2082V\u2082)/(\u03B3 \u2212 1) = (10\u2075 \xD7 6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2212 6.26 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/0.67 = (600 \u2212 1252)/0.67 \u2248 \u2212973 J.
The work is negative because the gas is compressed (973 J is done on the gas).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A cyclic process ABCA shown in the V\u2013T diagram (fig.) is performed with a constant mass of an ideal gas. Show the same process on a P\u2013V diagram. (In the figure, CA is parallel to the V-axis and BC is parallel to the T-axis)",fig:{w:300,h:220,alt:"V-T diagram: A at (T1,V1), B at (T2,V2) joined by a straight line through the origin direction, C at (T1,V2); B to C horizontal, C to A vertical",d:[["l",50,190,50,20,"a"],["l",50,190,300,190,"a"],["t",44,26,"V","e"],["t",300,206,"T","e"],["l",50,70,110,70,"d"],["l",50,150,110,150,"d"],["l",110,150,110,190,"d"],["l",230,70,230,190,"d"],["l",110,150,230,70,"a"],["l",230,70,110,70,"a"],["l",110,70,110,150,"a"],["t",44,74,"V\u2082","e"],["t",44,154,"V\u2081","e"],["t",110,206,"T\u2081","m"],["t",230,206,"T\u2082","m"],["t",116,166,"A"],["t",236,66,"B"],["t",104,64,"C","e"]]},a:"P\u2013V diagram: A\u2192B is a horizontal line (isobaric expansion, V\u2081 to V\u2082 at pressure P\u2081); B\u2192C is a vertical line down (isochoric, V\u2082, pressure falls from P\u2081 to P\u2082 = P\u2081V\u2081/V\u2082); C\u2192A is an isothermal curve (hyperbola) from (V\u2082, P\u2082) back to (V\u2081, P\u2081).",s:`A\u2192B: the V\u2013T line passes through the origin, so V \u221D T and the pressure is constant (isobaric). V rises from V\u2081 to V\u2082 at P\u2081: a horizontal line in the P\u2013V diagram.
B\u2192C: BC is parallel to the T-axis, so V = V\u2082 is constant (isochoric). T falls from T\u2082 to T\u2081, so the pressure falls: a vertical line down. At C, P\u2082V\u2082 = P\u2081V\u2081 (same T\u2081 as at A), so P\u2082 = P\u2081V\u2081/V\u2082 < P\u2081.
C\u2192A: CA is parallel to the V-axis, so T = T\u2081 is constant (isothermal). V falls from V\u2082 to V\u2081 and the pressure rises from P\u2082 to P\u2081: a hyperbola PV = constant.
The P\u2013V diagram is a closed loop A\u2192B\u2192C\u2192A (horizontal line, vertical line, hyperbola).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"The curves A and B in the figure show P\u2013V graphs for an isothermal and an adiabatic process for an ideal gas. The isothermal process is represented by the curve A.",fig:{w:260,h:170,alt:"P-V graph with two falling curves from the same point; curve A lies above curve B",d:[["l",40,140,40,20,"a"],["l",40,140,240,140,"a"],["t",34,26,"P","e"],["t",240,156,"V","e"],["p","M70 40C80 90 120 105 210 110"],["p","M70 40C76 100 120 125 190 132"],["t",214,114,"A"],["t",194,138,"B"]]},o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`At any point the slope of the adiabatic curve is \u03B3 times that of the isothermal curve through the same point: (dP/dV)ad = \u2212\u03B3P/V, (dP/dV)iso = \u2212P/V.
So from a common point the adiabatic curve falls more steeply.
Curve B falls more steeply, so it is the adiabatic; the less steep curve A is the isothermal. The statement is True.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"ptp",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"An ideal gas with pressure P, volume V and temperature T is expanded isothermally to a volume 2V and a final pressure P\u1D62. If the same gas is expanded adiabatically to a volume 2V, the final pressure is P\u2090. The ratio of the specific heats of the gas is 1.67. The ratio {P\u2090;P\u1D62} is ______.",n:.628,s:`Isothermal: PV = P\u1D62(2V), so P\u1D62 = P/2.
Adiabatic: PV^\u03B3 = P\u2090(2V)^\u03B3, so P\u2090 = P/2^\u03B3.
P\u2090/P\u1D62 = (P/2^\u03B3)/(P/2) = 2^(1\u2212\u03B3) = 2^(\u22120.67).
2^0.67 \u2248 1.59, so the ratio is 1/1.59 \u2248 0.628.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"pts",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"A container of volume 1 m\xB3 is divided into two equal parts by a partition. One part has an ideal gas at 300 K and the other part is vacuum. The whole system is thermally isolated from the surroundings. When the partition is removed, the gas expands to occupy the whole volume. Its temperature will now be ______ (in K).",n:300,s:`The system is isolated, so Q = 0.
The gas expands into vacuum, so it does no work: W = 0.
First law: \u0394U = 0. The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, so the temperature does not change.
The temperature remains 300 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pts",st:"Entropy and free expansion",q:"Water of mass m gram is slowly heated to increase the temperature from T\u2081 to T\u2082. The change in entropy of the water, given specific heat is 1 J kg\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 is",o:["m ln(T\u2081/T\u2082)","zero","m(T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2081)","m ln(T\u2082/T\u2081)"],c:3,s:`For a slow (reversible) heating, dS = dQ/T = m s dT/T.
Integrate from T\u2081 to T\u2082: \u0394S = m s ln(T\u2082/T\u2081).
With s = 1 (in the given units), \u0394S = m ln(T\u2082/T\u2081), which is positive since T\u2082 > T\u2081.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A Carnot engine (E) is working between two temperatures 473 K and 273 K. In a new system two engines - engine E\u2081 works between 473 K to 373 K and engine E\u2082 works between 373 K to 273 K. If \u03B7\u2081\u2082, \u03B7\u2081 and \u03B7\u2082 are the efficiencies of the engines E, E\u2081 and E\u2082 respectively, then",o:["\u03B7\u2081\u2082 < \u03B7\u2081 + \u03B7\u2082","\u03B7\u2081\u2082 = \u03B7\u2081 + \u03B7\u2082","\u03B7\u2081\u2082 \u2265 \u03B7\u2081 + \u03B7\u2082","\u03B7\u2081\u2082 = \u03B7\u2081\u03B7\u2082"],c:0,s:`\u03B7 = 1 \u2212 T_cold/T_hot for a Carnot engine.
\u03B7\u2081\u2082 = 1 \u2212 273/473 = 200/473 \u2248 0.423.
\u03B7\u2081 = 1 \u2212 373/473 = 100/473 \u2248 0.211. \u03B7\u2082 = 1 \u2212 273/373 = 100/373 \u2248 0.268.
\u03B7\u2081 + \u03B7\u2082 \u2248 0.479, which is more than \u03B7\u2081\u2082.
So \u03B7\u2081\u2082 < \u03B7\u2081 + \u03B7\u2082.
Also \u03B7\u2081\u03B7\u2082 \u2248 0.057, which is not equal to \u03B7\u2081\u2082, so option (d) is wrong too.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Work done by a Carnot engine operating between temperatures 127\xB0C and 27\xB0C is 2 kJ. The amount of heat transferred to the engine by the reservoir is",o:["2 kJ","2.67 kJ","8 kJ","4 kJ"],c:2,s:`T\u2081 = 127 + 273 = 400 K, T\u2082 = 27 + 273 = 300 K.
\u03B7 = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081 = 1 \u2212 300/400 = 1/4.
\u03B7 = W/Q\u2081, so Q\u2081 = W/\u03B7 = 2 kJ \xD7 4 = 8 kJ.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:`An engine operating between the boiling and freezing points of water will have
A. efficiency more than 27%
B. efficiency less than the efficiency of a Carnot engine operating between the same two temperatures.
C. efficiency equal to 27%
D. efficiency less than 27%.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["B and C only","A and B only","B, C and D only","B and D only"],c:3,s:`Carnot efficiency between 100\xB0C and 0\xB0C: \u03B7 = 1 \u2212 273/373 = 0.268, about 26.8%, which is just under 27%.
No real engine can beat a Carnot engine working between the same temperatures, and a real engine is less efficient than it. So B is true.
Therefore its efficiency is below 26.8%, so it is less than 27% (D true), not more (A false) and not equal (C false).
So B and D only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine operating between two reservoirs has efficiency {1;3}. When the temperature of cold reservoir raised by x, its efficiency decrease to {1;6}. The value of x, if the temperature of the hot reservoir is 99\xB0C, will be",o:["62 K","16.5 K","33 K","66 K"],c:0,s:`T_h = 99 + 273 = 372 K.
\u03B7 = 1/3 gives T_c = (1 \u2212 1/3)T_h = (2/3)(372) = 248 K.
New efficiency 1/6 gives T_c' = (5/6)(372) = 310 K.
x = 310 \u2212 248 = 62 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine with efficiency 50% takes heat from a source at 600 K. In order to increase the efficiency to 70%, keeping the temperature of sink same, the new temperature of the source will be",o:["900 K","300 K","1000 K","360 K"],c:2,s:`\u03B7 = 1 \u2212 T_c/T_h. For 50%: T_c = (1 \u2212 0.5)(600) = 300 K.
For 70% with the same sink: 0.7 = 1 \u2212 300/T_h', so 300/T_h' = 0.3.
T_h' = 1000 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Let \u03B7\u2081 is the efficiency of an engine at T\u2081 = 447\xB0C and T\u2082 = 147\xB0C while \u03B7\u2082 is the efficiency at T\u2081 = 947\xB0C and T\u2082 = 47\xB0C. The ratio {\u03B7\u2081;\u03B7\u2082} will be",o:["0.41","0.56","0.73","0.70"],c:1,s:`Convert to kelvin: 447\xB0C = 720 K, 147\xB0C = 420 K, 947\xB0C = 1220 K, 47\xB0C = 320 K.
\u03B7\u2081 = 1 \u2212 420/720 = 300/720 = 0.417.
\u03B7\u2082 = 1 \u2212 320/1220 = 900/1220 = 0.738.
\u03B7\u2081/\u03B7\u2082 = 0.417/0.738 \u2248 0.56.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Read the following statements
A. When small temperature difference between a liquid and its surrounding is doubled, the rate of loss of heat of the liquid becomes twice.
B. Two bodies P and Q having equal surface areas are maintained at temperature 10\xB0C and 20\xB0C. The thermal radiation emitted in a given time by P and Q are in the ratio 1 : 1.15.
C. A Carnot Engine working between 100 K and 400 K has an efficiency of 75%.
D. When small temperature difference between a liquid and its surrounding is quadrupled, the rate of loss of heat of the liquid becomes twice.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["A, B, C only","A, B only","A, C only","B, C, D only"],c:0,s:`A: by Newton's law of cooling the rate of heat loss is proportional to the temperature difference, so doubling the difference doubles the rate. True.
B: radiation \u221D T\u2074. (283/293)\u2074 = 0.870 and 1/1.15 = 0.870. So the ratio is 1 : 1.15. True.
C: \u03B7 = 1 \u2212 100/400 = 0.75 = 75%. True.
D: quadrupling the difference makes the rate four times, not twice. False.
So A, B, C only.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"In 1st case, Carnot engine operates between temperatures 300 K and 100 K. In 2nd case, as shown in the figure, a combination of two engines is used. The efficiency of this combination (in 2nd case) will be",fig:{w:260,h:196,alt:"Two Carnot engines in series: E1 between reservoirs at 300 K and 200 K, E2 between 200 K and 100 K",d:[["r",90,8,60,24],["t",120,24,"300 K","m"],["c",120,58,16],["t",120,62,"E\u2081","ms"],["r",90,86,60,24],["t",120,102,"200 K","m"],["c",120,136,16],["t",120,140,"E\u2082","ms"],["r",90,164,60,24],["t",120,180,"100 K","m"],["l",120,32,120,42,"a"],["l",120,74,120,86,"a"],["l",120,110,120,120,"a"],["l",120,152,120,164,"a"],["t",170,184,"2nd case","s"]]},o:["same as the 1st case","always greater than the 1st case","always less than the 1st case","may increase or decrease with respect to the 1st case."],c:0,s:`1st case: \u03B7 = 1 \u2212 100/300 = 2/3.
2nd case: E\u2081 takes Q\u2081 from 300 K and rejects Q\u2082 to 200 K, so Q\u2082/Q\u2081 = 200/300. E\u2082 takes Q\u2082 and rejects Q\u2083 to 100 K, so Q\u2083/Q\u2082 = 100/200.
Q\u2083/Q\u2081 = (200/300)(100/200) = 1/3.
Combined efficiency = 1 \u2212 Q\u2083/Q\u2081 = 2/3, the same as the 1st case.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine has efficiency of 50%. If the temperature of sink is reduced by 40\xB0C, its efficiency increases by 30%. The temperature of the source will be",o:["166.7 K","255.1 K","266.7 K","367.7 K"],c:2,s:`Let source = T and sink = T_c. \u03B7 = 0.5 gives T_c = 0.5T.
Efficiency increases by 30%, so the new efficiency is 0.5 \xD7 1.3 = 0.65.
Then (T_c \u2212 40)/T = 1 \u2212 0.65 = 0.35, so 0.5T \u2212 40 = 0.35T.
0.15T = 40, so T = 266.7 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine whose heat sinks at 27\xB0C, has an efficiency of 25%. By how many degrees should the temperature of the source be changed to increase the efficiency by 100% of the original efficiency?",o:["Increases by 18\xB0C","Increases by 200\xB0C","Increases by 120\xB0C","Increases by 73\xB0C"],c:1,s:`Sink T_c = 300 K. With \u03B7 = 0.25: 1 \u2212 300/T = 0.25, so T = 400 K.
Increasing the efficiency by 100% of the original makes it 0.50: 1 \u2212 300/T' = 0.5, so T' = 600 K.
Change = 600 \u2212 400 = 200 K, so the source temperature must increase by 200\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A Carnot engine takes 5000 k cal of heat from a reservoir at 727\xB0C and gives heat to a sink at 127\xB0C. The work done by the engine is",o:["3 \xD7 10\u2076 J","zero","12.6 \xD7 10\u2076 J","8.4 \xD7 10\u2076 J"],c:2,s:`T\u2081 = 727 + 273 = 1000 K, T\u2082 = 127 + 273 = 400 K.
\u03B7 = 1 \u2212 400/1000 = 0.6.
W = \u03B7Q = 0.6 \xD7 5000 kcal = 3000 kcal.
In joule: 3000 \xD7 10\xB3 cal \xD7 4.2 J/cal = 12.6 \xD7 10\u2076 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"The coefficient of a Carnot's engine, working between steam point and ice point, will be",o:["26.81%","37.81%","47.81%","57.81%"],c:0,s:`Steam point = 373 K, ice point = 273 K.
Efficiency = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081 = 1 \u2212 273/373 = 100/373.
= 0.2681 = 26.81%.
Answer: (a) 26.81%.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"A refrigerator consumes an average 35 W power to operate between temperature \u221210\xB0C to 25\xB0C. If there is no loss of energy then how much average heat per second does it transfer?",o:["350 J/s","298 J/s","263 J/s","35 J/s"],c:2,s:`Cold side T\u2082 = 263 K, hot side T\u2081 = 298 K.
Coefficient of performance = T\u2082/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) = 263/35.
Heat removed per second = COP \xD7 power = (263/35) \xD7 35 = 263 J/s.
Answer: (c) 263 J/s.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A reversible engine has an efficiency of 1/4. If the temperature of the sink is reduced by 58\xB0C, its efficiency becomes double. Calculate the temperature of the sink.",o:["180.4\xB0C","382\xB0C","174\xB0C","280\xB0C"],c:2,s:`Initially 1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081 = 1/4, so T\u2082 = (3/4)T\u2081.
After cooling the sink by 58: 1 \u2212 (T\u2082 \u2212 58)/T\u2081 = 1/2, so (T\u2082 \u2212 58)/T\u2081 = 1/2.
Subtract: 58/T\u2081 = 3/4 \u2212 1/2 = 1/4, so T\u2081 = 232 K.
Sink: T\u2082 = (3/4)(232) = 174 K.
The value 174 is the one given in option (c) (the book prints its unit as \xB0C, but the number that matches is 174).
Answer: (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A heat engine has an efficiency of {1;6}. When the temperature of sink is reduced by 62\xB0C, its efficiency get doubled. The temperature of the source is",o:["99\xB0C","124\xB0C","37\xB0C","62\xB0C"],c:0,s:`Initially T\u2082/T\u2081 = 5/6, so T\u2082 = (5/6)T\u2081.
After the change (T\u2082 \u2212 62)/T\u2081 = 1 \u2212 1/3 = 2/3.
Subtract: 62/T\u2081 = 5/6 \u2212 4/6 = 1/6, so T\u2081 = 372 K.
T\u2081 = 372 \u2212 273 = 99\xB0C.
Answer: (a) 99\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Two Carnot engines A and B are operated in series such that engine A absorbs heat at T\u2081 and rejects heat to a sink at temperature T. Engine B absorbs half of the heat rejected by Engine A and rejects heat to the sink at T\u2083. When workdone in both the cases is equal, the value of T is",o:["{1;3}T\u2081 + {2;3}T\u2083","{2;3}T\u2081 + {3;2}T\u2083","{3;2}T\u2081 + {1;3}T\u2083","{2;3}T\u2081 + {1;3}T\u2083"],c:3,s:`Let engine A absorb Q\u2081 and reject Q = Q\u2081T/T\u2081. Work of A: W_A = Q\u2081 \u2212 Q = Q(T\u2081 \u2212 T)/T.
Engine B takes Q/2 at T and rejects at T\u2083: W_B = (Q/2)(T \u2212 T\u2083)/T.
Equal work: (T\u2081 \u2212 T) = (T \u2212 T\u2083)/2, so 2T\u2081 \u2212 2T = T \u2212 T\u2083.
3T = 2T\u2081 + T\u2083, so T = (2/3)T\u2081 + (1/3)T\u2083.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A Carnot's engine working between 400 K and 800 K has a work output of 1200 J per cycle. The amount of heat energy supplied to the engine from the source in each cycle is",o:["1600 J","2400 J","1800 J","3200 J"],c:1,s:`Efficiency = 1 \u2212 400/800 = 1/2.
Heat supplied = work/efficiency = 1200/(1/2) = 2400 J.
Answer: (b) 2400 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Efficiency of cycles",q:"A heat engine is involved with exchange of heat of 1915 J, \u221240 J, +125 J, and \u2212Q J, during one cycle achieving an efficiency of 50.0%. The value of Q is",o:["640 J","980 J","400 J","40 J"],c:1,s:`Heat absorbed (positive entries) = 1915 + 125 = 2040 J.
Net work in the cycle = sum of all heat entries = 1915 \u2212 40 + 125 \u2212 Q = 2000 \u2212 Q.
Efficiency = work/heat absorbed: (2000 \u2212 Q)/2040 = 0.5, so 2000 \u2212 Q = 1020.
Q = 980 J.
Answer: (b) 980 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Two ideal Carnot engines operate in cascade (all heat given up by one engine is used by the other engine to produce work) between temperatures, T\u2081 and T\u2082. The temperature of the hot reservoir of the first engine is T\u2081 and the temperature of the cold reservoir of the second engine is T\u2082. T is temperature of the sink of first engine which is also the source for the second engine. How is T related to T\u2081 and T\u2082, if both the engines perform equal amount of work?",o:["T = \u221A(T\u2081T\u2082)","T = 0","T = {2T\u2081T\u2082;T\u2081+T\u2082}","T = {T\u2081+T\u2082;2}"],c:3,s:`Let the first engine take Q\u2081 at T\u2081 and reject Q at T, where Q = Q\u2081T/T\u2081. Its work is Q\u2081 \u2212 Q.
The second engine takes Q at T and rejects Q\u2082 = QT\u2082/T. Its work is Q \u2212 Q\u2082.
Heat is proportional to temperature for a Carnot engine, so work \u221D temperature drop: equal work gives T\u2081 \u2212 T = T \u2212 T\u2082.
So 2T = T\u2081 + T\u2082 and T = (T\u2081 + T\u2082)/2.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"A Carnot engine having an efficiency of {1;10} is being used as a refrigerator. If the work done on the refrigerator is 10 J, the amount of heat absorbed from the reservoir at lower temperature is",o:["99 J","100 J","90 J","1 J"],c:2,s:`Engine efficiency 1/10 means T\u2082/T\u2081 = 9/10.
As a refrigerator: COP = T\u2082/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) = 9/(10 \u2212 9) = 9.
Heat absorbed = COP \xD7 work = 9 \xD7 10 = 90 J.
Answer: (c) 90 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine has an efficiency of 1/6. When the temperature of the sink is reduced by 62\xB0C, its efficiency is doubled. The temperatures of the source and the sink are, respectively,",o:["99\xB0C, 37\xB0C","37\xB0C, 99\xB0C","124\xB0C, 62\xB0C","62\xB0C, 124\xB0C"],c:0,s:`Initially T\u2082/T\u2081 = 5/6. After cooling (T\u2082 \u2212 62)/T\u2081 = 2/3.
Subtract: 62/T\u2081 = 1/6, so T\u2081 = 372 K = 99\xB0C.
T\u2082 = (5/6)(372) = 310 K = 37\xB0C.
Answer: (a) 99\xB0C, 37\xB0C.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Two Carnot engines A and B are operated in series. The first one, A, receives heat at T\u2081 (= 600 K) and rejects to a reservoir at temperature T\u2082. The second engine B receives heat rejected by the first engine and, in turn, rejects to a heat reservoir at T\u2083 (= 400 K). Calculate the temperature T\u2082 if the work outputs of the two engines are equal.",o:["500 K","300 K","400 K","600 K"],c:0,s:`For a Carnot engine the heat is proportional to the temperature, so equal work needs equal temperature drops.
T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082 = T\u2082 \u2212 T\u2083.
T\u2082 = (T\u2081 + T\u2083)/2 = (600 + 400)/2 = 500 K.
Answer: (a) 500 K.`},{y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Three Carnot engines operate in series between a heat source at a temperature T\u2081 and a heat sink at temperature T\u2084 (see figure). There are two other reservoirs at temperature T\u2082 and T\u2083, as shown, with T\u2081 > T\u2082 > T\u2083 > T\u2084. The three engines are equally efficient if",fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"Four reservoirs at T1, T2, T3 and T4 stacked vertically, with Carnot engines E1, E2 and E3 placed between successive reservoirs",d:[["r",60,14,160,24],["t",232,31,"T\u2081"],["c",140,56,12],["t",162,60,"E\u2081"],["l",140,38,140,44],["l",140,68,140,74],["r",60,74,160,24],["t",232,91,"T\u2082"],["c",140,116,12],["t",162,120,"E\u2082"],["l",140,98,140,104],["l",140,128,140,134],["r",60,134,160,24],["t",232,151,"T\u2083"],["c",140,176,12],["t",162,180,"E\u2083"],["l",140,158,140,164],["l",140,188,140,194],["r",60,194,160,24],["t",232,211,"T\u2084"]]},o:["T\u2082 = (T\u2081\xB2T\u2084)^(1/3); T\u2083 = (T\u2081T\u2084\xB2)^(1/3)","T\u2082 = (T\u2081T\u2084)^(1/2); T\u2083 = (T\u2081\xB2T\u2084)^(1/3)","T\u2082 = (T\u2081\xB3T\u2084)^(1/4); T\u2083 = (T\u2081T\u2084\xB3)^(1/4)","T\u2082 = (T\u2081T\u2084\xB2)^(1/3); T\u2083 = (T\u2081\xB2T\u2084)^(1/3)"],c:0,s:`Equal efficiencies mean equal temperature ratios: T\u2082/T\u2081 = T\u2083/T\u2082 = T\u2084/T\u2083 = r.
Then T\u2084/T\u2081 = r\xB3, so r = (T\u2084/T\u2081)^(1/3).
T\u2082 = T\u2081r = T\u2081^(2/3) T\u2084^(1/3) = (T\u2081\xB2T\u2084)^(1/3).
T\u2083 = T\u2081r\xB2 = T\u2081^(1/3) T\u2084^(2/3) = (T\u2081T\u2084\xB2)^(1/3).
Answer: (a).`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"A Carnot's engine works as a refrigerator between 250 K and 300 K. It receives 500 cal heat from the reservoir at the lower temperature. The amount of work done in each cycle to operate the refrigerator is",o:["2520 J","772 J","2100 J","420 J"],c:3,s:`COP = T\u2082/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) = 250/(300 \u2212 250) = 5.
Work = heat absorbed/COP = 500/5 = 100 cal.
In joules: 100 \xD7 4.2 = 420 J.
Answer: (d) 420 J.`},{y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"Two Carnot engines A and B are operated in series. Engine A receives heat from a reservoir at 600 K and rejects heat to a reservoir at temperature T. Engine B receives heat rejected by engine A and in turn rejects it to a reservoir at 100 K. Both engines do the same amount of work. If the efficiencies of the two engines A and B are represented by \u03B7_A and \u03B7_B, respectively, what is the value of \u03B7_B/\u03B7_A?",o:["{1;12}","{5;12}","{12;7}","{12;5}"],c:2,s:`The standard condition for this series problem is that both engines do equal work, so T = (600 + 100)/2 = 350 K (the printed question does not state this, but it is needed to fix T).
\u03B7_A = 1 \u2212 350/600 = 5/12.
\u03B7_B = 1 \u2212 100/350 = 5/7.
\u03B7_B/\u03B7_A = (5/7)/(5/12) = 12/7.
Answer: (c) 12/7.`},{y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"ptc",st:"Efficiency of cycles",q:"An engine operates by taking n moles of an ideal gas through the cycle ABCDA shown in figure. The thermal efficiency of the engine is (Take C_V = 1.5R, where R is gas constant)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph of a rectangular cycle ABCDA: A to B at constant volume V0 from P0 to 2P0, B to C at pressure 2P0 from V0 to 2V0, C to D at constant volume 2V0 down to P0, D to A at pressure P0 back to V0",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",312,186,"V","s"],["t",52,24,"P","es"],["l",60,50,120,50,"d"],["l",60,120,120,120,"d"],["l",120,120,120,170,"d"],["l",220,120,220,170,"d"],["l",120,120,120,50,"a"],["l",120,50,220,50,"a"],["l",220,50,220,120,"a"],["l",220,120,120,120,"a"],["t",54,124,"P\u2080","es"],["t",120,184,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",220,184,"2V\u2080","ms"],["t",54,54,"2P\u2080","es"],["t",110,134,"A","es"],["t",110,46,"B","es"],["t",228,46,"C","s"],["t",228,134,"D","s"]]},o:["0.15","0.32","0.24","0.08"],c:0,s:`The cycle is a rectangle: A(V\u2080, P\u2080) \u2192 B(V\u2080, 2P\u2080) \u2192 C(2V\u2080, 2P\u2080) \u2192 D(2V\u2080, P\u2080) \u2192 A. Work = area = P\u2080V\u2080.
Heat enters in A\u2192B (constant volume) and B\u2192C (constant pressure).
Q_AB = nC_V \u0394T = 1.5 \xD7 (2P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080) = 1.5P\u2080V\u2080.
Q_BC = nC_P \u0394T = 2.5 \xD7 (4P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 2P\u2080V\u2080) = 5P\u2080V\u2080.
Efficiency = P\u2080V\u2080/(6.5P\u2080V\u2080) = 0.154 \u2248 0.15.
Answer: (a) 0.15.`},{y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"A Carnot freezer takes heat from water at 0\xB0C inside it and rejects it to the room at a temperature of 27\xB0C. The latent heat of ice is 336 \xD7 10\xB3 J kg\u207B\xB9. If 5 kg of water at 0\xB0C is converted into ice at 0\xB0C by the freezer, then the energy consumed by the freezer is close to",o:["1.51 \xD7 10\u2075 J","1.68 \xD7 10\u2076 J","1.71 \xD7 10\u2077 J","1.67 \xD7 10\u2075 J"],c:3,s:`Heat removed from the water: Q = mL = 5 \xD7 336 \xD7 10\xB3 = 1.68 \xD7 10\u2076 J.
Cold side 273 K, room 300 K. COP = 273/(300 \u2212 273) = 273/27.
Work = Q/COP = 1.68 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 27/273 \u2248 1.66 \xD7 10\u2075 J.
This is close to 1.67 \xD7 10\u2075 J.
Answer: (d).`},{y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"pts",st:"Entropy and free expansion",q:`A solid body of constant heat capacity 1 J/\xB0C is being heated by keeping it in contact with reservoirs in two ways
(i) Sequentially keeping in contact with 2 reservoirs such that each reservoir supplies same amount of heat.
(ii) Sequentially keeping in contact with 8 reservoirs such that each reservoir supplies same amount of heat.
In both the cases body is brought from initial temperature 100\xB0C to final temperature 200\xB0C. Entropy change of the body in the two cases respectively is`,o:["ln2, 2ln2","2ln2, 8ln2","ln2, 4ln2","ln2, ln2"],c:3,s:`Entropy is a state function. The body goes from the same initial state to the same final state in both ways.
So its entropy change is the same in both cases and does not depend on the number of reservoirs.
\u0394S = C ln(T_f/T_i) = 1 \xD7 ln(200/100) = ln 2 (the question intends the temperatures 100 and 200 in the ratio 1 : 2).
Both cases give ln2.
Answer: (d) ln2, ln2.`},{y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"ptc",st:"Work and heat in cycles",q:"The given P-V diagram represents the thermodynamic cycle of an engine, operating with an ideal monoatomic gas. The amount of heat, extracted from the source in a single cycle is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V diagram of an engine cycle: a rectangle with pressure between P0 and 2P0 and volume between V0 and 2V0, traversed clockwise",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",312,186,"V","s"],["t",52,24,"P","es"],["l",60,50,120,50,"d"],["l",60,120,120,120,"d"],["l",120,120,120,170,"d"],["l",220,120,220,170,"d"],["l",120,120,120,50,"a"],["l",120,50,220,50,"a"],["l",220,50,220,120,"a"],["l",220,120,120,120,"a"],["t",54,124,"P\u2080","es"],["t",120,184,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",220,184,"2V\u2080","ms"],["t",54,54,"2P\u2080","es"]]},o:["4P\u2080V\u2080","P\u2080V\u2080","{13;2}P\u2080V\u2080","{11;2}P\u2080V\u2080"],c:2,s:`The gas absorbs heat in the constant-volume rise (P\u2080 to 2P\u2080 at V\u2080) and in the constant-pressure expansion (V\u2080 to 2V\u2080 at 2P\u2080).
Monatomic gas: C_V = 1.5R, C_P = 2.5R.
Q\u2081 = 1.5 \xD7 \u0394(PV) = 1.5(2P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080) = 1.5P\u2080V\u2080.
Q\u2082 = 2.5 \xD7 \u0394(PV) = 2.5(4P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 2P\u2080V\u2080) = 5P\u2080V\u2080.
Total heat taken from the source = 6.5P\u2080V\u2080 = (13/2)P\u2080V\u2080.
Answer: (c).`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine, whose efficiency is 40%, takes in heat from a source maintained at a temperature of 500 K. It is desired to have an engine of efficiency 60%. Then, the intake temperature for the same exhaust (sink) temperature must be",o:["1200 K","750 K","600 K","efficiency of Carnot engine cannot be made larger than 50%."],c:1,s:`First engine: 0.4 = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/500, so T\u2082 = 300 K.
Second engine, same sink: 0.6 = 1 \u2212 300/T\u2081, so 300/T\u2081 = 0.4.
T\u2081 = 750 K.
Answer: (b) 750 K.`},{y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"ptc",st:"Efficiency of cycles",q:"Helium gas goes through a cycle ABCDA (consisting of two isochoric and two isobaric lines) as shown in figure. Efficiency of this cycle is nearly (Assume the gas to be close to ideal gas)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph of a rectangular cycle ABCDA: A to B at constant volume V0 from P0 to 2P0, B to C at pressure 2P0 from V0 to 2V0, C to D at constant volume 2V0 down to P0, D to A at pressure P0 back to V0",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",312,186,"V","s"],["t",52,24,"P","es"],["l",60,50,120,50,"d"],["l",60,120,120,120,"d"],["l",120,120,120,170,"d"],["l",220,120,220,170,"d"],["l",120,120,120,50,"a"],["l",120,50,220,50,"a"],["l",220,50,220,120,"a"],["l",220,120,120,120,"a"],["t",54,124,"P\u2080","es"],["t",120,184,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",220,184,"2V\u2080","ms"],["t",54,54,"2P\u2080","es"],["t",110,134,"A","es"],["t",110,46,"B","es"],["t",228,46,"C","s"],["t",228,134,"D","s"]]},o:["9.1%","10.5%","12.5%","15.4%"],c:3,s:`Helium is monatomic: C_V = 1.5R, C_P = 2.5R. Work = area = P\u2080V\u2080.
Heat absorbed in A\u2192B (isochoric): 1.5(2P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080) = 1.5P\u2080V\u2080.
Heat absorbed in B\u2192C (isobaric): 2.5(4P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 2P\u2080V\u2080) = 5P\u2080V\u2080.
Efficiency = P\u2080V\u2080/6.5P\u2080V\u2080 = 0.154 = 15.4%.
Answer: (d) 15.4%.`},{y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A Carnot engine operating between temperatures T\u2081 and T\u2082 has efficiency {1;6}. When T\u2082 is lowered by 62 K, its efficiency increases to {1;3}. Then T\u2081 and T\u2082 are, respectively",o:["372 K and 310 K","372 K and 330 K","330 K and 268 K","310 K and 248 K"],c:0,s:`Initially T\u2082/T\u2081 = 5/6. After lowering: (T\u2082 \u2212 62)/T\u2081 = 2/3.
Subtract: 62/T\u2081 = 1/6, so T\u2081 = 372 K.
T\u2082 = (5/6)(372) = 310 K.
Answer: (a) 372 K and 310 K.`},{y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A diatomic ideal gas is used in a Carnot engine as the working substance. If during the adiabatic expansion part of the cycle the volume of the gas increases from V to 32V, the efficiency of the engine is",o:["0.25","0.5","0.75","0.99"],c:2,s:`Diatomic gas: \u03B3 = 7/5, so \u03B3 \u2212 1 = 0.4.
Adiabatic: TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, so T_cold/T_hot = (V/32V)^0.4 = (2\u207B\u2075)^0.4 = 2\u207B\xB2 = 1/4.
Efficiency = 1 \u2212 T_cold/T_hot = 1 \u2212 1/4 = 0.75.
Answer: (c) 0.75.`},{y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"A Carnot engine, having an efficiency of \u03B7 = 1/10 as heat engine, is used as a refrigerator. If the work done on the system is 10 J, the amount of energy absorbed from the reservoir at lower temperature is",o:["100 J","99 J","90 J","1 J"],c:2,s:`Engine efficiency 1/10 means T\u2082/T\u2081 = 9/10.
COP as refrigerator = T\u2082/(T\u2081 \u2212 T\u2082) = 9.
Heat absorbed from the cold reservoir = 9 \xD7 10 J = 90 J.
Answer: (c) 90 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"ptc",st:"Efficiency of cycles",q:"The temperature-entropy diagram of a reversible engine cycle is given in the figure. Its efficiency is",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"T-S diagram of a reversible engine: a right triangle with vertical side at S0 from T0 up to 2T0, a sloping side from (S0, 2T0) down to (2S0, T0), and a horizontal side back along T0",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",312,186,"S","s"],["t",52,24,"T","es"],["l",60,60,120,60,"d"],["l",60,120,120,120,"d"],["l",120,120,120,170,"d"],["l",220,120,220,170,"d"],["l",120,120,120,60,"a"],["l",120,60,220,120,"a"],["l",220,120,120,120,"a"],["t",54,64,"2T\u2080","es"],["t",54,124,"T\u2080","es"],["t",120,184,"S\u2080","ms"],["t",220,184,"2S\u2080","ms"]]},o:["1/3","2/3","1/2","1/4"],c:0,s:`In a T\u2013S diagram the area of the loop is the net work, and the heat absorbed is the area under the part where S increases.
Work = area of triangle = \xBD \xD7 (2S\u2080 \u2212 S\u2080) \xD7 (2T\u2080 \u2212 T\u2080) = \xBDS\u2080T\u2080.
Heat absorbed along the sloping side = average temperature \xD7 \u0394S = (1.5T\u2080)(S\u2080) = 1.5S\u2080T\u2080.
Efficiency = 0.5/1.5 = 1/3.
Answer: (a) 1/3.`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A Carnot engine takes 3 \xD7 10\u2076 cal of heat from a reservoir at 627\xB0C, and gives it to a sink at 27\xB0C. The work done by the engine is",o:["4.2 \xD7 10\u2076 J","8.4 \xD7 10\u2076 J","16.8 \xD7 10\u2076 J","zero"],c:1,s:`T\u2081 = 900 K, T\u2082 = 300 K. Efficiency = 1 \u2212 300/900 = 2/3.
Work = (2/3)(3 \xD7 10\u2076 cal) = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 cal.
In joules: 2 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 4.2 = 8.4 \xD7 10\u2076 J.
Answer: (b) 8.4 \xD7 10\u2076 J.`},{y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"pts",st:"Second law and reversibility",q:'"Heat cannot by itself flow from a body at lower temperature to a body at higher temperature" is a statement or consequence of',o:["second law of thermodynamics","conservation of momentum","conservation of mass","first law of thermodynamics"],c:0,s:`This is the Clausius statement of the second law.
Answer: (a) second law of thermodynamics.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pts",st:"Second law and reversibility",q:"Even Carnot engine cannot give 100% efficiency because we cannot",o:["prevent radiation","find ideal sources","reach absolute zero temperature","eliminate friction"],c:2,s:`Efficiency = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081 equals 1 only if the sink is at T\u2082 = 0 K.
So 100% efficiency would need the sink at absolute zero, which cannot be reached.
Answer: (c) reach absolute zero temperature.`},{y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"pts",st:"Second law and reversibility",q:"Which statement is incorrect?",o:["All reversible cycles have same efficiency.","Reversible cycle has more efficiency than an irreversible one.","Carnot cycle is a reversible one.","Carnot cycle has the maximum efficiency in all cycles."],c:0,s:`All reversible engines working between the same two temperatures have the same efficiency, but reversible cycles working between different temperature pairs have different efficiencies.
So statement (a) is wrong. Statements (b), (c) and (d) are correct.
Answer: (a).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"An ideal gas heat engine is operating between 227\xB0C and 127\xB0C. It absorbs 10\u2074 J of heat at the higher temperature. The amount of heat converted into work is",o:["2000 J","4000 J","8000 J","5600 J."],c:0,s:`T\u2081 = 500 K, T\u2082 = 400 K. Efficiency = 1 \u2212 400/500 = 0.2.
Work = 0.2 \xD7 10\u2074 = 2000 J.
Answer: (a) 2000 J.`},{y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"The efficiency of a Carnot engine operating with a hot reservoir kept at a temperature of 1000 K is 0.4. It extracts 150 J of heat per cycle from the hot reservoir. The work extracted from this engine is being fully used to run a heat pump which has a coefficient of performance 10. The hot reservoir of the heat pump is at a temperature of 300 K. Which of the following statements is/are correct?",o:["Work extracted from the Carnot engine in one cycle is 60 J.","Temperature of the cold reservoir of the Carnot engine is 600 K.","Temperature of the cold reservoir of the heat pump is 270 K.","Heat supplied to the hot reservoir of the heat pump in one cycle is 540 J."],c:[0,1,2],s:`Engine: W = \u03B7Q = 0.4 \xD7 150 = 60 J, so (a) is correct.
Engine cold reservoir: 0.4 = 1 \u2212 T/1000, so T = 600 K, so (b) is correct.
Heat pump: COP = T_hot/(T_hot \u2212 T_cold) = 10 gives 300/(300 \u2212 T) = 10, so T = 270 K, so (c) is correct.
Heat supplied to the hot reservoir = COP \xD7 W = 10 \xD7 60 = 600 J, not 540 J, so (d) is wrong.
Answer: (a), (b), (c).`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"ptf",st:"Internal energy and first law",q:`A steam engine intakes 50 g of steam at 100\xB0C per minute and cools it down to 20\xB0C. If latent heat of vaporization of steam is 540 cal g\u207B\xB9, then the heat rejected by the steam engine per minute is ______ \xD7 10\xB3 cal.
(Given : specific heat capacity of water : 1 cal g\u207B\xB9 \xB0C\u207B\xB9)`,n:31,s:`Heat rejected = heat to condense the steam + heat to cool the water.
Condensation: 50 \xD7 540 = 27000 cal.
Cooling from 100\xB0C to 20\xB0C: 50 \xD7 1 \xD7 80 = 4000 cal.
Total = 31000 cal = 31 \xD7 10\xB3 cal.
Answer: 31.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A heat engine operates with the cold reservoir at temperature 324 K. The minimum temperature of the hot reservoir if the engine takes 300 J heat from the hot reservoir and delivers 180 J heat to the cold reservoir per cycle, is ______ K.",n:540,s:`For the minimum hot temperature the engine is a Carnot engine: Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = T\u2081/T\u2082.
T\u2081 = 324 \xD7 300/180 = 540 K.
Answer: 540.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"In a Carnot engine, the temperature of reservoir is 527\xB0C and that of sink is 200 K. If the workdone by the engine when it transfers heat from reservoir to sink is 12000 kJ, the quantity of heat absorbed from reservoir is ______ \xD7 10\u2076 J.",n:16,s:`T\u2081 = 527 + 273 = 800 K, T\u2082 = 200 K.
Efficiency = 1 \u2212 200/800 = 3/4.
Heat absorbed = work/efficiency = 12000 kJ/(3/4) = 16000 kJ = 16 \xD7 10\u2076 J.
Answer: 16.`},{y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"300 cal of heat is given to a heat engine and it rejects 225 cal. of heat. If source temperature is 227\xB0C, then the temperature of sink will be ______ \xB0C.",n:102,s:`For a Carnot engine Q\u2082/Q\u2081 = T\u2082/T\u2081.
T\u2081 = 227 + 273 = 500 K. T\u2082 = 500 \xD7 225/300 = 375 K.
T\u2082 in \xB0C = 375 \u2212 273 = 102\xB0C.
Answer: 102.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A heat engine operates between a cold reservoir at temperature T\u2082 = 400 K and a hot reservoir at temperature T\u2081. It takes 300 J of heat from the hot reservoir and delivers 240 J of heat to the cold reservoir in a cycle. The minimum temperature of the hot reservoir has to be ______ K.",n:500,s:`The hot reservoir is coldest possible when the engine is reversible: T\u2081/T\u2082 = Q\u2081/Q\u2082.
T\u2081 = 400 \xD7 300/240 = 500 K.
Answer: 500.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"For an ideal heat engine, the temperature of the source is 127\xB0C. In order to have 60% efficiency the temperature of the sink should be ______ \xB0C. (Round off to nearest integer).",n:-113,s:`T\u2081 = 127 + 273 = 400 K.
0.6 = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/400, so T\u2082 = 0.4 \xD7 400 = 160 K.
In \xB0C: 160 \u2212 273 = \u2212113\xB0C.
The book's key prints 113 (with an asterisk); the correct value is \u2212113\xB0C, whose magnitude is 113.
Answer: \u2212113.`},{y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Changing source and sink temperatures",q:"A reversible heat engine converts one-fourth of the heat input into work. When the temperature of the sink is reduced by 52 K, its efficiency is doubled. The temperature in Kelvin of the source will be ______.",n:208,s:`Efficiency 1/4 gives T\u2082/T\u2081 = 3/4.
Doubled efficiency 1/2 gives (T\u2082 \u2212 52)/T\u2081 = 1/2.
Subtract: 52/T\u2081 = 3/4 \u2212 1/2 = 1/4, so T\u2081 = 208 K.
Answer: 208.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Refrigerators and heat pumps",q:"If minimum possible work is done by a refrigerator in converting 100 grams of water at 0\xB0C to ice, how much heat (in calories) is released to the surroundings at temperature 27\xB0C (Latent heat of ice = 80 cal/gram) to the nearest integer?",n:8791,s:`Heat removed from water: Q\u2082 = 100 \xD7 80 = 8000 cal.
Minimum work means a reversible (Carnot) refrigerator: Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = T\u2081/T\u2082 = 300/273.
Heat released to the surroundings Q\u2081 = 8000 \xD7 300/273 = 8791.2 cal.
To the nearest integer: 8791.
Answer: 8791.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"ptc",st:"Efficiency of cycles",q:"An engine operates by taking a monatomic ideal gas through the cycle shown in the figure. The percentage efficiency of the engine is close to ______.",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"P-V graph of a rectangular cycle ABCDA: A at (V0, P0), B at (V0, 3P0), C at (2V0, 3P0), D at (2V0, P0), traversed A to B to C to D",d:[["l",60,170,310,170,"a"],["l",60,170,60,20,"a"],["t",312,186,"V","s"],["t",52,24,"P","es"],["l",60,50,120,50,"d"],["l",60,130,120,130,"d"],["l",120,130,120,170,"d"],["l",220,130,220,170,"d"],["l",120,130,120,50,"a"],["l",120,50,220,50,"a"],["l",220,50,220,130,"a"],["l",220,130,120,130,"a"],["t",54,134,"P\u2080","es"],["t",120,184,"V\u2080","ms"],["t",220,184,"2V\u2080","ms"],["l",60,90,120,90,"d"],["t",54,94,"2P\u2080","es"],["t",54,54,"3P\u2080","es"],["t",110,144,"A","es"],["t",110,46,"B","es"],["t",228,46,"C","s"],["t",228,144,"D","s"]]},n:19,s:`The cycle is a rectangle: pressure from P\u2080 to 3P\u2080, volume from V\u2080 to 2V\u2080. Work = (2P\u2080)(V\u2080) = 2P\u2080V\u2080.
Heat enters in A\u2192B (isochoric) and B\u2192C (isobaric). Monatomic gas: C_V = 1.5R, C_P = 2.5R.
Q_AB = 1.5(3P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 P\u2080V\u2080) = 3P\u2080V\u2080.
Q_BC = 2.5(6P\u2080V\u2080 \u2212 3P\u2080V\u2080) = 7.5P\u2080V\u2080.
Efficiency = 2/(3 + 7.5) = 2/10.5 = 19.05% \u2248 19%.
Answer: 19.`},{y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"pte",st:"Carnot engine",q:"A Carnot engine operates between two reservoirs of temperature 900 K and 300 K. The engine performs 1200 J of work per cycle. The heat energy (in J) delivered by the engine to the low temperature reservoir, in a cycle, is ______.",n:600,s:`Efficiency = 1 \u2212 300/900 = 2/3.
Heat absorbed Q\u2081 = W/\u03B7 = 1200/(2/3) = 1800 J.
Heat delivered to the cold reservoir Q\u2082 = Q\u2081 \u2212 W = 1800 \u2212 1200 = 600 J.
Answer: 600.`},{y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pts",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : In an insulated container, a gas is adiabatically shrunk to half of its initial volume. The temperature of the gas decreases.
Reasons (R) : Free expansion of an ideal gas is an irreversible and an adiabatic process.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is false but (R) is true","(A) is true but (R) is false","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)"],c:1,s:`Adiabatic compression: no heat leaves, and work is done on the gas, so its internal energy and temperature rise (TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant). Hence (A) is false.
(R) is true: free expansion is rapid, irreversible and no heat is exchanged.
Answer: (b) (A) is false but (R) is true.`},{y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"pte",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as assertion A and the other is labelled as reason R.
Assertion (A) : Efficiency of a reversible heat engine will be highest at \u2212273\xB0C temperature of cold reservoir.
Reason (R) : The efficiency of Carnot's engine depends not only on temperature of cold reservoir but it depends on the temperature of hot reservoir too and is given as \u03B7 = (1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081).
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A) is true but (R) is false.","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is false but (R) is true."],c:1,s:`\u2212273\xB0C is 0 K. With T\u2082 = 0 the formula \u03B7 = 1 \u2212 T\u2082/T\u2081 gives \u03B7 = 1, the highest possible value, so (A) is true.
(R) is true: it states the formula and the dependence on both T\u2081 and T\u2082.
The formula in (R) is exactly what shows that the efficiency is greatest when T\u2082 is lowest, so (R) explains (A).
Answer: (b).`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:"One mole of a diatomic ideal gas (\u03B3 = 1.4) is taken through a cyclic process starting from point A. The process A \u2192 B is adiabatic compression, B \u2192 C is isobaric expansion, C \u2192 D is adiabatic expansion, and D \u2192 A is isochoric. The volume ratios are V_A/V_B = 16 and V_C/V_B = 2 and the temperature at A is T_A = 300 K. Calculate the temperature of the gas at the points B and D and find the efficiency of the cycle.",a:"T_B = 909 K, T_D = 791 K, efficiency = 61.40%",s:`A\u2192B adiabatic: T_B = T_A(V_A/V_B)^0.4 = 300 \xD7 16^0.4 = 300 \xD7 3.03 = 909 K.
B\u2192C isobaric: T_C = T_B \xD7 (V_C/V_B) = 1818 K.
C\u2192D adiabatic with V_D = V_A = 16V_B, so V_D/V_C = 8: T_D = 1818 \xD7 (1/8)^0.4 = 1818/2.297 = 791 K.
Heat in (B\u2192C) = C_P(T_C \u2212 T_B) = 3.5R \xD7 909. Heat out (D\u2192A, isochoric) = C_V(T_D \u2212 T_A) = 2.5R \xD7 491.
Efficiency = 1 \u2212 (2.5 \xD7 491)/(3.5 \xD7 909) = 1 \u2212 1227.5/3181.5 = 0.614 = 61.4%.`},{y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"ptc",st:"Statements, matching and subjective",q:`An ideal gas is taken through a cyclic thermodynamic process through four steps. The amounts of heat involved in these steps are Q\u2081 = 5960 J, Q\u2082 = \u22125585 J, Q\u2083 = \u22122980 J and Q\u2084 = 3645 J, respectively. The corresponding quantities of work involved are W\u2081 = 2200 J, W\u2082 = \u2212825 J, W\u2083 = \u22121100 J and W\u2084 respectively.
(i) Find the value of W\u2084
(ii) What is the efficiency of the cycle?`,a:"(i) 765 J   (ii) 10.82 %",s:`In a complete cycle \u0394U = 0, so total heat = total work.
Total heat = 5960 \u2212 5585 \u2212 2980 + 3645 = 1040 J.
W\u2081 + W\u2082 + W\u2083 + W\u2084 = 2200 \u2212 825 \u2212 1100 + W\u2084 = 275 + W\u2084 = 1040, so W\u2084 = 765 J.
Heat absorbed = Q\u2081 + Q\u2084 = 5960 + 3645 = 9605 J.
Efficiency = net work/heat absorbed = 1040/9605 = 0.1083, about 10.8% (the key gives 10.82%).`}]})();
